        <p>They did even more than their share, but what money they could raise was needed for the worthy objects of the society. ' _ _</p>
        <p>Ito OR6ANISING SECRETARY (the Rev. Mr.Wadrnan) gave an interesting address on the work of the s ciety, and said that last year they had an income of £30,000. In the Liverpool diocese they were already .£5O in advance of the amount contributed last year and that, he thought, was highly satisfactory. They had yet three more sales to bold in the diocese and they would easily heat last year's record. The money was spent in the right way in trying to get the best possible results out of the outcasts and destitute. They had a splendid object lesson in St. George's, Warrington. The society did not provide industrial schools, but taught the child to live in the right way and gave it a good Curistian home. ChiHren in the society's homes did not wear any uniforms which was going to mark them as children of charity. They provided for the future welfare of the child and 9; per cent. of the children they had had out of 17,500 during the 30 years the society had been in existence, had done well. (Applause.) He proposed a hearty vote of thanks to Mrs. Buckton.</p>
