      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>WARRINGTON PROOF.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON PROOF.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. Milner, of 131, Forster Street, Orford Lane, Warrington, says : "For about three years, off and on, I Euffered from shooting pains in my back, and after I had been sitting for any length of time I found it difficult to get up out of my chair. When I stooped</p>
        <p>Be sure you get the same Pills as Mr. Milner had.</p>
        <p>4 - -4'4, ti) . . . tfoir , • "</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>EitY DAY HEROISM,</head>
        <p>EitY DAY HEROISM,</p>
        <p>[COPYRIGHT.]</p>
        <p>"I would like to have done that '' How often ifnese words rise to our lips in reading of some one or other of these acts of supreme devotion, -which from time to time brighten the dull and crime-tarnished pages of our press, and give the lie to the oft-repeated croak that the days of chivalry are over. There are men and women to whom such occa- kAions come more than once or twice in a life- time. who seem to be selected as the chosen in- struments of Providence in saving human hiveii from perilous placcs. And there are men and women, having in them all the gallant stuir that gallant, deeds are made of, to whom in the even tenour of their way the call of glory never se-ems to come; there are boys who have reduced life-saving in theory to a fine art, and haunt canal-banks and rivers ill the laudable hope of reselling a fellow-creature, but never have the opportunity of nutting their powers to the test. And, while we are on this subject, a word should he said for those unfortunate indivi- duals. who, not being able to swim. have bees forced to look on at another's daring rescue,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>commoN PIUDENCE.</head>
        <p>commoN PIUDENCE.</p>
        <p>• "There were four great cowards on the said a woman whose child was savedi from a. canal, "but Only this gentleman would .attempt to swim in and save him." Coroners frequently brand men with shame for their in- capacity to save life from drowning. 1 word 43f praise is more suitable to such a .man for abstaining from. the silly and useless proceed- ing, of Anfusing and still further endangering the drowning person by floundering unskilfully in his company. Bather, every man awl woman owes it as a common duty to make hist or her- self efficient in technical skill for all .erner. I;encies; and pray God to give him courage to use his skill at the appointed hour. But surely, if it be cowardly not to do a thing. it cannot be heroic to do the same thing, but a commcm. act of, duty; and thus, detracting bystanders, we pay,a.very poor compliment to the person we intend to applaud.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>MODERN HEROES.</head>
        <p>MODERN HEROES.</p>
        <p>Looking over the lists of names of men, women and children whom the loyal Humane Society has honoured with its coveted decors- tion,.we may well rejoice to think how many in- stances have come to light of deeds of bravery and self effacement, actuated by sentiments of the purest uriselfishness. Acts of this kind are being performed daily, albeit most of them are hidden from the common eye. The • names honoured by the Society are only a tithe out of the many noble deeds whose doers are neither thanked nor rewavded. Their only record, their only reward,- is in the conscience of the doer: save for those -few, brought, often unwillingly, before the public eye, serving as an example and an incentive to the rest of mankind. It is good to think that in the common course of ,hurndrUni such gallant deeds as are enumerdlecl in this simple chronicle are daily taking place. It good to think that in. common crowds of common people, we may be rubbing shoulders with a man .who has proved himself ready and willing to lay down his life for another,: that the blackened, sweat-streaked miner may have ,looked death in the face in saving a comrade in distress: that, any day, the greasy engineer may have to grapple alone with the momentous Aoice between self azfd duty in the throbbing engine-room when the crisis comes.</p>
        <p>The .heroism of daily life has this quality, that raisesit, in some resoects,, above the kind a courage that shines conspicuously Onzthe field. of battle. It is performed very,often in cold, blood; there is no applauding crowd to see; no bugle to sound the charge; no comrade to asAsist, To feel the icy water close upon, you; to fight the forces of fire alone, on a forlorn quest that: may find only a charred corpse at the end: -To stick to duty, the kind of dull duty whose path is not the -way to' glory but to silence and the. grave. To look' one's last on light, and life; and happiness, green fields and pleasant places and friends' faces, requires, .a.supreme effort of renunciation. and a great self• Sacrifice,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>SOME BRAVE FELLOWS.</head>
        <p>SOME BRAVE FELLOWS.</p>
        <p>One of the bravest fellows I call to mind, hav- ing saved a woman from drowning in some very foul and dirty ,water, confessed that he was all but deterred from the act by the considera- lion that he was 'wearing his one decent suit of ciothcs, which ,would be snoiled by the filthy water. It was this small -pecuniary sacrifice, Hashing in the moment of indecision across the honest fellow's mind that cost him more pangs than the risk of life involved.</p>
        <p>Tho medals of the Royal Humane Society, and the Albert Medal for saving life at sea, take .tho place in modern days of the " Corona Civica" of the Romans, which was awarded for saving tho life of a fellow-citizen, and was con- sidered inure honourable than all military prizes. Any man may bo proud to wear them, since they are a sure sign of some signal :act of individual courage. The following little :account, culled from the Society's very early records, expresses rather quaintly the reticence of true gallantry:—" 1862 a young gentleman rowin.g near Eton .was' capsized, and being unable to swim, sank, and was carried by the current into deep water. An Eton boy named Bowdon, only 13 years of age, without waiting to strip, Plunged in and brought him ashore; and looking upon :the matter as a simple occurrence, ,walked home and thought no more about it, until he was sought out for recognition."</p>
        <p>Only one out of hundreds of gallant deeds that never see daylight. They go on and on through the years, and there and there one is published and proclaimed. Here, a young student, seeing a child fall from a pier, fights the waves for over half-an4tour before, he can land it. Here is the case of the young Irishman Brown, who dived into the river at Dublin Bridge in a heavy flood, and brought to shore a drowning boy. What makes Brown's feat more remarkable is that at the time he was suffering front the effects of a re- cently broken leg. This ,was the sixth life saved by him in five years.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>VETERAN LITE-S 1 VER</head>
        <p>VETERAN LITE-S 1 VER</p>
        <p>The name of David T.Trquhart is °worthy to be dug up out of the annals of the.siitiOs.- He .was the only man able to swihi out of a boatload ,Of 'twelve, mostly women, capsized into. the Dor- noch Firth. swimming and diving 'repeatedly, Urquhart • rescued seven of, the helpless ones, five being 'women, his last journey 'being ac- complished with two clinging to his clothes, and a third dragged, •nnconscious by the hair. This was accomplished in all the fufy of a storm on the hill-bound loch, in which it was no incon- siderable .feat for a man to save his own life. We find an old clergyman; on his peaceful way to perform divine service at Dymchurch, going. to the aid of a wrecked shin, and despite the remonstrances and refusals of assistance from bystanders, succeeded in bringing eshore out of the. waves. three drowning sailors. _ _</p>
        <p>. . We read. of. a simple -farmer,• an old man of sixty, who espied twelve shipwrecked souls, clinging to the rocks on the .wild Cornish. coast, and all -but .washed away by the tide. The old man started out, quite alone, with a stout rope for his sole aid, and swim.ming and struggling along the .eoast for over a mile, brought them one by one to the safety of his own roof. There are soldiers and Sailors innumerablq in this goodly list of life7saverB--ship's boys and midshipmen, schoolboys, doctors; policemen, and some 'women.. There is, notably, Harriet Atkin- son, a governess,. and a very delicate girl, who 'plunged into a pobl'to save her employer's child, after, three men and two women had, vainly llttempted assistance. It is doing poor justice to such goodly doings to try to enumerate them here. We must, however, mention James Fosse o Peancaire, who saved 60 lives in the great flood of the Phone in 183; nor can we forbear the remarkable 'de:votion- of Tom_ Lee, of Fife, 'totally blind. man, who, being called- to the assistance Of some little children who were being. carried Out to dashed in, -guided by their voices, and.brought them hack to safety. _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>IN THE COURTS</head>
        <p>IN THE COURTS</p>
        <p>Borough Police Court.</p>
        <p>SATURDAY.</p>
        <p>Before Mr. R. T. Fairclough (in the chair), Councillor Locker, Messrs. A. J. Jolley and T. Pemberton. GAVE UP HIS WORIC.—Jobn Norbury,Workhouse, was charged with neglecting to provide proper maintenance for his wife and two children who had become charge- able to the Union. Mr. B. Shearer, who prosecuted, said that prisoner, his wife and two children were admitted to the Workhouse on the Bth May. Prisoner gave up work at the Vulcan Foundry to come to War- rington. There was work for him but he left without any apparent reason. Inspector Wainwright said he had cautioned prisoner about drinking and throwing himself out of work. On the 28th May he visited No. 3, Hopwood-street, where prisoner and his family were living with a relative. There was no bed for the children to lie on and for some weeks they had been sleeping in the clothes they wore. He left his employ- ment because he could not have a sub.—Prisoner said be would only be too willing to work if he had a home to go to. fie would go to the Vulcan now if he could. There were a number of grown-up children in good, positions in Warrington, but they never helped him. He could not get a house because he had no rent book. —He was sent to prison for 14 dass. WHY HE LEFT THE SHlP.—James Harris, 19, Beswick-street, was charged wit, assaulting his mother the previous day.—Superintendent Williamson,in asking for a week's adjournment, said, prisoner lived with his mother and was well known in that Court, the , police having a long history about him. On Februaryl '2Bth, 1911, he was committed for three years to a Borstal institution. tie was released on licence in March last and a ship was found for.. him, but he deserted it at Newport before it sailed., uade his way back to Warrington, but after worki . fortnight he gave up his situation and slept out. His mother remonstrated with him and he picked up a piece of wood and struck her across the arm. The remand was necessary in order that the police could communicate with the Borstal Committee and the Prison Committee. —Asked why he left the ship which had been found for him, prisoner said the sailors were all foreigners and he could not understand a word they said.—Superintendent Williamson; There were no foreigners in the last place he had.—The prisoner was remanded for a week. DROPPED &lt;1 CASTE BAD.—John Kelly, 23, Lockett- street, was charged with having been drunk in Bridge- street the previous night.—Snperintendent Williamson said prisoner was charged with drunkenness but a more serious charge would be preferred against him. At 10.15 the previous night P.C. Turner saw the prisoner staggering about Bridge-street drunk and carrying a dinner basket. The officer saw prisoner on his way home, but an hour later the constable found ' him again in Bridge-street Prisoner was bleeding very profusely from a wound in hia left wrist. He said a policeman had tripped him up. He was taken to the Infirmary where the wound was stitched. Prisoner also asked the officer to let him go and he would give him a drink. At the Police Station prisoner was searched and he dropped a cash bag containing £4 2s. 9d. from under his jacket. At 2.30 that morning it was found by P.C. Sankey that the offices of Mr. Gandy, corn merchant, Wilderspool- road, had been broken open, the window having been smashed. Mr. Gandy bad identified the bag as his prcperty and stated that over £6 was missing from his office. The prisoner was a stranger to Warrington, but had been working here for a few weeks.—Prisoner : I was a bit drunk, but I know nothing about the cash.— The case as adjourned for a week.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>MONDAY.</head>
        <p>MONDAY.</p>
        <p>Before the Mayor (in the chair), Aldermen Bolton, Pierpoint and James Evans, Messrs. S. Wright, G. Pilling, W. Roberts and W. Furness. A Cumous Excum—Thomas Gray Walworth was I charged with having used obscene language on Central Station and further with assaulting P.C. Joynson, of the company's police.—rhe officer stated that prisoner alighted from a compartment in the rear portion of the 11.30 train from Liverpool, the previous night. He went to the ticket collector and inquired for the Liverpool platform. The official asked him if he had come by train and prisoner answered in the negative. The officer followed prisoner down the steps and he admitted that be had come by train but said he had given his ticket up at Ashton-under-Lyne. He also said he was going back to Liverpool on the next train. He threatened that he would break his (witness) neck if he missed the train. Half-way down the steps prisoner kicked him and struck him in the face. He again threw him to the ground in the sub-way. He gave prisoner over to P.C. Harrison, of the borough police.—P.C. Harrison stated that prisoner was under the influence of drink.—Prisoner said he had been bad with neuralgia and had put some quinine into a bottle of stout and drunk it, and must have dozed off in the carriage. He must have jumped up and got out at Warrington, thinking he was in Manchester. He had held three public-house licences in Liverpool.—The Chief : It must have been a very large bottle of stout as he was very much under the influence of drink. Had he been sober he would not have done what he did. He had a ticket from Liverpool to Manchester.— Prisoner was fined 53. and costs or seven days. PRISONER INDIGNANT.—James Devenish, 5, Back of Foundry-street, Bolton, was charged with assaulting Detective Wishy, of the London and North-Western Railway police, on Bank Quay Station. Detective Wisby stated that on Saturday afternoon prisoner came off the platform and when asked for his ticket at the barrier, made no reply. He was asked where he was travelling from but would not say. He used filthy language and would not give his name and address. He made a rush past the barrier, striking Mr.Plinston, the ticket collector, and witness. On being detained in a room he again made a rush at witness and struck him in the face and chest.—Prisoner said he had not travelled on the railway. He was going to Bolton at the time. It was he who was assaulted.—Mr. Plinston said they could get nothing but filthy language from the prisoner.— Prisoner also stated that the slide to the booking office window was closed and he went on the platform to inquire for a train. He refused to give his name and address because he was cruelly assaulted.—Mr. W. Roberts (a magistrate) : How many pints had you had ?—Prisoner (indignantly) : I don't drink pints, sir. I had two glasses.—He was fined as. and costs or seven dar. IMPRISONMENT A CHARITY.---John Heaton, no fixed address, was charged with stealing 4s. 6d. the money of Alfred Mortimer.—The Chief Constable said the charge was one of larceny as bailee. About 9.40 on Saturday morning prosecutor, who lived at 9, Cloth Hall-yard, gave the prisoner a jacket and vest to pledge for him, as he wanted to purchase a pair of hoots. Prisoner pledged the articles in Winwick-street for 4s. 6d., saying that they were his own praperty, and that he wanted the money to pay his rent. He bad taken the money and had been guilty of a very mean theft. P.C. Moreland arrested him. He had been 87 times previously before the Court. It was a charity to send him to prison for the full term, for he was before the Court nine, ten and eleven times a year.. -Heaton was sent to gaol for three, months with hard labour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>TITESDAY.</head>
        <p>TITESDAY.</p>
        <p>Before Councillor H. Roberts (in the chair). Messrs. C. Jackson Holmes, W. Richardson and Col. Edelsten. A RECONCILIATION.—In the case in which Ethel Lambert had asked for a separation from her husband Herbert Lambert, Mr. A. Browne said that since the adjournment the defendant had found his wife a new home. It would be remembered that defendant did not appear in Court when the case was last before the magistrates. It was suggested that he should now promise to conduct himself as a husband should and allow his wife proper food. She was nearly starved befae, the defendant, having laid out the money him- self.—Defendant promised to do as suggested, and the case was dismisPed, the Chairman remarking that there was ne reason why the parties should not be happy, for they had been married such a short time.—Mr. Browne: The house they are going to live in is 25, Pierpoint- street, which will be away from all relations. - WINDOW SM ASH ING .—M artha Green. Philip-street, was charged with committing wilful damage to the extent of ss. to a window at the shop of Mary Littlefair, SO, Cartwright-street. She was also charged with having used obscene language.—lt was stated that the prisoner deliberately pot her foot through the window.—Prisoner denied having done damage. She had been 19 times previously before the Court and was sent to prison for one month.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>FRIDAY.</head>
        <p>FRIDAY.</p>
        <p>Before the Mayor (in the chair), Alderman Pierpoint, Messrs...T. Looker, 0. Pilling W. Furness, S. Wright,</p>
        <p>_ and A. J. Jolley. MOTHER AND ON CHARGED.—WiIIiam Jenks (la), 20, Albert-streer, was charged with stealing a pair or boots value 9s. 6d., the property of Mr. Henry Myers, a stallholder in the Market.—The hors mother was charged with receiving the goods.—The case was adjourned until to-day in order that Mr. Myers, who lives in Manchester, might have an opportunity of identifying the goods. HUSBAND AND WIFE.—The adjourned case in which Sarah Jane Allidon, 370, Wilderspool-road,. was sum- moned for assaulting her husband, Frederick Henry Allison, Spring Dale, Fairfield-road, Latchford With- out, was beard. Mr. C. Unsworth said that at the previous hearing the defendant pleaded guilty to the assault, but the question arose as to her allowing the husband to see their two children. She undertook to take them to Mrs. Dyson's, in King-street, at stated times. Mrs. Allison, however, said that her husband could see the children at the Stockton Heath' Swing Bridge. Her only excuse was that she did not want to see him. Words passed between them, and getting no satisfaction,the husband walked away.—The defendant said the children dare not see their father as he was a brute. She refused to go t,o Mrs. Dyson's because there was ,a woman there who had caused their separation.--Complainant 'said his wife lhad told him' that so long as he saw the children through a window, that was sufficienr.—Mrs. Allison was (bound over for 12 months, and ordered to pay the costs of the case. • ROBBED HIS BROTHEL—John Barry, of no fixed address, was charged with stealing on the 20th inst., a watch and chain with medal attached, value 155., the property of his brother, Thomas Barry, 32, Parr-street. —Prisoner had admitted stealing the watch and pledg- ing it for 4s. 6d. He had given himself up to the pence. —Prosecutor said he did not wish to press the case, as his brother was consumptive.—Barry was sent to prison for two miniths.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>REL/G/01/S e-SOC/AL</head>
        <p>REL/G/01/S e-SOC/AL</p>
        <p>St. Peter's Church.</p>
        <p>Services in celebration of the school anniversary were held at St. Peter's Church on Sunday. The Vicar (Rev. B. Percival) occupied the pulpit in the morning, and said that to exclude religion from their day schools would debar a large proportion of the children of this country from all religious culture. Many never attended Sunday school, and they were the poorest of the poor. To exclude all religions instruction from our day schools would be to wage a war upon the consciences of Churchmen. If they had read the reports of the Socialist conference, held at Blackpool during the Whittuntide holiday', they would see that nothing would please the infidel and the Socialist better than to keep religious instruction from the young. Religions instruction was well adapted to commence each day's work; to remind them that they belonged to God, however poor, and that they had a heavenly Father who cared for them, and to Whom they might pour out their heart's desire and wishes. He was opposed to the movement that would get rid of all denominational teaching; and pitied the man who 'did not know what he believed. The creedless man had I been well called " The churchless man." He was also I opposed to it because it interfered with the liberties of I the people. " The preacher at the evening service was the Rev. Stephen Jones, vicar of Widnes. An interesting and successful service of song, entitled "Given in charge," wfai4,•given be the afternoon .by the members of the yolnlg women's Bible class. Creeds and Selfishness.</p>
        <p>At St. John's Presbyterian Church on Sunday, the Rev. Wm. Reid spoke of the Spring as that expression of God which bespoke not only His faith- fulness and provision, but also the melody and beauty of His nature, and emphasised the fact that it emerged out of the darkness and bareness of winter, and while the storms of late winter showed his reluctance to depart, the future lay with Spring,and the quiet victory was assured to her. Speaking of love, he said its force and power to serve was measured by its purity. The foreign matter of selfishness impaired its value. Unfortunately, however, that was allowed to enter into our creeds, and, worse than all, into our lives. Lines of longitude and latitude were all right on a map, but to attempt to chalk them on the wave were rather futile, and however outlined or interlined Church and Society might be, true love laughed at all would-be barriers or restrictions, and realized that" the love of God is for ever brosier than the measures of man's mind."</p>
        <p>[NOT</p>
        <p>lirge audience assembled in the evening when Mr. Kernick preached a very able sermon,and special hymns were again given, the little children repeating their hymn, and notwithstanding the large congregation,t hey came out of the ordeal with flying colours. Collections were taken at each service, and realised the splendid sum of £45. Mr.Kernick, in thanking the congregation for their generosity, pointed out that the members of Sankey Bridges Wesleyan. Chapel had shown the deep interest they took in their own church,by the fact that at the morning service, when the congregation was with few exceptions, composed of members of the church,the snm realized was no less thin £2l 10s. This showed that the members took their full share in the responsi- bilities of their work. It is a remarkable coincidence that the number of coins collected was 1913.</p>
        <p>a . aPoo, ON At.iii'v. WAR R \Nor ..4.: .4% I Me' • t \).) (-7-ba.rcriith f. i • 1,...)......._ t 3. ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>Sankey Sri dges Wesleyan Sunday School Anniversary.</head>
        <p>Sankey Sri dges Wesleyan Sunday School Anniversary.</p>
        <p>One of the most successful anniversary celebrations ever held by the Sankey Bridges Wesleyan Sunday School took place on Sunday, in the morning at the Wesleyan Chapel, and in the afternoon and evening at Parr Hall. There was an excellent attendance at the chapel in the morning, when the preacher was the Rev. F. W. Kernick, M.A.,of Mellor,Blackburn, and formerly of the Warrington circuit. The opening service was hearty and gave a good send-off for the rest of the day. Mr. Kernick expressed the great pleasure it gave hitu to have the opportunity of revisiting his old friends at Sankey Bridges, who always held a warm corner in is heart. In the afternoon, a good congrega- tion assembled at the Parr Hall at the children's service. The chair was taken by Mr. Thomas C. Miles,of Sheffield, an old scholar at Sankey Bridges, and in the course of his address he spoke of the great influence for gcod which his old Sunday school had been to him. An address on" Memory" was given by the Kev. W. A. Beckett. Special hymns were given by the children and choir during the afternoon,and the singing afforded great pleasure to the congregation. Both hymns and singing were most suitable to a Sunday school anniver- sary, for the hymns were of a genuine Sunday school character and the choir and children entered heartily into the service. The excellence of the service was a credit to tne conductor, Mr. C. R. Muir, and the pianist, Mr. W. Pacey, who received con- gratulations on all sides on the result of their arduous labours. One of the most pleasing features of the service was a children's hymn, in which a verse each was sung by the following little girls :—Edith Blythe, Annie Beddard, Eva Day, Lily Cope and Jessie Muir and each sang their verse very sweetly. Mr. Tynan also took the solo part of another hymn very creditably. A</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>Wycliffe P.S.A.</head>
        <p>Wycliffe P.S.A.</p>
        <p>There was an open =Wing of this society on Sun- day, the president, BPV. I'. T. :lames, M.A., being in the chair, and Mr. John Harrison, of Liverpool, spoke upon 4' Continuity, or Enduring to the End." He had seen the beginnings of many things, but unfortunately they had soon come to grief. He had seen men start well, but something hindered them in pursuing good work. To do the wrong thing was very easy, but often to do the right was most difficult. He had known men who had been taken from the gutter, who had proved really good soldiers of the Cross. Some such characters he knew to-day in Liverpool. where they were working a mission successfully. At first they said they would fall away again,- hut God took hold of them and ; used them for his glory. He was glad to see so many young men present. He would impress upon them that to be a Christian was to work and fight, and those who endured to the end would be saved. A most pleasing festure of the meeting was the presence of the Sylvan Quartette Party(Alsdame Sarah Silcock and Mrs.Sephton and Messrs. Sephton and Dutton), who feelingly sang "There is a green hill far away " and " God is a spirit." Madame Silcock and Mr. Sephton sang a duet "Love Divine." Mr. Percy Crampton read the lesson. The President led the prayer. A vote of sympathy was passed with Mr. J. Jones on the death of his brother.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>Wesleyan Methodist Church. Liverpool District Synod.</head>
        <p>Wesleyan Methodist Church. Liverpool District Synod.</p>
        <p>The Synod of the Liverpool District met at Birken- head. The Rev. T. Galland Hartley presided, and the Rev. T. A. Turney acted as financial secretary. After sixteen years as chairman of the district, and forty-five years as a circuit minister, the Rev. T. Galland Hartley was given permission to become a supernumerary. The Rev. George Lenton, after forty-seven years' active ministry, also retires at the next conference. Two ministers enter the active work after serious illnesses—the Revs. William Chant and R. Middleton. Army and Navy work has been carried on under great difficulty, but with success. Regular prison work has been carried on at Liverpool, Lancaster and Preston. About 2 per cent. of the inmates have declared them- selves Wesleyan?. Many tributes were paid to the splendid work of the late Rev. Joseph Jackson in connection with the Liverpool Mission. Letters of sympathy were sent to the families of the Revs. W. J. Boote and J. Ayrton. The Secretary presented the membership returns. A spirit of hopefulness prevailed. There it an ilicrease of adult members, in the district there are 24,858 members, with 2,082 on trial. Wesley guilds show a reduction of 597 last year and 223 in the present year. are giving place to Brotherhoods. The former have decreased 939, and the latter show an increase of 193.</p>
        <p>At Wednesday's session it was reported that there were several Wesleyan churches in Liverpool where the congregations were small as a result of changes in the area they served. Good work of a social character was done, but it was being found increasingly difficult to meet expenses. In the Great Homer-street district, one of the poorest in the city, a site purchased several years ago had been recently sold. A representative cornmision was appointed to study the spiritual needs of the poorest city areas. It was pointed out that suburban extension was rendered impossibll owing to the cost of maintaining work in the poorer areas. The Rev. S. F. Keaele spoke on the menace of compulsory thilitary service. A resolution expressing profound concern at the growing desecration of the Sabbath was adopted. Notice of motion was given "that the Rev. George Jackson, of Toronto, be not appointed to a tutorship at Didsbury College."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>LONELY WOOD TRAGEDY.</head>
        <p>LONELY WOOD TRAGEDY.</p>
        <p>William Walter Burton, a -11;i;l:Tt —trapper, was on Wednesday committed I.) the Dorchester Assizes by the Wimborne magistrates on a charge of murdering a young woman named Winifred Mary Mitchell. It is alleged that Burton, who is a married man, shot the woman through the head in a lonely plantation, and afterwards buried her body in a grave he had previously dug.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
