      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>BRONCHO BILL'S VISIT.</head>
        <p>BRONCHO BILL'S VISIT.</p>
        <p>On Monday WarriN3ton was treated to a flying visit from Broncho Bill's Wild West Exhibition, the large tent with two full-sized zings being pitched in Wilderspool-road. An extensive programme of smart variety turns was gone throngh in the afternoon and evening, the whole being crowned by a magnificent spectacular exhibition of real life in the Wild West Broncho Bill himself is an interesting and entertain- ing Westerner, polite and courteous to a degree. Born and bred in Trueson City, Arizona, he loves the roving life of the cowboy and sits his fins black horse with becoming ease and qrace. He first came to England four years ago with a number of Sioux Indians in a dramatic entertaintnent, but went back to his native home, only to return with the present show, of which Mr. Henery is the general manager, early this year. Broncho Bill has held the position of deputy sheriff of Trueson aty for over six months, and shoots, rides and lassoos with remarkable skill.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>AN ADMIRER OF ENGLISH BEAUTY.</head>
        <p>AN ADMIRER OF ENGLISH BEAUTY.</p>
        <p>Of England he said, "The country is nice, the people are nice but the weather is terrible. We have had nine weeks solid rain, and when you get 70 horses tramping through the field I reckon you want to spend a fortune on boot polish. All I can say of the girls is that they are mighty pretty out here, and our own girls do not compare with them." The horses with the show are :—Mustangs, 1?; Bronchos, 8 ; and British horses, 28. There are 10 Sioux Indians with a chief from the White Pine Ridge reservation. The best turn of the afternoon was the daring cycling performance by "The Qnetas " who ride at lightning speed inside the smallest tea-cup track in the world. Ls Papilioe are a couple of charming lady performers who, suspended in mid air by a rope held between the teeth, perform the moat dangerous and fascinating feats. Comical Walker is the great clown and provides much food for laughter. Splendid examples of horsemanship lassooing, running, Whip cracking, Ao., were given by a number of cow- boys and Indians, to the delight of all. As a finale there was the sensational and effective attack on the Deadwood Stage Coach, the horses careering madly round the ring during the " hold up."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>DURHAM BOY'S ROMANCE.</head>
        <p>DURHAM BOY'S ROMANCE.</p>
        <p>The Melbourne" Argus " tells an interesting story of how a Durham boy got to Australia. Last March a letter was received by Miss Edith Guiana, secretary of the Melbourne City Newab ys' Society, from a boy named George Bell, employed in a coal mine at Durham, England, who had been so imprersed by reading an account of Miss Onian's recent visit to England that he was filled with yearning to get away from the smoke and grime of the pits to a place where the sun shone and he could breathe the fresh air of the fields.</p>
        <p>The simple but forceful appeal made by the boy was not made in vain, for the day following its publication Miss Onians received a communication from a farmer's family near Beaufort, stating that they had decided to pre tbe youth the opportunity he desired to obtain a practical knowledge of farming and pastoral life. The boy would be treated as one of the family, and the situation would meet his wishes in every respect, as it would be all out-door work, which would enable him to enjoy plenty of fresh air and sunshine. They were prepared to pay him 103. a week for the first twelve mouths, and, if he proved trustworthy and reliable, he would receive an annual increase of 25. 6d. per week. The youth would be given a room in the homestead, and it was desired that be should put in his spare time by reading and study. The farmer was quite willing to advance the mon y for the boy's passage to Australia.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>WILD CAT.</head>
        <p>A WILD CAT.</p>
        <p>A fine specimen of the wild cat, a genus, t ow thought to be extinct in the Highlands of Scotland, has been shot by a gamekeeper on an estate on the west of Ross- shire. It weighed over 91b., and measured over 31t. It was beautifully striped, sod had a bushy tail.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, May 24, 1913. RELIEVING OFFICERS.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, May 24, 1913. RELIEVING OFFICERS.</p>
        <p>Warrington Guardians and Suggested</p>
        <p>New Appointment.</p>
        <p>IS THERE WORK FOR THREE?</p>
        <p>Having received 109 applications for the pool ion of relieving officer in one of their districts, the member; of the Warrington Board of Onsrdiass are now undecided as to whether the work to be dealt with calls for any appointment to be made. The gnesti )11 was relied at the meeting of the Board on Wednesday, at which Mr. E. L. Bent presided, _ms. _r _a.nmdptshoenr,emwresrsh elialsilo, present :—Mre. Collingwood, Miss Brosdbent, Rev. A. M. Mitchell, Messrs. A. J. Jolley (vice-chairman), G. Mather, J. W. Knowles, J. Billings, W. C. Brooks, W. Furness, ft. Lee, F. McKinley, 0. Allen, P. Longton, W. Honghton, T. Roliinson, W. Flanagan, M. W. Beton, T. Ritchie, J. A. Hughes, 1. Dale, A. Bottomley (clerk), R. I. Partridge (assistant clerk), and W. J. Brown (master of the Workhonse). LADY APPLICANT FOR THE POSITION.</p>
        <p>Tile CLARK rsputed that be had received 109 applicsitioss for the poet of relieving officer. The Rev. A. N. : Are there any lady applicant..? lb* CLARK: I believe so. _</p>
        <p>The Rev. A. M. MITCHRLL : Cin you tell tie what number?</p>
        <p>The CLERK : No, sir. I cannel, tell you, but I know there is one lady applicant. The Rev. A. M. Mumma, : Was the advertisement so worded as to lead ladies to believe that they might apply? The CLERK: In this sense it was—l advertised as the Guardians instructed me, for a relieving officer. Knowing that the position of relieving Lift-oer is bald by ladies in some Unions, some of them naturally thought we might consider the question of a laly, too. Mr. SETON : Have any of our own officers applied for the post ? 'I he CLAIM Yes, si The Rev.A. M. MITCHELL : Don't you think it ought to be made ohm that ladies are eligible for this position ? The CLERK : The fact that it has bten made clear is evidenced by the fact that at least one lady has applied for it..</p>
        <p>Tbe Rev, A. M. MITCHALL : What you have said leads one to the opporite conclusion. Only those who are in the know" would apply. The CLARK I simply asked for a relieving officer, as the Guardians instructed me. -The Rev. A. M. MiTcitieta, : Very few ladies would understand it, was open to them. Mr. W. C. BROOKS How many of our own officers are applicants? Air. MATHER Before that is answered, I think there is an inclination on the part of many members. of the Board further to consider the whole question To go through Iho applications this morning would be more than we ()mild cope with. In view of the feeling that the matter should have further consideration, it would be unfair to say how many of our own officials or how many ladies have applied, or who have applied at all. Mr. W. C. BROOKS: I take it that ',bat is behind Mr. Mather's statement is the question of whether we should appoint a new officer or manage without one. Mr. MATHAR: That is the resin point. Considering what we have seen to-day, as to the number of relief cases to be dealt with, the question arises : Is there sufficient work now for three relieving officers in this district, or could two do it, as they tiseci to ? lam only throwing it out as a suggestion for the Board to consider. Mrs. SIMMON : It ie a pity we did not Link of this before advertising the position. The CHAIRMAN : I think most of the members were not in touch with the particular circumstances. The Rev. A. M. MITCHELL : It is not too late to mend our ways, is It? The CHAIRMAN: I hope not. The Rev. A. M. MITCHELL: I am glad Mr. Mather has brought the matter forward. I think it is the opinion of a gc.od many that, if some committee Inquired Into the matter and went through the books,it would be found that there is not really any pressing neceesitT for the appointment of this ufficer. The CHAIRMAN suggested that it might be found adviera'ile to defer making a y appointment for six months,to see whether the work could be satisfactorily done without a third officer. Mr. BILLIKGE considered it would be a retrograde step to talk of reducing the number of relieving officers, but. he suggested a revision of the districts. The matter was referred to the Finance Committee, it being understood that if that body considered an appointment ought to be made they should select candidates to appear before the made,</p>
        <p>A BREEZE. The Rev. A. M. MITCHELL q testioned the procedure with regard to certain tenders and his reiterated objections led to a breeze. Mr. titvowLEs strc ngly objected to what be termed the rev. gentleman's " rcurrilous remarks" and said they reflected upon the House Committee. The object of the rev. gent'emm appeared to be t 3 figure in the newspaper reportr. The Rev. A. M. MITCHELL rose to reply, wl'en Mr. HOUGHTON intervened with the remark : I protest agskinst the standing orders bring infringed in this way. The Rev. A. M. MITCHELL endeavoured to address the 80.. rd.</p>
        <p>.—Th—e-dit.AIRMAN (warmly): ircu might obey the chair. The Rev. A. M. Mrrctini.t.: It is not fair. LATE REGISTRAR'S APPLICATION.</p>
        <p>The Finance Committee reconsidered the letter of Mr. W. 11. Harris-n, late registrar of births and deaths for the Warrington sub-district, in which he applied to the Board for moms form of work.lt was recommended that the Clerk be instructed to inform Mr. Harrison that they were unable to take any action thereon. ASSISTANT CLERK'S SALARY.</p>
        <p>The Finance Committee recommended that the salary of Mr. R. I. Partridge' first assistant clerk,be increased by £2O per annum. 'ibis was agre-d to.</p>
        <p>THE TREATMENT AN SUPERVISION OF LUNATICS.</p>
        <p>Dr. F. Needham, one of the Commissioners in Lunacy, who recently visited the Workhouse, reported that of the women of unsound mind housed in the institution, six were in the body of the house five in the infirm ward, and 21 in the hospital, while Of the men eight were in the house,two in the hospital, seven in the irflrm ward and 42 in the imbecile ward proper. The wide distribution of these patients made adequate supervision almost imposrible, and he was, therefore, glad to learn that the plans for a new imbecile block were now before the Local Government B ,ard and were expected to be approved. It appeared, however, that they contained no provision whatever for the accommodation of any attendants or nurses and no rooms for admi tistrative purposes. If that were so, they would, if built, be deficient ia an essential part ioular, haying regard to the care and comfort of the patients and the comfort of the attendants, who were at present in several instances very unsuitably accommodated away from their patients. The Rev. A. M. fsliTctiELL : Does the inspector find any fault with the hours the attendants have to work? _ _ _</p>
        <p>The CLERK No, sir. He does not mention that point, but he.tinds fault with the accommodation for them.</p>
        <p>'The report was referred to the House Committee. APPLICATIONS FOR INCREASES OF SALARY. Dr. W. Megaw, resident medical officer, wrote apply- ing for an Increase of salary. lie pointed out tbat he had held the position for over twelve months, during which time there had been a large increase in the num- ber of patients treated in the hospital. Over 1,6G0 cases had been under his treatment during his term of office.</p>
        <p>Mr. W. C. BRCORS moved a resolution to the effect that the Board. could not see their way to entertain the application at the present time. There was no seconder of this prcpoiition, and the application was referred to the House Committee. Mr. 0. Moss, assistant master at the Workhouse, also applied for an advance of salary. This application was also referred to the House Committee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>COLOUR AND DANGER.</head>
        <p>COLOUR AND DANGER.</p>
        <p>INSECTS AND ANIMALS THAT ARE BRIGHT BUT "UNPLEASANT."</p>
        <p>Mr. F. Balfour Browne, speaking to teachers at the Zoological Society on Saturday, said that probably a great deal more than was justified had been made of the idea that brightness in the cokur of animals and insects was associated a ith their danger. It was a fact that a number of insects and animals which were extremely bright in colour were not eaten by birds and animals, and it was believed that they had a taste that was unpleasant. The bright colours were associ- ated with some unpleasant character, such as taste or poscerful sting, and they were known as warning colours It was very probable that in some past age the great snakes had legs as lizards had now. They heard tales of snakes devouring oxen and donkeys, but the largest snake known was not longer than 35ft., and would be quite incapable of doing this. An ordinary full-sized sheep would be too much for most of them.</p>
        <p>THE CANDLE AUCTION. A 200-year-old custom was observed at Tatwortb, near Chard, Somerset, recently, when the "Candle Auction" was held for the letting of a field called Stowell Meadow. The tenants of the estate, of which the meadow is part, meet at the village inn and bid for the renting of the ground during the burning of an inch of tallow canile.</p>
        <p>,ik.4 ' "",1"."-•</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>FARM AND GARDEN.</head>
        <p>FARM AND GARDEN.</p>
        <p>CHESHIRE FRUIT PROSPECTS.</p>
        <p>Cheshire prophets are alreadv predicting an exceptionally good fruit season. The general outlook is as favourable as it possibly can be. 'Argo fruit growers expect apples will be a heavy crop; the show of bloom has been one of the fineot ever seen. THE PROMISE OF MAY. COUNTRY NEVER LOOKED BETTER.</p>
        <p>The count 7 has never looked better in the middle of May than it does at present, says the " Times." There is a richness of verdure and luxuriance of growth that promise plentiful production of hay, corn, and grass, end if the prospects were to be reckoned by appearances the farming interest could look for- ward to the harvests of the year with complacency. But the landscape flatters somewhat the actual con- dition of things, the exuberance of growth of clovers, grass, and winter corn obscuring the defects of the wet season. Where the land is light and natural drainaqs good, things look remarkably well, and work is fairly well forward, most of the rnangels having been sown in a moderate seed-bed. The fro- quint rains, howeverniave been too mach for the heavy loam. and clays. There has been liberal growth, brit against the luxuriance of the clovers and winter wheat have to be seta considerable proportion of uneeeded corn land and inability to make progress with the sowing of green crops. The retentive soils have been in a sodden condition since the middle el Ilast month, and sowing has been impracticable. The stimulus of the rains and intermittent periods of sunshine have been of little benefit. LIVE STOCK EXPORT TRADE. Although the export business has not yet begun in earnest there are many indications of quickening activity and a readiness to effect transactions as soon as the obstacles have been removed. But, while tuying for Smith America, with a few notable excep- tions, has been largely of a provisional nature, many cargoes of pure-bred cattle have already been shipped. They have gone mostly to South Africa, bet others have crossed the Atlantic, and even the Belgian Congo has taken a consignment, The businese transacted in the past few weeks is distinfoished by two main features. The first is the multiplicity of the breeds included. The great beef varieties no longer have a monopoly of the markets abroad. Shorthorns, Herefords, end Aberdeen-Angus have been included in considerable numbers, but there have also been consignments of Ayrshire!, Devons, South Devons, Swore; and Jerseys. The other noteworthy feature is the activity of South Africa. It looks as if a new era were opening rip for stock raising, both in the Union of South Africa and in Rhodesia. DISEASES OF ANIMALS. The returns for the week ending May 10th show that there were 78 fresh outbreaks of swine fever, the cases notified coming from 23 counties in England, four in Wales and two in Scotland. York- shire headed the list with 13, followed by Denbiish- shire with eight and Sussex with five. The pigs slaughtered numbered 1,797. New outbreaks of parasitic mange were 38, the animals attacked number- ing 68. There were nine fresh cases of anthrax, two of glanders and three of sheep scab.</p>
        <p>• WOULD HE? Would a farmer keep a workman who cost .£1 a week and did 10s. worth of work? Of course not. Than why keep a cow whose milk does pay for her keep? And how is he to know whether or not she is profitable unless he finds out what she is giving? These questions are asked by "Farm and Home." When keeping milk records, or carrying out experi- ments with milk in order to ascertain what weight of butter or cheese can be obtained from a definite quantity of milk, it should always be weighed. Measuring milk leads to inaccurate results. A gallon of milk at GO deg. F, weighs 10.3 lb., if the milk is of average quality. When the milk has been cooled to a low temperature a gallon of it by measure would weigh more than the above weight. Freshly-drawn milk, which is then at a temperature of 101 deg. F., and contains a I t of froth and air, if measured by the gallon wonld weigh much less than 10 31b. to the gallon. THE FARM STRIKE 1N CHESHIRE. The very fact that the farm labourets are unorganised— as has been very evident in the recent strike in Cheshire—should incline the farmer to lend a sympathetic ear to his request, for it would be a disastrous thing if the continual strikes which upset industrial centres, with the less and lawlessness with which they are so often accompanied, were brought into farm labour (says "Farm and Dome." It would, from the farmer's point of view, be far better if all wages paid were in cash, for the figures often quoted show a great disparity between urban and rural rates of pay and mi.lead public opinion, though most farm labourers are well aware of and appreciate the fact that the payments in kind swell their total considerably. Indeed' if the casual nature cf the tr.wn workmen's labour be borne in mind, together with the high Ant he has to pay, the travelling expenses that must of necessity be incurred, and the drawbacks incidental to the absence of any plot of ground on which to grow vegetables and herbs which he now has to buy, his lot will probably be found to be no better —and it is often worse—than that of the average farm labourer. But even the town labourer is not contented, and it is not likely that the demonstration that be is as well off as his town brother will satisfy the country worker, who feels the shoe pinch. The question in Parlia- ment and the discussions taking place in different parts of the country show that this matter is corning to the fore. We plead for its sympathetic consideration, and hope it will never be allowed to "get ripe for legislation." It is not, in cur opinion, a fit subject for legislation, but one for fair and honest determina- tion by the parties concerned who are interested in each other's welfare.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>DAIRY EDUCATION. BOARD OF AGRICULTURE PROPOSAL TO CHESHIRE.</head>
        <p>DAIRY EDUCATION. BOARD OF AGRICULTURE PROPOSAL TO CHESHIRE.</p>
        <p>At the Cheshire Education Committee at Crewe on Monday, a letter was submitted from the Board of Agriculture with regard to the proposed Farm Institute hr Cheshire. The Board understood that it was the general desire throughout the county that the pro- posed institute should be a special school to provide instruction in dairying, particularly cheese-making. With this view the Board were in agreement, and they were of opinion that, while it might be found desirable to include provision for instruction for such sub- sidiary subjects as poultry keeping, &amp;c, the general scope of the school should be confined to the teaching of dairying, as the characteristic industry of the dis- trict. Such a school might with advarbge be made available for instruction in cheese-making to residents of neiuhbonring counties, and they suggested that the Cheshire County Council should approach the councils of Shrorshire and Staffordshire with a view to enlist- ing their co-operation in the matter. A special wchool of the nature indicated would be eligible for grants under the Farm Institute scheme on the same terms as an institute providing instruction in general agricultural subjects The Director of Education was requested to com- municate with the counties mentioned with a view to arranging for a conference which the Board of Agricul- ture suggested. The committee is trying to secure a suitable farm in the neighbourhood of Crewe for the purcose of a farm institute.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>IN THE GARDEN.</head>
        <p>IN THE GARDEN.</p>
        <p>FLEETING iIiTT EFEEC flVE.—rt is quite possible to have a beautiful summer garden if one ;Ter° to make a selection of the many things that can be annually raised from seed, starting early in the year with those that must have heat, following on with those that require little or no heat, and later with those that may be sown in the open air where they are intended to bloom. Whilst we must admit says, "Farm and Home," that some annuals are very fleeting, yet the majority of them if grown properly are quite as long- lasting, some of them being far mora effective than the average summer bedding plants, as geraniums, calceolarias, etc. Annuals grown as one often sees them on a poor, hungry soul, the contents of a packet sown upon S space that would suffice for one properly- developed plant, is it little wonder that they are unsatisfactory raid never have a chance to show their value? 07: crowding results in poor growth, prema- ture flowering and early failure. POTATOES AWD TOMATOES.—Now that growers will very soon be thinking of planting out tomatoes on walls and borders, a word of warning may be oppor- tune, and that is not to plant them close to potatoes. Many years ago Nye remember a friend telling us that having a spire piece of wall in front of which potatoes were growing, he resolved to plant tomatoes on the wall. All went well until the potatoes took the dis- ease. From thtse it spread to the tomatoes, and quite destroyed them, It was noticeable, he told us, that the progress of the disease was much slower on the tomatoes, but this may have arisen from the different texture of the leaves,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>BIG SEWERAGE SCHEME.</head>
        <p>BIG SEWERAGE SCHEME.</p>
        <p>More Opposition to £18,500</p>
        <p>Expenditure on Sanitation.</p>
        <p>A PROTEST FROM GRAPPENHALL</p>
        <p>The big sewerage schemo for the parishes of Latchford Without, Grappenhall and Thelwall, gave rise to corsiderable diecussion at the meeting of the Runcorn Rural District Colucil on Tuesday. A pro- posal, emanating from Grappenhall, that another engineer should be consulted eith a view to preparing a less expensive scheme, was defeated.</p>
        <p>HIM-IT-OF-WAY DIFFICULTY. Mr. I ister-Kaye moved the following resolution : That having regard to the heavy cost of Mr. Diggles' scheme, and especially hiving regard to the fact that there is no right of road to the land proposed to be bought, and that it will be necessary to acquire such right of road, entailing a further heavy expense, another engineer be employed to advise on the whole scheme, to see if a less expensive scheme than the one pr,p3sed by Mr. Diggle can be suggested." Mr. Kaye said the object of his moving the resolu- tion was to save the parish very large expense. At the recent Local Government Board inquiry they un ter- stood, and the engineer appointed by the District Council (Mr. Diggle) stated, that the scheme would cost £18,500. lie (Mr. Kaye) also understood that Mr. Diggle had a free hand and concluded that the amount stated was not the cheapest scheme they could get. By private subscription they raised sufficient money to engage Mr. Mountain, another engineer, who said a scheme which would be achquate for at least 25 years, could be prepared at a cost of £12,000. After the inquiry he found that there was going to be consider- able difficulty about a right-of-way in Thelwall. To secure that, compulsory powers would have to be sought ani that would mean about £6OO or £7OO. They had no right-of-way to land which they hid bought, but he understood that if another engineer WM appo nted to consider the scheme outlined by Mr. Diggle, that obj,ction would be withdrawn. At Grappenhall they had a practically unanimous meeting in favour of a modifimi scheme. There was one dissentient.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>I/6 RATH.</head>
        <p>A I/6 RATH.</p>
        <p>He (Nfr Kaye) was proposing that resolution as a matter of £a. d. for the parish. The scheme meant at least a Is. Gd. rate. Rates were going up every years and it was not right that they should be saddled with that still greater increare. It was not right for one man to have a free band in the preparation of the scheme. If the scheme were approved, Mr. !Jiggle would receive 7E', per cent., and if it were abandoned he would rezeive 5 per cent, of the total proposed oust. There ought to have been some competitioe. It wasft smelling matter, but they at that end of the district smelled the most with a Is. 61 rate staring them in the faoe. Mr. Mountain said the sewerage work of the the three parishes could be done for £12,000. It was not likely that they oould pass that over. His sugges- tion was that they should call in another engineer, and If he agreed with Ir. Diggle then everything would be all right and the difficulty about the right-of-way rould be removed, tie believed they had a skilled engineer in Moore. Mr. Pilling had promised him twice thet he would second the resolution, hut he did not Fnow whether he would or not. People at the Grappenhall end of the rural district knew more about the matter than those at the Runcorn ed. They were absolutely unanimous in each parish that something should be done.</p>
        <p>"AFRAID OF SECONDING TUE RESOLUTION."</p>
        <p>Mr. Pilling admitted that he did promise to second the resolution, but it was on the spur of the mommt. On thinking it over, he thought the matter was a very serious one and there was no doubt about the serious- ness of appointing another engineer. It was almost a certainty that he w mld not agrte with the present engineer in every detail. They had had a large experi- ence of Mr. Diggle's work and he did his work well. Before they really went into the matter, he thought that Mr. Diggle should bo asked to meet the Grappenhall Sewerage Committee and that the whole question should be gone into. If they could see any possible way of reducing the cost, he was sure Mr. Diggle would do all he could to assist them. Ho was vcry much afraid of eeconling the resolution, because it was a serious matter.</p>
        <p>Mr. Lister-Eaye :If he is not going to second it-- Mr. Pilling : I feel I should be pacing myself in a very peculiar position, for that is a very serious matter. Mr. Kaye (appealing to the Chairman) : Ilas ho right to speak now,if he is not seconding the resolution? 'The Chairman : I take it Mr. Pilling is justified in making his explanation. (Hear, hear.) Mr. Kaye : I understood he was re-appointed district councillor on the understanding that he would go in for this.</p>
        <p>Mr. Pdlins : I wa3 not appointed on that ground at all.</p>
        <p>"THE MOST EFFECTIVE SCHEME FOR THE LEAST MONEY."</p>
        <p>Mr. J. W. Hayward raid be knew the pelple in the neighbourhood, and, ifreport were true, they would not have supported Mr. Pilliog, but have voted for Mr. liaye'e resolution. _</p>
        <p>111. Pilling : No, no. It was not mentioned. Mr. Hayward sail thy wanted to do the most effective thing for the least money. By passing tho resolution, they might reduce the cost very consider- ably, and he knew that had the hearty support of people in the neighbourhood, He seconded the resolution. Mr. Hallam, as one who knew the district, said some- thing would have to bo done in the near future. A DRASTIC STEP.</p>
        <p>Mr. Woodward, waking as an outsider and having no feeling with one party or the other, said he thought the general feeling was that they should as far as possible cut down elaborate expenditure necessitated by the sanitary arrangements for the growing parishes in the district, lie thought there was a way out of the present situation, without coming to such a drastic resolution as that proposed by Mr. Kaye. It was a serious thing to carry a scheme on as far as they had done, and then to have a proposal to appoint another engineer. It might mean the overthrow of the whole scheme, and a new scheme being placed before them. The scheme had been submitted to the necessary authorities, and they had a Local Government Board</p>
        <p>inquiry at wh'ch there did not appear to have been any v:tal opposition to the scheme. Mr. Kaye : There was strong opposition. Mr. Woodward : Had vital opposition been offered to the scheme in the earlier stages, I could have under- stood that resolution, but for the moment I am non- plussed. Continuing, he soggested that the scheme be reconsidered by the Grappenhall Sewerage Committee, in conjunction with Mr. Diggle, whom they should definitely ask if it were possible for the scheme to be made leas expensive. It was not right that one scheme should be thrown into the melting pot and another man engaged to bring about another scheme. What was the position of the District Council as regarded liability and expenditure on the present scheme ? The Chairman : If the scheme is knocked on the head, the cost will be about £9OO. Mr. Woodward: Will that have to be borne by the District Council ? Mr. Kayo: It would amount to saving us so greot a sum that it would not matter. THE CLERK'S EXPLANATION. The Clerk,speaking in regard to the land, said that pro- visional agreement was entered into by Admiral Jonas Parry, for whom Messrs. Robert Davies an Co., acted as solicitors, which gave the C tuncil rights with regard to access to the land. At the Local Government Board inquiry, the question was raised by counsel acting on behalf of the same solicitors for the adjoining owner, as to what the access was that Admiral Jones Parry had, it ens claime.l by them that it was only an agricultural right of aocesr. Messrs. Hobert Davies and Co., having full knowledge, did not feel justified in claiming any greater right of access than they were willing to give on the other hand for their other client. There was opposition at the inquiry, but he thought he was right in saying that the engineer called in by the opposition was not a'ole to lay his finger on a single point of weakness in Mr. Diggle'a scheme. The whole of the objection was on the ground of cost. Mr. Kaye : That is rat hr strong. LOCAL GOVERNMENT BOARD SATISFIED.</p>
        <p>The Clerk said that the Local Gomnment Board having heard all the opplsition raised and mentioned, which represented many interests, had stated to the D;strict Council as fairly as they could, before giving their sanction to the scheme, that subject to eerta'n alterations as to manholes, they would accept the szherne in its entirety, as they were perfectly satisfied nith it. He did not suppose tie engineer would raise any objection with regard to the land if,the members of the committee could show him similar suitable ground. It the Council thcught it advisable for the Committee to confer, he felt sure that the engineer would lay before them as fully as he could every possible point in connection with the old scheme, to see if it could be amended. Mr. Kaye : Can he put all this before the meeting? The eleck : The points have been raised. If yon appoint another engineer the scheme becomes abortive. They were liable to 5 percent. on Mr.Diggle's estimate. That would be charged against every parish within the Council's area, and the whole of the district would be charged one penny in the SCHEME REFERRED TO LOCAL COMMITTEE. Mr. Woodward moved an amendment to the effect that the matter be laid before the local Committee to see if it were possible to reduce the emit of the scheme. Mr. Linaker, sen., said they would do no good if they were going to have two doctors on the job. He asked Mr. Kaye if the £12,000 included the whole of the district.</p>
        <p>Mr. Kaye : Yes. The Clerk : It was distinctly stated at the Local Government Board ingliry that fhelwall would not be included in that.</p>
        <p>The Chairman- did not think it would be wise to carry Mr. liaye's resolution, as it was drawn up, as he could foresee difficulties would arise. He sympathised with the district in the increase in rates which over- shadowed them</p>
        <p>Mr. Kaye : Espcially if it is n)t necesstry. The Chnirman soggetted e friendly conference between Mr. Diggle and the representatives of the three districts. The right-of-way seemed to be the right to walk over but not to cut the land. It would be a thousind to one that they could not get another engineer to twee with Mr. Diggle's scheme. He thought the peisuasive powers of the joint Committee with Mr. Diggle would be far more effective than calling in another engineer. Mr. Linakor, son. : It is going to cost a lot to get Thel wall in the scheme.</p>
        <p>. . It was decided that the Chairman and Mr. Woodward Join the special committee. On the amendment to Mr. Kayo's resolution being put to the meeting, it was carried by 12 votes to seven.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>HOSPITAL NOT SUFFICIENTLY USED.</p>
        <p>e, .</p>
        <p>S</p>
        <p>When we travel we all try to leave troubles behind, but aches and pains arc apt to make themselves felt wherever we go, spoiling all enjoyment. Those Home Companions Holloway's Pills and Oint- ment—should also be Travelling Companions—. Remember! Holloway's Pills—the Pills which give strength—make you " Fit " and Well. Holloway's Ointment—the Ointment that soothes and heals. HOLLOWAY'S PILLS cure Constipation, Indiges- tion, Biliousness, Dizziness, and Sick-Headache, so frequently induced by change of air and other attendant conditions Holloway's Pills will be • found a sure and safe remedy. HOLLOWAY'S OINTMENT cures Cuts, Bruises, Abrasions, Rheumatism, Stiff Joints, Sprains and Strains, and quickly relieves Colds, Coughs, and affections of the Chest and Lungs.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of the Runcorn Rural District Council was held at Dutton on Tuesday, Mr. C. E. Linaker, presiding. HOSPITAL ENIPTY. The Medical Officer (Dr. Adams) reported two cases of scarlet fever, one from Appleton and the other from ilelsby. There was also one case of diptheria at Netherton ; one case of pulmonary tuberculosis at Frodsham ; and three other tuberculosis cams at Stockton Heath. When the report had been adopted, Mr. Woodward said be was sorry to sly that, notwithstanding that the hospital was entirely empty, people were not willing to send their patients to the institution. It was in the into-eats of the community that all oases of infections diseases eh )uld be sent to the hospital, the record of which was very good. (Hear, hear.)</p>
        <p>UNFAIR TO EMPLOYERS. On the proposition of gr. Whitlow, stsconded by Mr. Antwiss, the following resolution was passed : " That before a man is engaged on the highway it be ascer- tained whether ho has left his last situation in a proper manner." It was stated that one man had left his employer without giving any proper notice, which was not fair to the man wh3 employed him in the winter. The Chairman said it was not right that men should be taken away from their masters without proper warning.</p>
