      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>THE KING'S VISIT. TREAT FOR CRIPPLED CHILDREN.</p>
        <p>In connection with the visit uf the King and Queen to St. Helene, it has been arranged that accommodation will beprovided for the crippled children of Us town through the kindness of Mr. Watkia Oppenheim, who will lend a portion of his ground at the corner of Cowley Hill-lane and Bishops-road for the purpose. Mr. Fred Brown, organiser of the Crippled Children's Treat will superintend the arrangements and provide motor- cars for the conveyance of the children.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>STRIKERS ALLEGED THREAT TO KILL.</head>
        <p>STRIKERS ALLEGED THREAT TO KILL.</p>
        <p>A dispute affecting the jokers employed at Messrs. Pilkington Bros.' Cowley 11111 Glatsworks, St. llelens, had a scquel on Saturday at the local court, when three men, James Herbert Mattbews,Jesse Flodgette,snd John William Wollner, were charged with assaulting Sidney Pennington.</p>
        <p>There were charges Against Samuel WhittPker and Joseph Bart(,n for Reuniting John Wilson.</p>
        <p>Tte men at the works 'a-truck, and trouble arose between them and other joiners employed near by, who were working for private employers, and who, it is understood, lately obtained increased wage.. Mr. Douglas Hogg, for the prosecution, objected to an adjournment, and said there had been a threat to kill Penningtcn. Only formal evidence was given, however, end the Bench adjourned the hearing until to-day (Saturday). Mr. Kelly, for the accused, Enying he could not proceed as ha had only just been instructed. He felt it would be his duty to retire if the Bench proceeded then. On undertaking that they would not vis't the works in the meantime, the accused were allowed tall.</p>
        <p>CRUELTY TO CHILDREN. IS ST. HELENS A BLACK SPOT?</p>
        <p>A public meetine, to draw attention to the great need for more adequate support of the St. Helens branch of the National Sooiety for the Prelention of Cruelty to Children, was held on Monday afternoon at St. Helens Town Hall. /dr. E. H Cozens-Hardy presided, and said the local branch of the society had drawn upon head- quarters to the extent of £l,OOO from the amounts con- tributed to the central fund by branches in other parts of the country. They needed an additional inspector, but so far they had not subtrribed nearly sufficient for one.</p>
        <p>'I he Hon. A. Holland-HP•bert, of the society's Central Rxecutive Committee, said he had just read a report by the inspector of inspectors, who reported on some slum children just outside Liverpool as the worst he had ever seen, and on the top of that they had just received a report from the St. Helens Chief Constable, who said hat "In spite of the close attention given by their Iced inspector, there appeared little improvement, and that S. Helens was most certainly the worst town with which he bad been connected during his police career of twenty years. Tilers is about sufficient work here for four inspectors." During last year their one inspector dealt with 323 cases concerning the welfare of 1,076 children, which were new cases; in addition to those brought forward from the previous year. To cope with that work was more than one man could do. They must have another inspector, and the appointment of another inspector must depend upon the funds. Tbe Rev. A. Baines (vicar of St. Helena) moved a resolution commending the work of the society, and appealing for a greater measure of practical help. He said it was very often the rule to decry St. Helens, but be was very jealous of the reputation of the town, and would like to say that perhaps they had in proportion !o their total population a large number of people—he would say even more than in the Potteries, which he knew very well—than may other community in England belonging to the class from which sprang such cases as the society dealt with. When they said that, the fact' still remained that swab a number of cases bad been dealt with during the past sear as concerned 1,000 children. He thought the churches should do more than they had done in the past. Sir David Gamble seconded the resolution, which Mr. Percival Sharp supported, and it was carried unanimously. Dr. Buchan (hon. secretary) moved a vote of thanks to Mr. Ilibbert, and said he could certainly claim that side by side with the homes where children were neglected and ill-treated there was no town in England where greater care and greater love for children were shown than in the great majority of homes in St. Helens.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>PURE BEER BILL.</head>
        <p>A PURE BEER BILL.</p>
        <p>The text was issued on Saturday of a Bill to prohibit the use of hop substitutes and to provide for the mark- ing of imported hops, which was presented in the [louse of Commons by Mr. Courthope, Mr. .Wheler, Sir George Younger, and other members. The measure p-orides that a brewer is not to use any hop substitute in the brewing of beer for sale, or receive or keep any beer containing any hop substitute, or in the brewing of which any hop substitute has been used.. Contravention of these provisione renders a brewer liable to an excise penalty of £lOO and the forfeiture of the article. No hops are to be imported into the United Kingdom except in bags or pockets clearly inscribed outside with the name of the hop grower or the official seal of the distiict where grown.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="0" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
