      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, May 24, 1913. BY-ELECTION LESSONS.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, May 24, 1913. BY-ELECTION LESSONS.</p>
        <p>Councillor Kendall on Charges Against the Government.</p>
        <p>TORY GOSPEL OF EXPEDIENCY.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Progressive Ltbour League resumed its open-air campaign on Monday evening, when a succef•sful meeting was held near the Old Mill, at Bridge Foot. The principal speaker, Councillor James Kendall, of Manchester, draw some useful lessons from the by-elections, and also touched upon the principal measure of reform upon which the Government is engaged. It wee announced that Mr. A. H. Crotileld would address a meeting under the auspices of the League on Monday, June 2nd. TIIE NEWMARKET RESULT.</p>
        <p>Mr. F. W. JAMES, who presided, said their opp-nents in Warrington seemed to be indulging in jollification at the resnit of the Newmarket election. They were, however, (pito welcome to any satisfaction they could derive from the methods they had employed in that campaign. (Hear, hear.) The Tories seemed to have forgotten the by-election at Londonderry, ehich showed that even Ulster now had a majority of Home Rulers, and they also ignored the way in ehich they abandoned their principles at Kendal and supported a Cozetervative Free Trader in order to gain the scat He (the speaker) had never yet heard of a by-election at which the Liberal candidate had stood as a Tariff Reformer. The Liberal party always stood by its principles and fought elections clots-handed, (Applause.) Some of the Tory newspapers were itoting that, after the Newmarket result, the Government ought to resign. In view of the fact that they had a majority of over 100 behind them, there was no need vsha•ever to take that course, and they would have the sat is'ac- lion of placing on the statute book such measures 96 Home Rule and Welsh Disestablishment, wh'oS they had already piloted through the House of Commons. (Applause.) THE AWAKENING OF RURAL ENGLAND.</p>
        <p>Councillor KENDALL said tbat judging from the importance which their opponents were attaching to the Newmarket result, one might be jnatifled in concluding that Newmarket was the hub of the political universe. On inquiry, however, be found it was a place noted for the rearing and breeding of horses and j^ckeys— (laughter)—and not generally a place which they would regard as a political centre. Because the Tories had won a by-election there, the people were asked to believe that it, was the end of all things, especially of Radical things—(laughter)—and the Government was expected to resign forthwith. Meantime, Mr. Asquith and Mr. Churchill were touring round the Mediter- ranean ; 'Warrington looked as if there had not been an great cataclysm ; and Manchester was pursuing its business as well as it could after the Whit week holidays. (Laughter.) No matter. how enlightened and up-to-date the dwellers in towns might he, with regard to political affairs, there were many agricultural districts still under the grip of the old inffience which had so long held them down. Yet, even in those rural parts there was evidence of a remarkable awakening, and the men who possessed votes and who had hitherto used them simply as they were told, were beginning to think for themselves. When they fully realized what the different political parties stood for, they would find what a mistake they had made in supporting those who wished to rule them as they had been kept in sub- jection in the past. TARIFF REFORM THROWN OVERBOARD.</p>
        <p>Liberals were not by any means ashamed of the results of the by-elections which had taken place since the Government was last returned to power. The Tories bad managed to win eleven rents in two and a half years and the Libersls had won one, so that the Tories had a net gain of ten. Of those, three were presented to them by an unfortunate split in the progressive ranks. Therefore, in two and a half years the Tories had the remarkable gain of seven seats—a proof that the Government had survived that period with le.s loss of populaiity among the voters than any previous Government. (Applause.) by-electiors had taught them something with regard to the policy—or want of policy—(f their opponer.ts. No chapter in the history of this country was so brimful of messures of practical use- fulness for the masses of the people es the past six years bad been. Any Liberal was justified in feeling proud of the Government which had established such a record. On the other band, it was just as important that the people should know what the Tory party hat to offer, and, if the by-elections had done nothing elre, they had thrown some light upon the wonderful secrecy with which the tory party had got together • what they called their programme. At the Kendal election they threw over the first constructive measure which they had sworn to maintain at all costs and which they had almost wrecked their party in maintaining. They adopted a candidate, who, because of his Free Trade views, had previously been estracised by his party in Kendal, and when be succeeded on the Free Trade ticket the Tory party in the House of Commors said: " Well, as be has won, we will recognise him." (Laughter.) That election proved tbat Tariff Reforu was practically dead, as far as the Tory party were concerned, and thay were looking round for a nice, comfortable way of giving it a decent interment.</p>
        <p>BRINGING THE CONSTITUTION UP-TO,D.kTE.</p>
        <p>At the by-elections one of the great cries of the Tory party tam that the Radlonia had destroyed tbe constitution. What bad actually happened, however, was that the House of Lords had been put into its pryer place as a reasonable, instead of the dominating part of the Constitution. The Tori.ts were fond of talking about the destruction of the Constitution, but they overlooked the fact that one of their own proposals for the reconstruction of the House of Lords was to tnke from the King one of his most valuable prerogatives—that of creating peers—and one of the greatest safeguards of the people. The Liberals had, far from damaging the Constitution, toned it up and brought it up-to-date.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>ALLEGED ATTACK ON RELIGION.</head>
        <p>THE ALLEGED ATTACK ON RELIGION.</p>
        <p>Another charge brought against the Government by their opponents was that they were attacking religion. Ile Bishop of Chester had writ ten a letter urging the Liberals of Altrincham to put their Cnurch before their politics or their country—to put the Interests of their Church before the interests of the people. lie said every Liberal should vete Tory this time because " these wicked Radicals "had made an attack upon religion. Personally he (the epenker) bad spent many years in what he had alwnys thought was religious work tmong the children in the slums of Manchester. To gay th•t Itadicals were engaged in attacking religion was a foul charge to bring against them. The Radical party was dominated by religious men. Thera never 'vas a Cabinet in the history of this oountry which was 50 full of mr.n of true religicus feeling and thought. Church that could nt live w:thout Fotablishment and Endowment would die sooner or later. The Churches of the twentieth ern' ury-must be built upon and supported by the love of the pop le. All that the Government intended to do was to tree religion from State shackles. They believed religion had sufficient force, power and goodness in itself to command the respect and love of the pecple, without money endow- ments or embellishments. Some of the best apns and daughters of the Church were supporters of thrt great emancipation movement, believing that the Church would be stronger and better NN hen freed from all Sate control and interference. The Welsh Church Bill would pass, in spite of the opposition of narrow- minded bigots and th, se who were anxious to maintain those splendid positions which wou!d be endangered when they were dependent upon the people who had to pay for them. IRELAND'S JUST DEMANDS.</p>
        <p>Dealing with Home Rule, Councillor Kendall stated that seven-eights of the people of Ireland wanted it and one-eighth did not, The new theory of the Tory party was that they must let the one-eighth IMO their oWn way and make the seven-eighths bow down to their will, The Govern- ment were restoring to Ireland the right of self- government which was taken from her by fraud over 100 years ago. It would be a great mistake if they ever attempted to crush out the spirit of nationality which had never died out of the Irish nation. (Hear, hear.) He (the speaker) was glad to have lived long enough to see the certainty of justice being done to Ireland, and he was proud to belong to the party which had stood consistently for that magnificent policy, although they forfeited power and had to go into the ilderness for twenty years because they were faithful to the Irish people. The ell )rts of the Orangemen to disturb the people of this country had failed because it 'if as now realized, not only by Radicals, but by a large section of the Tory party, that the only way to retain I eland as a loyal part of the Empire was to recognise her just demands and give her the right which all o r Colonies enjoyed, to govern herself. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>WHAT MR. HAROLD SMITH s rAND's FOR.</head>
        <p>WHAT MR. HAROLD SMITH s rAND's FOR.</p>
        <p>Finally, the speaker said that when Mr. Harold Smith again came before the electors of Warrington, he hoped they would not forget that he stood for opposition to the reforms r.hich had made the history of the LibErai party so great during the past six years, for restoring the House of Lords to its old position of prestige aud power, for putting hick the clock in Ireland, and,instead</p>
        <p>For tculars, iLod, ...ement and dgration Camroisaioner, Victorian Clovernrnent Office, Strand, London, W.C.</p>
        <p>of giving justice to seven-eighths of the peOple,yielding to the vulgar outcry of the one-eighth who claimed to over-ride the majority. When they realised what Mr. Smith stood for, he believed the electors of Warring- ton would have no hesitation, wl,en the opportunity came, in giving a very different verdict from that which tbey recorded at the last election, and returning a roan who would be on the side of those movements which were for the betterment of the masses of the people, the uplifting of all and the injury of none. (Applause.) MISREPRESENTATION OF THE INSURANCE</p>
        <p>VI RIA (AUSTRALIA); LAND of SUNSHINE, FRUIT &amp; FLOWERS. For Farmers, Farm Workers, British Lads tor Far Work, Dowesbies and Artisszkg, V?" pAi• %v\N*' . 0 ~cV.,inv 041/4 parti, e s t?:..i7 ploPij Aiel.' "-- ‘JiheTt'tle,-:</p>
        <p>CTO</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>AT THE RACES.</head>
        <p>AT THE RACES.</p>
        <p>[COPYRIGHT.]</p>
        <p>All ways lead one way. From north, south, east, and west, a steady and full flow of humanity pours unceasingly in the direction of a common centre. Tramcars and trains, sprightly hansoms and wheezy four wheelers, swiftly-moving motor-cars and rowdy wag- gonettes, carry their impatient burdens through the long black lines of pedestrians, who seek their destination with typical English delibera- tion. At all the spots where traffie converges, dozens of boys do a roaring trade in selling "specials'. which give all the latest news re- lating to the horses about to run and the prophecies of experts concerning the issues of impending contests. Men of every age and condition study the information given with knitted brows, making here and there a hurried pencil mark against the name of some fancied horse, or comparing the lists of probable winners given by different authorities.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>ALL SORTS AND CONDITIONS.</head>
        <p>ALL SORTS AND CONDITIONS.</p>
        <p>Mechanics and artisans of every description, publicans, clerks, colliers, farmers, grooms, men dressed in professional style, and labourers, with here and there a group of übiquitous office boys, jostle each other in the procession. Long before the racecourse is reached vehicular traffic has come to a standstill; hawkers of race- cards, lead pencils, and cheap tips, add to the busy hum of life which circlets round the huge enclosure, where the multitude splits up into various sections, according to the prices they are prepared to pay for admission. Members and favoured friends enter the innermost enclosure, where they have the privi- lege of inspecting the horses and their riders as they parade before the races are run off. and where reliable information relating to the chances of each candidate is supposed to bc available. The next stand, admittance to which is inferior in price only to the inner ring itself, is frequented by the class which is con- tent to hang in the wake of the recognised aristocracy in racing matters, and as the cheaper places are only separated from the superior ones by iron bars, there is always the chance of beholding some of the great people, or even of hearing advice which may be worth following. So the enclosures grade off until a point is reached, furthest from the finishing post, where the cheapest places are.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>FLAT CATCHEES,</head>
        <p>FLAT CATCHEES,</p>
        <p>Hero may be seen cadgers and tipsters of every type, shouting or whining to their clientele in the fashion peculiar to racc-cours, frequenters. Here is a swarthy, clean shaven man, with a voice of thunder, who command the attention of a large circle. "What did tell you yesterday ?" he repeatedly derne.nds. while his eye-balls threaten to leave their sockets. Receiving no reply to his challenge, he continues with increased emphasis "I gave Boodle! I gave Mirage and Snail! Prancing Tortoise and Sleepy Weasel! A nap! gentleman which brought me in £lO from the Daily Dotter in this envelope, as you may see for yourselves. I get £2 for a winner and £5 for a nap. I don't want your money! ' I am here to do you a good turn. I give you five winners for one shilling! My price to the Daily Dotter is £lO. "Who an I ?" I'm Tom Bunkum! The man who hat the private ear of every horse and jockey ii Lord Diddlehem's otable, and am known on every race course in Dngland! One shilling only." Whereupon Torn does a flourishing trade, his customers including several ladies. A little further on is another tipster whc vigorously insists upon offering the sum of £5( to any person who can produce one cf the tipster's cards of the preceding day which did not name every winner. One stolid looking spectator muttered audibly, "Why, you weren't here yesterday!"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>BOISTEROUS "BOOKIES."</head>
        <p>BOISTEROUS "BOOKIES."</p>
        <p>By this time the bookmakers, having set up their stalls, set to work. One of them, with a cash bag strapped round his shoulder by means of a broad belt studded with crowns and sovereigns, was surrounded by a crowd of curious youth, one or two of them evidently being grooms from the country. As they st‘Jod with wide-open mouths, gazing with staring eyes and hands in pockets, one of them whispered to his companion "Say, Jem! Think these is real?" "Beal!" interrupted the bookmaker. "Feel 'em, my lad! You'll find 'em as real as you are; there's no fear of this firm breaking—you can stake all your money or that!" Then he resumed his cry "Three to one, bar." The approach of the first roe( causes the noise of the bookmakers' voices to increase a hundredfold. There was one bookmaker who evidently worked on a. different system. This he explained to an at- tentive audience. "I am a poor man myself," said he, "and, like you, can take few risks." If you want to speculate with half a crown, give it tome and pick your choice. My commisedon is twelve and a half per cent., and I hand over all the rest to the backer of the winner." This method of gaming evidently met with popular approval, judging by the number of half- crowns which were transferred to the book- maker. Not far away, sitting on a comfortable bank of rich green grass, is a whole family. father, mother, and all the children down to the baby, taking lunch in approved picnic fashion; they seem to be little troubled by the prevailing excitement. Their intention is evidently to indulge in the pleasure of side- shows, the actual racing being quite a secondary consideration. Another group, composed chiefly of young men, is busily engaged in following the operations of a lottery. The presiding genius sells cards, each containing a number, at one shilling each. When eight have been sold the operator takes one of the shillings for himself, draws a duplicate number, and hands seven shillings to the holder of the winning card. Soon the races are in full rising; large sums of money change hands with the conclu- sion of each race. A BAD DAY.</p>
        <p>It has been a bad day for the bookmakers, because the favourite horses in nearly, every race have for once won. A party of three in one of the select enclosures seems to have hr.(' a particularly trylng time. Great crowds cf successful "backers," arranged in single hi- await payment from this combination, whic proclaims itself as the "strongest and safr,, turf agency in England. As one pal-tiler soothing the impitient °nen with prY-miscs thr all their demn.n&amp; will Fllnrtiv satisfiee another Is booking bets for the next race, &amp;IA the third ;45 paying out the deposited money as fast as it comeccin; and in this way hue.inesA is kept goirg until the last race is over: then the crisis ,comes. The crowi surges round the temporary stand of the bookmaker. threaten- ing it with instant destruction. Altogctlior th, principals seems to b^ in for an ugly time. Thrre is very little cash left in the bag to sati.fy de- mands, and while desperate efforts are being made to borrow money elsewhere, the book- maker in charge ptHi.es back his white sin hat and endeavours to gain time by paying out small amounts, or by censuring the b9l,lpt spirits f'.%r their want qf conadenz.</p>
        <p>"Shall we be able to pay up F" he loudly shouts in reply to the question of a timid in quirer. "Hand ycr card in 'ere, young feller "How much is it, Dick P" he asked. turni.nr to the clerk. "Ten shillings, sir." "Ter bob!" exclaimed the cashier contemptuoq.ly as ho counted the amount. "I thour,ht it would 'ave been a hundred quid at least! Herr you are, my tad, and mind you don't lose it." Other bookmakers now began to appear with substantial offers of assistance; one brought a bundle of banknotes, another a small bag of gold, and a third a large amount of silver. The business of paying out then commenced in earnest; but it was not until quite a late hour that the last creditor was satisfied. Then the members of the "strongest and safest :.titf agency in England" took off their hats and mopped the perspiration from their faces. Even for them a day at the races has its perild.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>RusluN gius, L“Ey.</head>
        <p>THE RusluN gius, L“Ey.</p>
        <p>The Icing, to encourage the attempt of the hfanx Chamber of Commerce to revive the textile indus- tries of the Isle of Man, has ordered from the Ruskin Mills, Laxey, a quantity of homespun tweeds which have been diapatched to Buckingham Palace; These mills were founded on the suggestion of the late John Ruskin.—" Daily Telegraph."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>Act'.</head>
        <p>Act'.</p>
        <p>Mr. E. WYNNE JONFS said the Newmarket election had been won by 'abuse and misrepresentation of the Insurance Act. On the other hand, the Tory candidate at Kendal said he regarded it as a great measure which ought to have a fair chance. l'be Tories ought ,to be the lest to taunt the Government with trying to break up the Empire, when !hey themselves were constantly fouling their own nest. It was an insult to the British worker to any that he needed Tariff Reform to enable him to compete with the foreigner. The Empire had been built up and could be maintained by trusting the people. By giving self- government to the people of South Africa we had blotted put the memories of the bitter hatred and sub- hti:uted loyalty. The same result would be achieved in Ireland. (Applause.) Sir Edward Carson had been refk rred to as the modern Mesas, hut the comparison was an insult to the ancient Moses who stood between the people and the p:ague, whereas Sir Edward Carson was engaged in spreading the plague. Sir Ed,vard professed ta derive comfort from the Newmxrket election, yet the leading Tory papal* had declared that Horne Rule and the Welsh Church Bill had had nothing to do with the result. The time would come when the Tory party would ne longer dare to misrepresent the Insurance Act—in fact they would not do it in the towns now, because its benefits were so thoroughly appreciated. (Applau:e.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>RAILWAY CLERKS' CONFERENCE.</head>
        <p>RAILWAY CLERKS' CONFERENCE.</p>
        <p>Nationalisation Recommended in the Interest of Employes and Public.</p>
        <p>The sixteenth annual conference of- the Railway Clerks' Atmciatiou was held at York last Saturday and Sunday. Tnere were over 210 delogates in attendance, the Warrington Branch, which is 120 strong, being repre- sented by Mr. J. Dutton. THE INCREASE IN RATES: NATIONALISATION SUGGESTION.</p>
        <p>Mr. Romeril, in his presidenLial address, mentioned that during the last seven years, the membership had increased from 4,030 to 20,579. lle said the additi ous already secured to railway clerks salaries' scales needed to be increased by at least three times tb raise railway clerks to an equality with the. Civil Service, and they had an undeniable claim to similar conditions of employ- ment. No practical and earnest railwayman could tail to recognise the fauTts and dangers of the railway system as at present organised. Toe public dissatisfac- tion viith the present management of the industry was &amp;Hy becoming more pronounced, and he had no hesita- tion in vying that the present system could not ccn• tinue. The increase in fares and other charges already instituted, and the proposal to raise freight rates by four per cent. in spite of the large. growth in net receipts enjoyed by the railway companies, would never have taken place had the railways of this country been brought under public oe nership and control as intended by the late M. Gladstone nearly seventy years age. To-day these new priva'e taxes were being imposed by the assistance of the G vernment itself. It was obvious to evcry intelligent wage-earner that this process necessarily resulted in all trade union effort being nulliflod ty increases in the cost of living. .(Applause.) The solution of all the &amp;fa:ditties attendant upon nationalisation could be reached by a National Railway Board, made up of three prineiral elements, one-third to consist of representatives of the users of the rail- ways, to be elected by suzh bodies as Chambers of Commerce, one—third of Members of Parliament,includ- ing a Minister of Railways, who would set as president of the Board ; and all appointel by the Government of the day, and subject to being changed with the change In the Government ; and the remaining third to be composed of e!ecied representatives of the railway workers. The new authority sheuld be set up apart from the State itself, and whilst it should not go to the Chancellor of the Exchequer for grants in aid, neither should he have the right to commandeer its surpluses. (Applause,) With such an arrangement be felt confident that the interests involved would be fairly balaneed. NORTII,EASTE3N CLERKS' SALARIES.</p>
        <p>The standing orders were sn-pended to enable the question of the salaries paid to the North-Eastern Rail- wy staff to be diecusted.</p>
        <p>Mr. W. El. Ainsworth (West Hartlepool and Whitby) moved the following resolution :—" That this Confer- ence of railway clerks protests against the unsatis- factory manner in which the management of the North- Eastern Railway have handled the petitic.n of the clerks for improved salary conditions, particularly in not granting an interview to the salary deputation, and hereby 1115tru0:5 the Executive of the Railway clerks' Association to press the management for a speedy interview, and that failing an early reply in the afilrma- tive Parliamentary action be taken at once." The resolution was seconded by Mr. Chambers (liddlebrough). it was decided to inclu le ate South-Eastern Railway in the resolution, whicii was carried unanimously. The President announced that the North-Eastern and the South-Eastern were the only two companies in the kingdom which had denied a board-rot:oi interview to their clerical staff.</p>
        <p>Resolutions were passed condemning 'alleged attempts by railway offis.lials to intinti.late railway clerks and stationmasters from joining a trade union infavour of tbe nationalisation of railways and canals and sailing for Government inapt orlon of railway offices. and the adoption of statutory regulations in regard to sinita- tion, ventilation, and limitaticn of hours of labour. It was agreed to urge on the Government the neces- aity for ob:ainiog full information regarding rates of pay, hours of labour, and other c, nditions of all grades of railway workers, and to ascertain the amount of additional annual revenue resulting from the increased railway charges. Resolutions were also adopted in favour of a standard scale of wages for railway clerical workers, of improved remuneration for positions of more than ordinary responsibility ; in favour of higher salaries and improved conditions of service for stationmasters and agents,to be approximate to those paid to postmasters. It was alsu resolved to urge all branches to use their ir,fluenca to induce the local health authorities in their respectiye areas to provide adequate sanatoria treatment for all sufierers under the National Insuranco Act, QENKRAL SUB.IEC-T§.</p>
        <p>The Conference was resumed on Sunday morning, when a private session was held. At this it was decided to form a political fund on the Jambi of a levy of Gd. per member per annum, which on the present member- ship would produce .C.a9o a year. A long discussion also ensued on the question of fusion with other railway unions, but the matter was not pressed to a vote, In the subsequent open session a variety of subjects were dealt with, The Executive were instructed to organise a national agitation for the absorption of all labour liberated by working agreements or labour- saving devices in such a way as would reduce the work- ing hours of staffs generally. Reference was made to the growing employment of women clerks on railways, and a resolution was passed declaring that they should ho paid on the same scale as men clerks, and "should fairly work round all turns with the men." An emphatic protest was also carried against the intro- duction of the "premium " or " superior " clerk into tha railway service and the preferent'al promotion into good positions of "comparative outsiders" with only two or three years' service. Another resolution called for the complete reversal of the Osborne judgment. By a large majority and without discussion, the con- ference passed a resolution in favour of adult suffrage, "so that working women may be enabled by means of political power to have some opportunity of improving their economic position and of meeting the serious situation created by. the continued increase in the cost of living." Mr. Chandler (Sheffield) moved" that this Conference enters an emphatic proteat against the action of .the Government in endeavauring to suppress the organ of the W.S,P.U., the " Suffragette," believing this to be a direct attack on the liberty of the Press which has until recently been the boast of this country. It further denounces the infringement upon the right of free speech as evidenced by the prohibition of the women's meetings in Hyde Park," The resolution was carried unanimously,</p>
      </div>
