      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <p>LOCAL CONGREGATIONAL</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>PROGRESS,</head>
        <p>PROGRESS,</p>
        <p>, WYCLIFFE EFFORT FOR THE rADGATE-LANE SCHOOL CHAPEL THE NEW SPIRIT IN CONGREGATIONALISM</p>
        <p>• large eonward march of Congregationalism is evidenced • te in an effort to cope with tbe religious needs and rapidly growing district by the erection • °f a school -chapel in Padgate-lane. The building is .e_opected to be completed in July and this week tha • _° ngregation of the parent church —Wycliffe—have alzde an effort, by means of a two-days' sale of work, t.(3, c°l33Plete their promised contribution of .:c_:3so to • . ,_L .th raeme and to provide a surplus for the needs of own church. Great interest had been taken in 'Lie enterprise by the lady workers, and when the sale 13‘1,1),,,e,ned in the Memorial Ball, Edgeworth-street, on eunesday afternoon, the stalls were most attractively • arranged " ged and heavily laden with a great variety of -Irates.. • '1:110 opening ceremony was performed by Mr. J. Hue ortb, of Bowdon, under the presidency of : the /I.B'. Rigby Armitage. Among others prexent were (fte.v. T. T. James), the Rev. W. BracecamP, . • "sfs. Ziba Armitage, J. Ball (secretary of the build- - itg f E Wallington J. Longland, S. P. Silcock \vre,uitect of the new chapel), E. S. Garland, U. boalLuagton, W. H. Payton and S. W. Jeffery, Mrs. ' Abs. Henry Roberts, and Mrs. Bracecamp. ,'"Yer Was offered by the Rev. W. Bracecamp. A HAPPY OUTLOOK. BALL, in an explanatory statement, said the new • 03071-chapel in Padgate-lane was expected to be ready thas,t !Ile middle of July, the architect (Mr.Sticok) and • ltwoUllders (Messrs. Dolan and Son)having carried out ‘Ftirk most expeditiously. Owing to the holidays .ornw Opening would be deferred until September. (jaa building would seat about :350 and bad been so osesigued as to be excellently adapted for school pur- s,'!eit• It would consist of a schoolroom, a large ball, 1,41° guild room behind, and a number of class rooms, rentdell _Would be entirely separate from each other but er• Led accessible by means of corridors. The ' that the was a very fine structure, and they were hope- to -6at the building,when completed, would be a credit (Ap"gregationalism and to religion generally. nh aau- ) The total estimated cost, including laud Aurnishing, was £2,900. The .£4OO required for build.°.te had already been raised. As to the laa7 "lag fund, they were much gratified to e received such handsome support from Wycliffe taietids and many outside supporters, including !he • ill'es,4sh.ire Congregational Union and the Chapel Build- • ks°t.Y. The total promises up-to-date amounted a-so which included undertakings by Wycliffe as .£2o(!itimurilty to raise £350 and by Cockhedge to raise m was The outstanding amount from Wycliffe, i which t wo„,u,°Ped to raise by that sale was .C 272 and any surplus ret„74 go to their ordinary Church expenses. Mr. Ball ascCred to the rapid growth of the Padgate-lane district exter,Ba,id it presented an admirable opening for the buti."3loo of religious enterprise. They hoped the Play.B of worshippers at present at Cockhedge would 2 'NC an admirable centre from which to start a new the:re hi a new district. As far as they could gauge (4liPakuse, the prospects were of a very happy character. • .) 16,THE GOOD WORK AT COCKIIEDGE. „ had 'dffAIRMAN said that for nearly fifteen years they ' dat heart the desire to enlarge Cockhedge Mission Of tha Present enterprise was the result of the efforts ope;.7.Wyclitle and Cockhedge people to expand their I.°oB in a better neighbourhood. The work done gatiedge mission—helped largely by the emigre- tap el Wycliffe Church—had been enormous. He whoPi.ashed to know several people who bad given up the lottiid7 of their time to the collection of pence for the fund and they had raised a considerable amount flitd „-" way. It was a proud moment to know that they ' tts-cauired the land and that they would shortly have ' 'Art mission after such a long period of struggle. • ePtause.) Er,W SPIRIT IN CONGREGATIONALISM. ,200OIER HAWORTH said they were holding the l:et,:t a Must opportune moment in the history of ati,,,regation