      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>'TIT- LORD ADVOCATE'S STIRRING APPEAL TO THE</head>
        <p>'TIT- LORD ADVOCATE'S STIRRING APPEAL TO THE</p>
        <p>ELECTORS OF LYMM.</p>
        <p>Candi dr. L. Kay-Shuttleworth (L). slr. G. C. Hamilton (C). Decei : J. R. Kebty-Fletcher (C), 8,002 J. Crossley (L), 7,883 ; ons. majority 119.</p>
        <p>UNIONIST INSURANCE ACT BETRAYAL.</p>
        <p>Insurance Act by the Conservatives : a lucid explana- tion of the Land Act ; a few trenchant remarks about the attitude of the opposition with regard to trade unions, Tariff Reform, and an account of the working of conscription in Australia and New Zealand, formed the principal items on the programme. Mr. R. Barker was in the chair.</p>
        <p>Heatley Ward was the f.scene of activities on Thurs- day, the Conservatives holding a meeting at Rush Green, their opponents assembling in the Oughtrington School. The arrangements for last night (Friday) were a Unionist gathering at Broornedge ; and a Liberal attack on Heatley Heath,which thfy successfully assaulted last week. Both parties have arranged for meetings at the Cross to-night, a plan which will certainly make fir excitement, if nothing more. The Free Trade Union have also been busy in the district.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>7 ; NEXT WEDNESDAY.</head>
        <p>7 ; NEXT WEDNESDAY.</p>
        <p>tlie Altrincham Division takes pla Jednesday and the Liberals are ma g and, it is hoped, a successful effc . back the seat that was held for thf le late Sir W. J. Crossley from ember 1910.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>IHE NOMINATIONS.</head>
        <p>IHE NOMINATIONS.</p>
        <p>The High Sheriff (Mr. Alfred Watkin) attended at the Altrincham Court House on Tuesday to receive the nominations. This was the first meeting of the candi- ates and they shook hands with the heartiness of old school friends. After business had been dorie with they made the 'principal figures in a group which all the photographers said it was quite a pleasure to take.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>Conservatives Who Detest Tariff Reform.</head>
        <p>Conservatives Who Detest Tariff Reform.</p>
        <p>Twenty-five papers were handed in on each side. An attempt is made to give some significance ,in the way of a turnover of votes to the fact that One of Mr. Hamilton's papers was signed exclusively by Conservative Free-traders, led by Mr. F. R. B. Lindsell. It is a notorious matter that there are many Conserva- tives in Altrincham who detest the very name of Tariff Reform, and who carried their detestation to the length of voting against the 'Tory candidate in 1906. Bin having regard to the system of the Ballot Act, it is not at all easy to fix their number, and those who say that 900 Conservative Free-traders actually votea Liberal in 1900 are obviously making nothing better than a guess. Whatever the number it does have been (says the " Manchester Guardian '') t does not matter in the least uow. Disturbed by Liberal Budgets, and perhaps encouraged by the thought that Tariff Reform had been battered out of practical politics, all the Conservative Free-traders who had not actually thrown in their lot with the Liberals had made their peace with the party by the end of the 1906 Parlia- ment, and it is pretty generally accepted. that their votes have gone against the Liberal candidate at the last two elections. The fact that some of them joined in nominating Mr. Hamilton on Tuesday does not therefore affect the calculations or the hopes of the Liberal electioneers.</p>
        <p>Liberal Churchmen and Mr. Kay-Shuttleworth. The majority of Mr. Hamilton's supporters in the nominations were, of course, members of the Church of England, sound, true-blue Church and State men. But one finds DO authority for the suggestion that they were reinforced this time by a number of "Liberal Churchmen," who are going Conservative for the first time over Welsh disestablishment. The politician who left his party in the days of the Crimean War or the disestablishment of the Irish Church, and who suddenly persuades himself that be would have done no such thing if the Insurance Act, or whatever other topical iniquity happens to be going, had not taxed a patience long since exhausted is of course a familiar figure at .the ," nominations." He appears to have taken his place again in the parade on Tuesday. Diligent and expert scrutiny has failed to discover in Mr. Hamilton's papers the name of any Liberal, Churchman or otherwise, who has been recognised as such in time-, that count. But that Mr. Shuttleworth's papers contain the names of many sound Churchmen who support Welsh disestablishment as Churchmen and as Liberals is not open to question.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>MR. SHUTTLEWORTH'S NOMINATIONS.</head>
        <p>MR. SHUTTLEWORTH'S NOMINATIONS.</p>
        <p>The principal nomination papers for the Liberal candidate, the Hon. Lawrence Ughtred Kay-Shuttle- worth, Esq., Gawthorpe Hall, Burnley, were as follows :</p>
        <p>Proposed by William Ernest Thompson, 16, Orchard-road, Alttincham ; Becohded by John Alfred Morris, Oak House, Dunham-road, Altrincham. Assentors: Francis Milne, Bowdon; John Houghton Brancker, Bowdon ; Arthur Adlington Howarth, Dunham Massey; leville Clegg, Dunham Massey; William Armitage. Altrincham; Thomas William Killick, Bowdon ; George Arthur Pickard, Altrincham ; Joseph Henry Greenhow, Bowdon; David Ainsworth Little, Bowdon; and William Hughes, S:de. Proposed by John Noden, 2G, Lyme-street, Stock- port; seconded by John Barrodale, 50, Chatham- street, Stockport. Assentors: Robert Williams Gudgeon, Edward Shirley, Thomas Allcock, John Arthur Birkett, Robert Johnson, William Hepple, Arthur White, Alfred Raynor Alcock, ,Levi Benjamin Hadfield and John Thomas Hopkins ; all of Stockport. Proposed by George Arthur Pickard, Race Hill, Dunham-road, Altrincham ; seconded by William Robert Speechley, 16, Gladstone-road, A ltrincham. Assentors : William Sefton Guest, William Henry Morgan, William Walsh, William Richardson, James Hamer, George William Gotobed,John Henry Bamber, Oswin Lomax, Alfred Pettitt and Peter Foy ; all of Altrincham.</p>
        <p>Proposed by Henry Edwin Gaddum. The Priory, Bowdon, seconded by Henry Richmond Hutton, Breeze Hill, Fir•;-road, Bowdon. Assentors : Francis Potts, Arthur Brotherton Harvey,Harold Frederick Simpson ,Richard Hammond, Charles Price, Frank Ashworth, John Broady, William Bampson, George Podmore Brundritt, and Harold Stubbs Greenhow ; all of Bowdon.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>CAMPAIGN AT LYMM.</head>
        <p>THE CAMPAIGN AT LYMM.</p>
        <p>Despite the long-drawn-out campaign, political feeling in the Lynim district waxes stronger with the approach of polling day; party colours are becoming more prominent and as a theme of conversation politics have run all other interests off the field—even the Waterworks has become a dry subject. If anybody speaks on ordinary topics nobody will listen, but he has only to refer to the coming election to have all the township for an audience. On Saturday both candi • dates visited the locality, Mr. Hamilton having a busy time in the. evening addressing meetings in the Statham, Thelwall, and Oughtrington schoolrooms,and also in the Broom Edge Mission Room. At the former meeting the Conservative candidate had rather a rough time with the hecklers, his comparisons between a local Friendly Society and the Insurance Act, much to the detriment of the latter, being resented by some members of the audience. Mr. Kay-Shuttleworth paid a flying visit in the afternoon and met with a cordial reception at Booth's-hill. In the evening he spoke at meetings at Partingtou and Carrington, whilst the local Liberals held an open-air meeting at the Cross. On Monday evening a Liberal meeting under the presidency of Mr. J. B. D. Smith was held down Newfield, the Junior Conservatives have a gathering at Booth's Hill. Many people of both political parties took advantage of the visit of the Lord. Advocate on Tuesday to hear both that gentleman and the Candi- date in the Drill Hall ; whilst the Tory speakers visited Heatley and Warburton. Wednesday found the Con servatives, with Mr. T. F. W. Dixon as chairman, making another attack on Newfield, when the principal speakers were Mr. R. Smylie (of the Unionist Association of Ireland), a Dublin barrister, and Mr. W. H. Finch, whose address on the land question, Home Rule, and the Insurance Act, gave riae to many quaint remarks which the audience were not slow to take advantage of, with the result that there were plenty of good-humoured punctuations. Mr. Weyland gathered his friends around him and held an opposition Home Rule meeting. WARRINGTON LIBERALS TO THE FORE.</p>
        <p>In the meantime the Liberal Association were hold- ing a very successful _meeting at Statham ; the Rev. W. Cooper and Mr. Wynne Jones, both of Warrington, being the spalcers. ihe misrepretentations of the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>MR. URE AT LYME</head>
        <p>MR. URE AT LYME</p>
        <p>A Large and Enthusiastic Demonstration.</p>
        <p>INSURANCE AND SOCIAL REFORM.</p>
        <p>Lymm was honoured on Tuesday evening with a visit from the. Lord Advocate, the Right Hon. A. Ure, K.C., M.P., and the Hon. E. S Montagu, M.P. (Under-Secretary for India), who spoke at a great meeting held in the Drill Hall, in support of the candidature of Mr. Kay-Shuttleworth. The demonstra- tion was one of the finest ever organised by the Liberals of the district and gave promise that on Wednesday next Lymm will cast its influence decisively on the side of the pres.nt progressive Administration. The hall was crowded and the small band of opponents who sought to disconcert the speakers by questions and interruptions discovered that their tactics were so readily checkmated by pointed replies that they soon abandoned their truculent attitude, and before the close of the meeting were only too anxious to "hide their diminished heads." The proceedings from beginning to end were characterised by a splendid spirit of enthusiasm and. the resolution of confidence in the Liberal candidate was carried almost unani- mously. The Lord Advocate has few, if any rivals, in the lucid and convincing exposition of political affairs, and his simple yet masterly handling of the Insurance Act created a great impression. His urbane yet satirical method of dealing with interrupters helped to maintain the good humour of the meeting. Once in reply to a man who called out" Liar," Mr. Ure said : "I welcome interruptions. Don't wait to the end of my speech. And you need not trouble about being polite to me. If you are intelligent, that is all I ask."</p>
        <p>Mr. and Mrs. Kay-Shuttleworth had a magnificent reception when they appeared on the platform. The candidate immediately addressed himself to the answering of a sheaf of questions which were handed up, and his fr,nk replies to the " posers" of the opposition were greatly to the liking of the meeting. Mr. G. L. Welford (President of the Lvmm Liberal Association) was in the chair, and, in addition to the speakers there were also on the platform :—Mr. J. D. B. Smith (chairman of the Lymm Liberal Association). Sir N. A. Haworth, Major and Mrs. England, Mr. S. Davies, Mr. J. Alfred Morris (President of the Altrincham Liberal Association), Mr. R. H. Gotobed, Mr. and Mrs. J. T. Clarke, Mr. and Mrs. T. Wyatt, Mr. and Mrs. J. Radcliffe, Mr and Mrs. H. Champ, Mr. J. H. Mather, Mr. W. Mercer, Mr. D. Daniels, Mrs. Dodds (President of the Lymm Women's Liberal Associa- tion), Miss Welford (chairman) and Miss Mclntyre (treasurer).</p>
        <p>"THE GREATEST GOOD FOR THE STATE." The Chairman sounded tt.e right note in his opening speech, when he said that such a great meeting augured well for a Liberal triumph at the electlon. He was exceedingly proud to be a member of the Liberal Party, which had done the greatest good for the State. Its record was magnificent, as toe most beneficent measures which had given the greatest amount of good to the greatest number had almost without question been passed by the Liberal Party, He therefore, confidently made his appeal, especially to the younger members of the audience, to identify themselves with that party, and become active workers and propagators of its doctrines in that neighbourhood. He was pleased to think that the traditions of Liberal Governments had been maintained by the Government of the present day. But, great as had been the measures which. Liberal Governments in past times- had teen able to pass, he doubted whether any of them had exceeded in value and in importance the measures which had been passed by the present Liberal Government, or that would equal those that had vet to he passed, if the Liberals were maintained in office. (Hear, hear, and applause.)</p>
        <p>WHAT TORIES WILL DO TEN YEARS HENCE.</p>
        <p>He regarded the National Insurance Act as one of the very greatest measures which had been placed on,. the Statute Book. It was, of curse, easy to find defects and small tnistakes,but he wanted the audience to look at the measure from a large point of view. He did not hesitate to predict that in ten years from now no candidate of any party would be willing to de what the Conservative candidate was now doing in regard to that Act. He did not seem to be very happy about that measure, and at his meeting at Statham failed to score what he had thought would be- a very good point with regard to one of the friendly societies. (Laughter.)</p>
        <p>m,At this stage the candidate arrived and was lcudly cheered.</p>
        <p>ANSWERING THE. HECKLERS. THE CONTROL OF THE LIQUOR TRADE,</p>
        <p>A large batch of questions was answered by the candidate. They ranged from the Budget to the liquor trade, and Welsh Disestablishment to com- pulsory training. Mr. Shuttleworth was asked whether he was in favour of abolishing the " tied-house " system. lie said : " I think the tied-house system has many grave drawbacks, and not the least of them is that it is to the interest of the publican to push the sale of alcoholic liquors." " Are you in favour of fixing licences on the barrel- age of clubs, grocers included? "—"I think (the answer was) that the fure•ler regulation both of clubs and grocers' licences will have ta be undertaken. "</p>
        <p>OPPOSITION TO COMPULSORY MILITARISM.</p>
        <p>"Are you in favour of all the young men of this country being compelled to undergo a course of military training? "—" I think there is a great deal to be said in favour of physical training, but I see no reason why military training should be brought in. I believe it to be absolutely unnecessary for this country, which is dependent for its first line of defence upon a supreme fleet, to add to that the cost of universal compulsory military service. " ULSTER'S ATTITUDE—" WE WON'T AND YOU</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>SHAN'T."</head>
        <p>SHAN'T."</p>
        <p>Mr. Kay-Shuttleworth devoted bis speech to the Home Rule Bill, which he contended would allay dis- affection in Ireland, and draw that race nearer to the British Empire. (applause.) He defended the retention of 42 Irish members at Westminster, on the ground that the Imperial Parliament would retain the supreme power to over-ride the local Parliament set up in Dublin, and that, as long as there \VA real union, there must be representation of every part of the United Kingdom. (Applause.) The grievance of Ulster was that she feared she would be persecuted by the Irish Parliament. The Govern-- ment had, however, put safeguards into the Bill and he understood that Mr. Asquith was willing to include any further d afeg ti a rds if the Ulster members would produce them. They, however, took up the attitude of" We won't and you shan't"—an attitude which no mitiority could maintain when a majority for Home Rule had been won, not only in Ireland—which, without an swing of the peadulum, had declared in its favoer at every election since the agricultural labourer got the vote—but in Great Britain. TORY ELECTIONEERING METHODS.</p>
        <p>A member of the audience interposed with the ques- tion: "What did Newmarket say about it ?-" New- market, replied Mr. Shuttleworth, is typical of the electioneering methods of the Conservative party, which have won for them a. number of by-elections.- They are not prepared to fight on their own programme or upon the whole record of the Government. They point out that they are ready to amend this Bill and that Bill—how, they refrain from saying.- I,Ve have put upon the statute-book measures of a far-reaching character like Old-age Pensions, Labour Exchanges and National Insurance;. and, look-</p>
        <p>ing broadly at them as a whole, the Conserva- tive party would not dare to stand up at a by-election and threaten to repeal any one of them. (Cheers). The Conservatives have to admit that amendment of the Insurance Act must be based on experience of the working of the Act. That is exactly the position of the Government. What votes we have lost at by-electipns have been lost because people have not looked at the work of the Government as a whole, but have been annoyed because the shoe pinches in some small particulars. "ADVENTUROUS ECONOMISTS."</p>
        <p>Finally, Mr. Kay-Shuttleworth referred to Tariff Reform, which he said would endanger not only the purity of our politics but the supremacy of our trade. He appealed to the electors not to lytnd over the con- stituency to one of those adventurous economists, who, reluctant though he might be to explain the difference between Tariff Reform and Protection, was neverthe- less one of those remarkable men who believed that the only way to make trade more prosperous was to tax it.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>MESSAGE FROM MR. WILLIAM LONG.</head>
        <p>MESSAGE FROM MR. WILLIAM LONG.</p>
        <p>The Chairman read the following telegram from Mr. William Long, one of the oldest supporters of the party in the division :—" Absence from Thelwall prevents my presence to-night to support our future member. Hope for enthusiastic meeting, William Long, Thelwall."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>MR. ORE'S GREAT SPEECH.</head>
        <p>MR. ORE'S GREAT SPEECH.</p>
        <p>Tory Candidate's Significant Omission.</p>
        <p>Mr. Ure, who was received with cheers, begAn his speech by emphasising an important omission from the Tory candidate's' election address. Mr. Hamilton had singled out with great skill six leadirg questions upon which he and Mr.liay-Shuttleworth differed, but,strange to say, he seemed to have omitted one que,tion upon which, as he understood,many hundreds ot votes turned in that division of Cheshire, and upon which many votes turned in the Newmarket Division. That question ass the National Insurance scheme, and Mr. Hamilton was the first Tory candidate he had known who had made that omission. He understood the Tory candi- date said it was a mere oversight, and that he meant now to gain as many votes as he could secure upon the unpopular features of the Insurance Act.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>"POLITICAL TREACHERY AND BASE BETRAYAL."</head>
        <p>"POLITICAL TREACHERY AND BASE BETRAYAL."</p>
        <p>Mr. Ure repeatedly laid stress upon the fact that,the Tory party in the House of Commons had acknowledged that the Insurance Act was far and away the greatest social reform ever laid before any civilised comatry— (cheers)—that such a scheme had been the drevm of their lives, and that they would scorn to take advantage of its unpopular features for party purposes. However, an unprecedently long series of by-elections followed, and the temptation to gain votes by exaggeraticia and misrepresentation, and by the telling of half- truths and ‘. hole untruths, was too great for them and they fell. Until that time, the capital example of political treachery, base betrayal, and the breaking of promises had been their conduct in relation to old age p.nsions, but their conduct in relation to Nation.il Insurance was ten times worse. Nothing like it had been known in the political history of this country. In spi,e of the warnings of the best men in their own party in the House of Commons, and of the best writers in the best of their own Prize's, the wirepullers, tb, intriguers and the party managers of the Tory party set themselves deliberately to write the most discreditable chapter in their party history. (Applause.) They turned their backs upon all their promises aria assur- ances in the House of Commons and fought every by-election, not upon Welsh Dises%blishruent, not upon "one man, one vote" and certainly not upon Tariff Reform—(laughter)—they fled from that like stricken deer—(laughter)—but they fought every by-electioo, the Newmarket Division included, upon a measure which they dare not oppose in the House of Commons, and which they acknowledged was the greatest social reform of the age. (Cheers.) With an ingenuity which was almost fiendish, and with a callous cruelty which had never beel excelled, they turned the whole of their batteries upon taut measure, and took advantage of its unpopular features in order to damage and, if possible, to destroy it altogether.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>PURPOSE OE' INSURANCE.</head>
        <p>THE PURPOSE OE' INSURANCE.</p>
        <p>Mr. Ure pointed out that constituencies which were not familiar with the idea of insurance got entangled in the details of the Act He, however, asked the electors to consider the measure in a broad aspect. Its purpose was to lighten and, where possible, to relieve people altogether of the burden lying on the shoulders of the wage-earning classes —the burden of evil incident to sickness, permanent physical breakdown, and lack of work and wages in those employments where periods of depression followed periods of prosperity with the dismal regularity that night followed day. A LONG STRIDE TOWARDS POOR LAW REFORM.</p>
        <p>The cardinal virtue, the conspicuous merit, the pith 'and marrow of the Insurance Act was that hence- forward the State would extend its helping hand to relieve the evils that fell upon decent, respectable, indepeudent poverty before those evils had ripened into the far worse evils of pauperism. (Cheers.) .It was the longest stride we had ever taken or were likely to take in the direction of Poor Law Reform. (Cheers.) Discussing the bearing of the great measures of the Government on the complex problem of the Poor Law, Mr. Ure said that a sum of ..4;12,600,000 was being expended this year in paying pensions to the aged poor, preventing a million such people ever rcquiring to cross the threshold of the Workhouse. (Cheers.) Altogether we were going to spend this year £25,000,000 on social reform for the benefit of the common people. Those who were now driven "by sickness, break- down or unemployment of the breadwinner to enter the Workhouse, would, by the Ist of January, 1915, have vanished from the pauper roll. (Cheers.) All this was being accomplished, though the people did not recognise it, by the beneficent operations of the National Insurance Act. (Cheers.) That measure would also enable them to establish the identification of the third section which went to swell the pauper roll—the class known as the outcasts, the flotsam and jetsam of society—and when that had been done, the Government would take steps to grapple effectively with that aspect of the problem. By solving the pro- blem of the Poor Law, Mr. Ure emphasised that he meant the smashing of the pauper roll without doing any injury to any man, A'ornan or child who now required its aid. He claimed that that would be the outcome of the recent legislation of the Government. ((;heers.) A FINE IDEAL. "The success of the National Insurance scheme, " declared Mr. Ure in an arresting passage, "depends not upon the efforts of a Lady Bountiful in one part of the country, or of some dtstinguished philanthropist in another part. It does not depend upon the beneficent work of a charitable association here or a benevolent society there. It depends for its success upon the whole community, linked together in a bond of human brotherhood and harnessed to work for the common weal"' (Loud cheers.) HOW THE MONEY MUST BE RAISED.</p>
        <p>Mr.- tire next turned attention to the source from which the £25,000,000 for social reform was to he raised. Would they get it from Tariff Reform ?(Loud laughter.) He saw he need not worry them about that. (A voice : "Pass on.") They would get enough out of Tariff Reform to pay for one Dreadnought and the wages of her crew, at the very best.- (Laughter.) But they wanted £25,000,000.- Now they would turn to the only source from which it would come. Every Tory candi- date returned to Parliament, whether he liked it or not, whether he thought it or not, was pledged to smash Mr. Lloyd George's great Budget, and put in its place Tariff Reform. "They never will," exclaimed one of the audience and Mr. -lire proceeded : " No, of course they won't, as long as we don't part company with our reason, and as long as we remain sane men and woman." (Cheers.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>SOCIAL WELFARE BUDGET.</head>
        <p>THE SOCIAL WELFARE BUDGET.</p>
        <p>The only means of raising the £25,000,000, continued Mr. Ure, was by the great Budget—what the greatest of our political economists called the social welfare Budget. It was the only Budget they would ever see. They might see taxes increased or a principle embedded in that Budget expanded, but it would be just the great Budget over again. In this country we had fabulous- wealth side by side with deep destitution— we had vast wealth unequally distributed. Hence when we were going to raise revenue, we must follow, rigidly the rule laid' down more than 130 years ago by a sagacious fellow-countryman of his, Dr. Adam Smith, who stated people should be taxed according to their ability to pay. (Hear, hear.) During the time he had been in Parliament the incomes assessed for income tax had risen from £678,000,000 to £1,100,000,000— an increase of £422,000,000. When they reflected that a penny on the income tax would produce close on £3,000.000 and that out of 422,000 adults who died rast year 355,000 left no property at all, they would see that, vast as our wealth was, the bulk of our people were not only poor, but very poor. The Tariff Reformer pro- posed to make Dr. Adam Smith's first rule of taxation stand upon its head and to tax men according to their inability to pay, instead of according to their ability. If they raised money by taxing the necessaries of life, the poorer a man was, the larger was the proportion he would contribute to the taxes of the country. "That," declared Mr. Use "is the reason why the youngest and healthiest man now present will never live to see the day when the great Budget is destroyed and Tariff Reform put in its place, and the party that stands for that is doomed to perpetual exclusion from office and power as long as we retain our senses." (aeers). THE GOVERNMENT AND THE MINIMUM WAGE.</p>
        <p>Mr.•Ure next touched upon housing and agricultural problems, and stated that he objected to the Tory Housing Bill because it would have meant subsidising the landowners. If we were going to regenerate the rural population and re-people the country-side, we would require to make country life more attractive and to offer people a fair promise of a decent living and career on the land. (Cheers.) " If the proper remunera- tion for the agricultural labourer cannot be got," said Mr. Ure, "as I hope it will be, by friendly arrange- ment between master and man, I am here to tell you that the House of Commons will be ready to face in his</p>
        <p>case, as it faced in the case of the miners—the most highly organised industry of the country—and in the case of the sweated trades—the least organised industries in the country—the question of the minimum wage. (Cheers.) The Tories will say I have teen trying to stir up class hatred and to set class against class. That is a very old, stale and dis- credited cry now. It is as old as the class of the Good Samaritan, who was the most flagrant offender. It was he who, in the opinion of the priest and the Levite, was the fir.t man to stir up class hatred. That is the cry that is always levelled by selfishness and callousness at every man who tries to lift up his voice or stretch forth his hand to help his poorer neighbours—those who are not so well off as himself." (Cheers).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>MR. MONTAGU AND LABOUR UNREST,</head>
        <p>MR. MONTAGU AND LABOUR UNREST,</p>
        <p>Mr. E. S. Montagu, M.P., gave a brief but incisive address, iu which he strongly took the Opposition to task for their electioneering tactics. If he were a Con- servative; profoundly believing in the great share in the destiny of our country which the Conservative parti, under Lord Salisbury and Lord Beaconsfield, had played, he would not be thankful or proui of the victory that was won at Newmarket. It was gained as the result of the meanest sort of electioneering campaign that ever a party waged, and that was why it was hardly respectable, at the present moment, Ito be a Conservative politician. Mr. Montagu dealt at length with the unscrupulous way in which the Tories had exploited the insurance and housing questions. Referring to the present unrest, Mr. Montagu attributed it largely to the spread of education, the consequence being that the inequality of opportunity and of distribution of riches was on its trial. Every diffi ulty under which men or women lived had to he explained away. Everybody was thinking ; everybody was ambitious. Which party offered them most under those circumstances—the party that had gone from step to step, through Workmen's Compensation, Old Age Pensions, and Child Protection to Insurance, or the party that could articulate no coherent policy, except the self-contradictory, impos- sible and preposterous contentious of Tariff Reform ? The beauty of the Liberal creed was that, with every Act passed they raised the standard of living and increased the demand, and therefore the mandate of the people, for something more. It was a live creed and nothing was so important as to clear the decks of historic controversies that had encumbered the time of Parliament and people too long and proceed with social reforms. (Cheers.) RESOLUTION OF CONFIDENCE.</p>
        <p>Mr. R. H. Grotobed, in moving the usual resolution of confidence in the Liberal candidate, said Mr. Hamilton was tho champion of compulsory military service and Tariff Reform and was not a fool—at least, so be told them and they must accept his word. (Laughter). Mr. Ray-Sbuttleworth advocated the con- tinuance of the voluntary military sytem, of which we were proud, he was a staunch Free Trader and a supporter of the Government. There ought, therefore, to be no hesitation as to the candidate whom they would support, and be believed the temper of that meeting augured well for the part which Lymm would play on Wednesday. (Cheers.) He was glad to have that opportunity or renewing, before that great meet- ing, his vows in the Liberal faith which he understood had been called into question in some quarters. lie had therefore pleasure in avowing once again his adherence to Liberalism and he intended to do all he could to secure the election of Mr. Kay-Shuttlewortb. (Cheers.) Mr. RADCLIFFE seconded the resolution.</p>
        <p>Sir A. A. Haworth, in supporting, challenged the Tories to declare themssives dead against any of the great social measures which the Liberal Government had passed. The resolution was carried almost unanimously and with great enthusiasm.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>TORY CANDIDATE HECKLED.</head>
        <p>TORY CANDIDATE HECKLED.</p>
        <p>LIVELY MEETING AT STATHAM.</p>
        <p>Mr. G. C. Hamilton, the Conservative candidate, had a lively experience on Saturday evening, when he addressed a meeting at Statham. The audience included a good sprinkling of opponents, who repeatedly made their presence felt by dissenting from the candidate's statements on the Insurance Act and asking awkward questions. Mr. Thomas F. W. Dixon was in the chair. Mr. Hamilton criticised the Insurance Act. He con- trasted the benefits which members of a local branch of Oddfellows, the Plough Boy Lodge, received with those they got under the :National Insurance Act, but this caused dispute. That lodge, he said, gave to the mem- bers certain benefits. They paid in li. a week and they got 10s. a week in times of sickness. " so," someone shouted.</p>
        <p>"his no use saying No,' "Mr. Hamilton rejoined. •"They never paid it," said the man. " I am talking facts," Mr. Hamilton replied.—" Well that (a fact) is a lie and a half." " Will you please take notice," Mr. Hamilton con- tinued, "that this gentleman with the red rosette tells me lam telling a lie? My friend says I am telling a voice : " No.") Will you shut up ?—(lnterrup- tion and voices: "He won't shut up " ; " You have no right to say that; the Chairman nits that right, not you.") The Chairman asked for order and for all questions to be kept to the end of the meeting. "Now, let me continue," Mr. Hamilton went on. "I am going to warn you if ay man tells me I am telling a lie, like my friend here has told me----"( Cries of "No.") The Chairman : I understood him to say is was a lie and a half. Another man rose and said he was the youngest member of the lodge referred to and that the sickness benefit was 10s. a week, as Mr. Hamilton had stated,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>"A GRAVE RISE."</head>
        <p>"A GRAVE RISE."</p>
        <p>"It is not a lie," Mr. Hamilton added. "My friend there has made a wrong accusation against me. I warn him that any man who tells me I am a liar runs a grave risk. (More interruption.) I have come to speak to the electors, and am not going to be interrupted and told Ihm telling lies by gentlemen with red badges. I refer them to their leader. Mr. Kay-Shuttleworth would disown them if he were in the room, and if a man in blue at a Liberal meeting acted towards him as you have done to me I would disown that man; I would not want his vote. (Cheers and counter-cheers.) Men with blue badges when they go to a Liberal meeting behave as gentlemen should do. This lodge, I say, used to pay 10s. a week benefit for 52 weeks. (A voice: "They used to pay 12s. and always did.") I shall ask the steward to put you out into the garden to grow. (Laughter.) I am paying for this room." (A voice: " You are paying for nothing.") "1 am paying for everything," Mr. Hamilton sail. " Not a soul is paying a cent except myself. ''</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>AN APPEAL FO a ORDER.</head>
        <p>AN APPEAL FO a ORDER.</p>
        <p>A gentleman who said he was the representative of the district in the Couucil appealed for order and a fair hearing. When Mr. Hamilton resumed he was again met with challenging queries, whereupon, singling out one man in the audience, he said, "If you cannot be quiet you go out." He went on to say that in the lodge the members used to get a death benefit. Under the Act they get none. Then when a man's first wife died he got £10; when his second wife died he got £5, and if fortunate enough to find a third lady to marry him and she died he got another £5, while if he died his widow received £lO to enable her to bury him "decently." Moreover, under the old state of things there was no compulsion. Now there was compulsion and less benefits. In principle the Act was good, but it wanted mending.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>INTERRUPTED BY SCHOOLBOYS.</head>
        <p>INTERRUPTED BY SCHOOLBOYS.</p>
        <p>When the Home Rule Bill was under consideration there was another but slighter storm. Mr. Hamilton challenged a " friend " to discuss with him at any time and anywhere "any blooming question he likes." To a younger man the candidate said, "'You hold your tongue. I warned you before. Are you a voter ? " The young man replied : "That is my policy, not yours." " Now if you are not a voter," Mr. liamiltou said, " and you speak again, I shall ask this gentleman to put you out. lam not going to be interrupted by schoolboys; lam going to speak to voters. If you are wing Liberals sent here on purpose-" Before the sentence could be completed a well-dressed man in another part of the room rose. " You have no proof," he said, that they have been sent here." " I have had proof at other meetings," the candidate replied. "You have no proof here," the man said. "Very well, I give in," said Mr. Hamilton, but this did not satisfy, for another man shouted, " It is a libellous statement." "If," said Mr. liaroilton, "it is a libellous statement, take your action. Sit down." "We understand the rules of debate as much as you," someone else called out. Then a woman said, "This is our meeting, not yours," whereas there was laughter and cheers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>QUESTIONS AND ANSWERS.</head>
        <p>QUESTIONS AND ANSWERS.</p>
        <p>In reply to questions, Mr. Hamilton said he was in favour of every young man between the age of 17 and 21 doing four months' compulsory military training— every man, duke's son and the agricultural labourer's son ; he made no distinction between them, and if he got Lis way they would work together and live together in the same tent.</p>
        <p>Mr. Hamilton was asked (as to the Insurance Act and societies) if there were any matoria or maternity benefits under the old order, and he owned there were not any. But voluntary and philanthropic effort was not less effective than Government effort in the matter of sanatoria treatment. As to Tariff Reform, 'le quoted Mr. Bonar Law's declaration that while benefiting the industrial workers a reform of tariffs would add to the prosperity of agriculturists. At the same time food was not to be taxed more than it is. By exacting a small toll from the foreigner the home market wculd be benefited from every point of view. "Do you mean that the buyer is going to pay and not the consumer ?" _</p>
        <p>"1 maintain this, that I am not a Protectionist. Protection means putting a tariff wall so high against the foreigner that he can't get over it. Tariff Itefotm means putting up a small charge for the use of this market."</p>
        <p>" ot cannot do it. The customer 'will have to pay." " Good-night," Mr. Hamilton said, and left tor his next meeting, at Thelwall.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <p>ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>Warrington and District Association.</p>
        <p>About 20 club representatives were present at ameet- ing of this association, held on Monday night at the Crown and Sceptre Hotel, Buttermarket-street. It was decided that the League be again formed, and the com- petition start next September. It was also decided to form two divisions, one to be an opeg-age competition and a division with a limit age of 20 or 21. Several other important matte-s came up for discussion, but. owing to the senior clubs not being fully represented, it was decided to adjourn the meeting till the 26th, when the election of cfficers and discussion of rules will take place.</p>
        <p>. The committee intimated that the Dodd Challenge Shield will be given for the competition, and two sets of medals to both divisions if sufficient clubs come forward and enroll their membership. Mr. R. G. Brandreth is acting secretary (pro. tern.)</p>
        <p>GOLF.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON CLUB.</p>
        <p>The monthly bogey competition toor:place on Satur- day, when Mr. C. 1-1. Ford qualified in the first division with one down to bogey, thus winning the memento_ Mr. A. Bottomley qualified in the second division with 8 down. Below are the other scores : FIRST DIVISION. _ .</p>
        <p>E. L. Robinson 5 down J. E. Birtles 5 .” T. B. Handley . )1, F. Pendlebury fl • B. H. Tilston 7 ,f J. M. Murray 8 ,f Captain B. Evans lO 3: SFCOND DIVISION.</p>
        <p>A. Warburton 9 F. A. Frost E. A. White MERSEY CLUB. CROSFIELD PRIZE.</p>
        <p>The tbird qualifying round for the Crosfield prize was played on Saturday, and resulted in a win for Mr F. W. Gilbert with a net score of 89. MERSEY LADIES' CLUB.</p>
        <p>The second qualifying round for the Mersey ladies prize was played lest Tnursday, and resulted in a wirr for Miss Pownall with a net score of 83.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON HARRIERS IN INTER-CLUB RACE_</p>
        <p>At the Bollington Cricket Club sports on Saturday, an event of much interest was On two miles inter-club race, for which many cli%mpion athletes represented some noted organisations. The result was a victory for the Birchdale Harriers (National champions), who- repeated their last year's win with 10 points ; Salford Harriers, second, 24 points; Warrington A.C. (Northern champions) third, 26 points. The first man home was H. D. Baldwin (Derby and County /..C.). who won easily from R. Stanton and S. C. Greenway (Birchfield), then followed W. Scott (Salford), T. L. Lee (Birclifield), and L. Proctor (Salford). Time, 9min. 51sec.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>ON THE WING.</head>
        <p>ON THE WING.</p>
        <p>SANKEY BRIDGES SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>The Bath old birds' race was flown on May 17th. Twenty-four members sent 237 birds, which were liberated at 11.50. Wind West. Result (subject to correction) : Members. Velocity. Churm, P.A.V Summei field, P 952'6 • Tindall Bros., P 9487 Boyle and Skellamd. Deakin Sammerfield Churru 936'9 Harrison and Bergrss 9362 Tindall Bros. Deakin Wilcox and Donogue Woolley Bros. Ashton 923 5 Johnson Longmore Barlow and Perkins 911.3 Allen Wilson Keenan Bros 894 Wheatley BB4-1 Marsh and Joyner 8839 Williams and Mather Roughsedge Bracegirdle B4BB Randalls Welsby and Fisher Prince</p>
        <p>A wins special Ws. 61. presented by Mr. Valance. V wins Nomination N'aEe.</p>
        <p>P w.ns the penny pool. WEST LANCASHIRE SATURDAY FEDERATION. Second old bird race flown from Bath on Saturday. Liberated by Convoyer Sidney Taylor at 11.50 a.m. Wind, west ; weather, clear and hot. 1,763 birds com- peted. Official result :</p>
        <p>Velocity - H. Moston, Lymm F.O T. Newton, Lymm F.C. H. Moston, Lyrum F.C. J. Johnson, Hor;tley H.S. Stringfellow Bros., Preston Brook H.S 978 Taylor and Wright, Cadishead H.q Humphries and Son, Latchford H.S 973 Hooper and Son, Warrington District H.S 972 I'. Newton, Lymm KC 971 T. Mason, Warrington District H.S Jas.'Lowe, Lowton District H 968•7 Lowe and Lowe, Lowton and District H.S 968'5</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>ANGLING NOTES. -- Warrington Workingmen's Angling Association (N.A. Centre). THE RAMBLING MATCH.</head>
        <p>ANGLING NOTES. -- Warrington Workingmen's Angling Association (N.A. Centre). THE RAMBLING MATCH.</p>
        <p>Although the weather was not as desired on Sunday-, the journey to Chester was enjoyed by a large con- tingent. A start was made from the Bay Horse about 8.30 a.m., calling at the Gladstone Arms to pick up the Centre members. The party reached Chester at noon and sat down to an excellent dinner provided by Mr. G. Walker, himself a Northern angler. The return from Chester to the Gowy is only a few minutes run by char-a-bane and members were soon under control of Messrs. Stout and Potter, who started them off after explaining the conditions of the match, so that thee would be no mistake at weighing-in time. Angling was off as far as the match was concerned, the w:nner only weighing soz. 10drrns. One trout only was taken during the three hours' fishing, but it was good enough to secure second place. The return was com- menced at C. 30 p.m. and the anglers reached to Warring- ton headquarters about 8.30, everyone expressing satis- faction with the outing and with the way the arrange- ments were carried out by the Secretary. Members may rest assured it will not be the last rambling match for the Northern Anglers Warrington. WEIGHTS.-1, IL A. Cla-e, 53z. 103rms. ; 2, T. Aldcroft, soz. 6drms. ; 3, F. Jolley, soz. 2drms ; 4, C. Ashton, 40z., 7drins. ; 5, T. Edwards, 4oz. sdrms ; 6, T. Dixon, 4oz. 4drurs..; 7, A. Stout, 2oz. 12drms ; 8, T. Armitage, 2oz. &amp;arms. ; ,9, T. Singleton, 2oz. Gdrms. Nine members weighed in by 5.15 p.m. and nine prizes were given. Several members came up to weigh after tinie,but were disqualified before.weighing in the match catches. Mr. II Brittein weighed in a beautiful trout, turning the scale at 111). loz. gdrms.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>NORTHERN ANGLERS PRIVILEGES.</head>
        <p>NORTHERN ANGLERS PRIVILEGES.</p>
        <p>The sign boards are now in position informing anglers of the parts of the stream rented by the Northern Anglers, but for the benefit of those who follow these notes in the" Examiner "we will give them informa- tion here.</p>
        <p>The Northern Anglers' Association have acquired the exclusive right of fiShing on the river Gowy, so far as the Earl of Shrewsbury's estate extends, and members are free to fish on production of the Northern Anglers' travelling card, as long as they do not trespass or do damage to the property of the tenants or farmers. The privilege extends :—Down stream from Chester- road bridge at Mickle Trafford to the sign board, then two meadows at the Big Withens and two meadows at the Shaker Bridge. Up steam front Chester-road bridge, one side of the Black Brook to the London and North-Western Hallway. Both sides of Barrow Brook up to sign board, then one side up to the pontoon. The main stream up to the mill pod and the mill stream up to the church and on to Little Barrow, with the exception of four fields where sign boards are erected. A .S.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>INDIGESTION</head>
        <p>INDIGESTION</p>
        <p>is the Bane of Existence</p>
        <p>Kimoc Tonic</p>
        <p>the unfailing Vegetable Remedy is guaranteed to remove all stomach and liver troubles. Your money returned if it fails. All Kimoe Remedies are sold at 1 -, 2- and 4/-. They are obtainable from all chemists and from the pro- prietors. Healy and Bigelow, 27, Colquitt Street, Liverpool.</p>
      </div>
