        <p>JERSEY.</p>
        <p>"LOVELY GARDEN OF THE SEA."</p>
        <p>Unrivalled Bathing. Lovely Drives. Three 18 hole Golf Links. Bowling. Open Air Concerts. Special Services of Steamers.</p>
        <p>EXCURSIONS TO FRANCE, ETC. Send id. stamp for 93 page Illustrated Guide. DEPT. S., COMMERCIAL ASSOCIATION, JI4.:R,SEy</p>
        <p>DEATH OF THE REV, H. R. WILD.</p>
        <p>TWENTY YEARS IN CHARGE OF ST. LUKE'S CHURCH.</p>
        <p>A painful sensation was created in Warrington by the news of the death, which occurred suddenly on Wednesday morning, of the Rev. H. R. Wild since 1893 curate-in-charge of St. Luke's church. For some time Mr. Wild had been in indifferent health, but nothing serious was anticipated, and he conducted the services at St. Luke's Church on Sunday last. The</p>
        <p>following day he went to stay with his brother at Dinting, near Glossop, in the hope of recuperating, and it was found he was suffering from gastritis. He grew worse and on Wednesday had a heart attack which proved fatal. Mr. Wild had had charge of St. Luke's Church since its erection in 1893, and had done a quiet but much appreciated work in a populous, working-class district. His genial and kiadly disposition gained for him a host of friends. In later years his favourite recrea- tion was golf, of which he was an enthusiastic and skilful exponent. He was identified with the Mersey Club, which has its course at Penketh.—The funeral took place yesterday at Dinting.</p>
        <p>ARROL-JOHNSTON, BAYARD. PALLADIUM and TALBOT CARS.—SoIe Agent, J. S. GARLAND, 51, 53, and 55 Bridge et., Warrington. See the 8 II?. Bayard, £197.</p>
        <p>9'4;144 ts„ ' .cfP' ";'• 4,:f; 4.4ptt, . • • , rl4l:t N/1834119 , u. ;e2&amp;AIV ) &lt; , .•</p>
