      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>MR. EDWARD BECK'S WILL.</head>
        <p>MR. EDWARD BECK'S WILL.</p>
        <p>Substantial Local Bequests from a big</p>
        <p>Estate.</p>
        <p>£2,800 FOR INFIRMARY.</p>
        <p>• As Was announced in the " Examiner" a fortnight ago, the Warrington Infirmary benefits to a substantial extent by the will of the late Mr. Edward Beck (79), of Heddon House, Isleworth, Middlesex, formerly of Warrington, where he was a founder of the Dallam Forge Company. Limited, afterwards amalgamated with the Warrington Wire Company and Messrs. Pearson and Knowles as the Pearson and Knowles Coal and Iron Company, Limited. Mr. Beck left estate valued at 5.:1(12,252 and it is estimated that the duties payable will amount to 432,000.</p>
        <p>After making certain legacies dealt with below the testator left the residue of his property for division in seventy-four parts and his solicitors, in communicating the fact that four of these shares had been left to the Warrington Infirmary, said they were expected to be of the value of £7OO each. Thus the Infirmary benefits to the extent of £2,800.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>OTHER LOCAL BENEFICIAIRES.</head>
        <p>OTHER LOCAL BENEFICIAIRES.</p>
        <p>Two of these parts of £7OO Mr. Beck left to Mr. Walter Milner, secretary of the Whitecross Co. Ltd., three parts to Miss Ellen. Milner, the Limes. Lymro. and two parts to Mrs. Davies (nee Todhunter), the Parsonage, Penylan, Ruabon. "To Thomas Atherton, who for the greater part of his life has been associated with me in the service of the Pearson and Knowles Coal and Iron Co., Ltd.," be left £5OO. FOR THE TECHNICAL SCHOOL AND MUSEUM.</p>
        <p>To the Warrington Corporation for the Warrington Technical College [lnstitute?] testator bequeathed all his books on chemistry, metalluigy, engineering, electricity or other technical subjects; and to the Warrington Corporation for the Warrington Art Gallery the picture of ships by Sheffield and the six photographic views of Rome and Venice in his library.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>GENERAL BEQUESTS</head>
        <p>GENERAL BEQUESTS</p>
        <p>Other bequests included : £B,OOO to University Uollege Hospital, London, subject to the life interest of his brother, John Lister Beck.</p>
        <p>..-05,000 each to the Governesses' Benevolent Institu- tion, subject to the life interest of his sister, Ellen Beck ; and to the United Kingdom Beneficent Insti- tution, subject to the life interest of his sister, Edith Beck.</p>
        <p>To the superintendent for the time being of Nazareth I House, Isleworth, all his wearing apparel. Four years wages to his servant, Nellie Coornber. The following bequests to employes if still in his service or having left for reasons other than miscon- duct "in recompense for good and faithful service " —" A life annuity of F-400, a legacy of £2OO, his grey talking parrot, and the marble clock in his library to his housekeeper, Elizabeth Done ; a life annuity of £52 and a legacy of £26 to his servant, Annie Davison; life annuities of £26 and legacies of £l3 aach to his gardener (George Cotterill), his under- gardener (Walker), aud his labourers (May and Pritchard) ; £lO absolutely and £3OO upon trust for each of his servants, Edith Shepherd and Mary Smith.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>TO MAKE SURE OF.HIS DEATH.</head>
        <p>TO MAKE SURE OF.HIS DEATH.</p>
        <p>He directed that a surgeon should make sure of his death by such means as his trustees should think</p>
        <p>proper.</p>
        <p>TO THE ELECTORS OF THE ALTRINCHAM DIVISION.</p>
        <p>The electors of the Altrincham division will, we doubt not, be shrewd enough in I view of the contest in which they will be engaged next Wednesday, to look at the record of the Liberal Government during the seven years it has been in office. It is easy to generalise and say to the elector that Codlin and not Short is his friend, but in politics happily the elector can be his own judge. Fortunately he may here recall the wretched state to which the Conservatives, when they were in power, brought the affairs of this country—a state which they tried to hid e away by introducing a vague and high-sounding cri for " varying the basis of taxation," which is commonly known as Tariff "Reform." So bad was that record that the electorate, while it was fresh in their minds, nearly I annihilated the party that was led by Mr, BALFouß,with the doubtful assistance oF Mr. CHAMBERLAIN. What has followed? In seven short years the Liberals have settled South Africa and appeased India on lines which have extended the bounds of freedom and equality in an unprecedented manner. Labour troubles on a large and unknown scale have been wisely handled and Old Age Pensions and National Insurance have been placed at the disposal of a people who will not be slow to recognise their value and the worth of the party who first made them a living reality in this country. The Lloyd George Budget, which made these reforms and reforms yet to come possible, was of a magnitude, politically, only to be rivalled by the stern treatment meted out to a House of Lords that dared to persevere in its time-honoured hostility to the will of the people. The greater part of £100,000,000 has been struck off the National Debt, besides the removal of taxes on the necessaries of life amounting to 71/2 millions a year. Trade has " boomed " almost continuously, notwithstanding all the calls that have been made upon the resources of the country for the betterment of the conditions of those who are mostly responsible for the creation of the wealth of the country. Can the Opposition show any- thing like such a record—or a prospect of such a record, if, unfortunately, they are favoured once again with the reins of office?</p>
        <p>So much for the actual past. What shall we say of the future? In the first place we are told that Tariff "Reform" is not an issue at this election, but we would ask the thoughtful elector to reflect upon the fflords of Mr. F. E. SMITH, who in a recent speech at Cricklewood said :—" Our policy is a perfectly clear one, and on its protective side—on that side which is concerned with a general tariff on foreign manufactured goods—you may take it from me that whatever and whenever we return to power the first Budget which we pass into law will introduce that principle." The Conservatives are making a great bid for agricultural support by 'making a move in regard to a minimum wage for lab- ourers. Agricultural labourers will remember that Mr. CHAMBERLAIN and his party talked of Old Age Pensions and went to war instead. The same party are now talking of rural wages but they will, if they get the chance, embark upon a protective policy—the Colonies permitting—that cannot do other than impoverish the country and swamp all other considerations. We appeal to the electors in the Altrincham Division to .weigh well the relative claims of the men who are seeking their support. If they do so and eliminate all the extraneous blandishments they will assuredly vote for Kay- Shuttleworth.</p>
        <p>DAISYVACIIUM CLEANERS,42/-,63/-,B4b—Try one on hire.—J. S. GARLAND, 51 to 55, Bridge tit. Warrington</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
