      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, May 24, 1913.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, May 24, 1913.</p>
        <p>The Passing Week.</p>
        <p>THE ROYAL VISIT.</p>
        <p>There has been an alteration in the arrangements for the Royal visit, as far as the part which the school children shall take is concerned. It was originally intended that the scholars should be assembled on stands outside Arpley Station where their Majesties will detrain and enter the carriage in which they will drive through the town. This week, however, it has been decided that the children shall be massed on the lawn in front of the Town Hall where they will be able to witness the presentation of prominent townspeople to their Majesties. A Royal pavilion is to be erected and here the cere- monial will take place. Tenders have been invited for a scheme of declaration of the route which their Majesties will traverse. For this purpose the route will be divided into two sections—Arpley Station to Milner-street; Milner-street to Sankey Bridges.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>" STORES " MEETING.</head>
        <p>THE " STORES " MEETING.</p>
        <p>As coming events cast their shadows I before, so do past events often leave their trail. The short and sharp conflict which the management of the " Co-op " and a section of their employes had last week is to have a sequel next week. The Committee of Management have called a special meeting of members to be held to " receive the report of the committee regarding the recent dispute" and, by requisition signed by 99 persons, "to consider the present position of the society's employes in regard to their action in the present dispute." There is here, we think, the promise of a full evening's entertainment for those members who are in a position to be at the Co-operative Hall on Monday.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>CALL FOR TERRITORIALS.</head>
        <p>THE CALL FOR TERRITORIALS.</p>
        <p>With a view to making the path of the ex-Territorial somewhat smoother, they are notified that a certain number of them may re-enlist for one, two or three years instead of for a full period. As locally the Territorials are in a healthy aud growing condition, this concession on the part of the authorities ought to result in bringing the battalion up to full strength. The men go in training with the South Lancashire Brigade on the 17th of August atiDenbigh.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>SIR LUKE FILDES.</head>
        <p>SIR LUKE FILDES.</p>
        <p>Warringtonians will regret to hear that Sir Luke Fildes, R.A., is so ill that his con- dition is causing his relatives and friends much alarm. The London correspondent of the "Yorkshire Post," writing on Monday, says : Much anxiety is felt in Royal Academy circles respecting the issue of the grave illness of Sir Luke Fil-.es, R.A.. Sir Luke, who has contributed to this year's Academy, undertook his share of the work of hanging the pictures last month. He caught a severe chill just then, and an attack of influenza was followed by pneumonia. The crisis of the disease was passed ten days ago, but the patient is still extremely weak, and by no means out of danger. Lady Fildes is a sister of Mr. Henry Woods, R.A.. who spends most of the year painting in Italy. Mr. Woods returned to London for the opening of the Royal Academy, and is remaining here for the time being in great concern for his brother-in-law.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>MR. BECK'S PUBLIC BEQUESTS.</head>
        <p>MR. BECK'S PUBLIC BEQUESTS.</p>
        <p>Under the will of the late Mr. Edward Beck the Warrington Infirmary will benefit to the extent of nearly £3,000, the Technical Institute by the acquisition of a number of technical books and the Museum Art Gallery by the acquisition of a George Sheffield picture. There are other local beneficiairies under the will, but it is to the public bequests that we wish to draw special attention. Mr. Beck was evidently one of those who believe in doing a final act of justice to a community among whom they amass their wealth. His close connection with large works in which the danger to the life and limbs of the employes is not a slight consideration induced him to bear in mind the claims of the Infirmary, and he left a share of his residuary estate to that institution which will, it is expected, benefit to the extent of £2,800. This will go to the credit of the endowment fund and produce a sub- stantial yearly income by way of interest. The Warrington Infirmary is badly off for endowments, or many of the numerous appeals that are made for funds would not be necessary. It is to be hoped that the late Mr. Beck's thoughtfulness will be largely imitated,and that the Warrington Infirmary by these means may be relieved of some of the anxiety that makes the task of main- taining efficiency a very hard one indeed. Mr. Beck also remembered the Technical School, whose library on his special side of technical education will be materially enlarged ; and the Museum Art Gallery will be enriched by the addition of one of those pictures of ships that George Sheffield delighted to draw and the town is equally ' delighted to possess.</p>
        <p>LIGHT AND LEADING FROM WESTERN AUSTRALIA.</p>
        <p>"An old Warringtonian," writing with "thoughts of the Good Old Town" fixed in his mind, gives our readers some first-hand information relating to the conditions in Western Australia. First 6f all, he speaks with pride of his former home, and takes delight in recounting how, at an ironmonger's shop "down under," the man who served him had to acknowledge not only that he kept Warrington files but that " Stubs's" were the best files that could be had. But our observant correspondent gets at close grips with our politics, and says that his Colonial friends want to know what benefit they are likely to get by a preferential tariff unless foreign wheat is taxed. It is one of those conundrums that admit of no answer. The farmers at home are asking what benefit they are "likely to get" if food stuffs are not " reformed " as to tariff while other goods are. He sometimes reflects upon the remote- ness of the " benefit " if the Colonies are left free to compete against him. Townspeople are asking "what benefit they are likely to get" if wheat is taxed against the foreigner to allow the home farmer to put up his prices —as he would be entitled to do if Colonial competition would allow him to do so—if his manufactured ploughs cost him more. Mr. Bonar Law says, and . candidates like Mr. Hamilton in the Altrincham division continue to repeat, that while benefiting the industrial workers a reform of the tariffs would add to the prosperity of agriculturists. We are not told how that may be, and the various interests will continue to ask what benefit they are likely to get. In the mean.- time, it will be noted, the progressive forces in Western Australia are in arms against Protection. The Liberals are pledged to Free Trade and the Labour Party are making war upon the trusts, those inevitable and ugly growths arising from tariffs whose evil presence alone makes them, possible. "No Tariff 'reform, no trusts " is the one safe motto for an Englishman, whether in town or country.</p>
        <p>PREMIER MOTOR CYOLES.—SOIe Agent, J. S. GARLAND, 51 to 55, Bridge at., Warrington.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>NEW FREEDOM.</head>
        <p>THE NEW FREEDOM.</p>
        <p>President Wilson's Effort to restore pre-boss and pre-trust conditions.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>WHEN CORRUPTION THRIVES.</head>
        <p>WHEN CORRUPTION THRIVES.</p>
        <p>To THE EDITOR OF THE " EXAMINER. "</p>
        <p>Sir,—Dr. Woodrow Wilson, President of the United States, has published a book entitled" The New Freedom," which consists of the more suggestive portions of his campaign speeches as they were steno- graphically reported. It is, therefore, an authoritative and valuable record of what President Wilson has underlaken to do or attempt to do. "The New Freedom" seems scarcely an appropriate title since it is a oatriotic effort to restore and revive the old freedom which existed before the days of 'Political bosses and corrupt corporations. As there is a good deal in this book which applies to the industrial conditions in this i.ountry as, well as those in America. I hope you will permit me to quote some of the most important paragraphs. Speaking of the great change which has taken place in recent years with regard to the relations of employer and employe, President Wilson says :—" The employer is now generally a corporation or a huge company of some kind; the employe is one of hundreds or of thou- sands brought together not by individual masters whom they know and with whom they have personal relations." "There is something very new and very big and very complex about these new relations of capital and labour." "A new economic society has sprung up and we must effect a new set of adjustments." "We must not pit power against weakness." "The employer is gener- ally in our day, as I have said, not an indi- vidual but a powerful group, and yet the workingman,when dealing with his employer, is still under our existing law an individual." This, President Wilson argues, is why we have a Labour question, and the only rational conclusion to be drawn from his reasoning is that employes are clearly entitled to the support either of trade unions or of better laws. About the heartless and unscrupulous way in which trusts and trading corporations deal with competition President Wilson says :--7" .AmQrica is not a I place of which it can be said, as it used to be, that a man may choose his own calling and pursue it just as far as his abilities enable him to pursue it, because to-day if he enters certain fields there are organisations which will use means against him that will prevent his building up a business which they do not want to have built up, organisations that will see to it that the ground is cut from under him, and the markets shut against him. For if he begins to sell to certain retail traders, to any retail traders, the monopoly will refuse to sell to those dealers and those dealers will be afraid, and will not buy the new man's wares."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>People should take Counsel together.</head>
        <p>People should take Counsel together.</p>
        <p>About Parliament and the people and the importance of more opportunities for full and free discussion being given, President Wilson says a great deal,but the following may fairly indicate his groove of thought : —" For a long time there has been no sufficient opportunity of counsel among the people, no place and method of talk, of exchange of opinion, of parley. Communities have outgrown the folk moot and the town meeting." It is because citizens have foregone the taking of counsel together that the unholy alliances of of bosses and big businesses have been able to assume to govern for us." I conceive it to be one of the needs of the hour to restore the processes of common counsel and to substi- tute them for the processes of private arrange- ment which now determines the policies of cities, states and nation. We must learn, we freemen, to meet as our fathers did, some- how, somewhere for consultation. There are schoolhouses all over the land which are not used by the teachers and children in the summer months, which are not used in the winter time in the evening for school pur- poses. These buildings belong to the public. Why not insist everywhere that they be used as places of discussion like the old town meetings to which everybody went and where every public officer was freely called to account and made to bear criticism to the uttermost? "</p>
        <p>Light on Public Matters. In a chapter headed "Let there be Light," Dr. Wilson resolutely opposes and condemns every kind of secrecy and private arrange- ment in reference to public matters. He says, "a first necessity is to open the doors and let in the light on all 'affairs which the people have a right to know about." They have been too secret, too complicated, too round-about; they have consisted too much of private conferences and secret understand- ings. "If there is nothing to conceal then why conceal it? If it is a public game why play it in private? The very fact that so much in politics is done in the dark, behind closed doors promotes suspicion. Everybody knows that corruption thrives in secret places and avoids public places." I intended to refer to President Wilson's attitude on the Tariff, which is very important, but I must deal with it in another letter, as this is already too long. In the meantime may I suggest to all who are anxious about progress and the general welfare that the foregoing views of so distinguished a statesman as President Wilson deserve almost as much considera- tion in this country as they do in America, since we have much the same evils to contend with. Whether it is called the new freedom or the old freedom, it is being ruthlessly taken from the people. Combinations and monopo- lies are increasing every day with the object of keeping prices up and preventing fair competition. Local Government is little better than a farce, and the public good is one of the last things considered. Our laws do not prevent the strong from crushing the weak, and should therefore be altered with- out delay.—Yours &amp;c.,</p>
        <p>W. CHRIMES. 679, Knutsford-road, Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>FURTHER BEQUESTS TO LOCAL CHARITIES.</head>
        <p>FURTHER BEQUESTS TO LOCAL CHARITIES.</p>
        <p>By the will of Miss E. J. Massy, Greenbank, Latchford, which has just been proved, £lOO each is left to the following :—The Warrington Infirmary and Dispensary, the National Society for the Prevention of Cruelty to Children, Church of England Waifs' and Strays' Society, and the Institution at Warrington for the relief of widows and orphans of clergy of the diocesp. The estate in this case was valued at £40,370.</p>
        <p>WHAT IS A MUSICAL INSTRUMENT? The question is suggested by the report of one of the Commissioners in Lunacy who recently visited the Warrington workhouse. Referring to the recreation provided for patients of unsound mind, he somewhat naively remarks: "There is a gramaphone, but no musical instrument." Hard on the gramaphone But perhaps the records were such as to make "talking machine" a more fitting description.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>~ . , .. 41111</head>
        <p>~ . , .. 41111</p>
