      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>Presentation to Dr. Hibbert.</head>
        <p>Presentation to Dr. Hibbert.</p>
        <p>Dr. J. Cocte Hibbert, who is leaving Warrington to take up the position of Medical Officer of Health at Blackburn, was entertained at a complimentary dinner and presented with a silver rose bowl at the Town Hall, on Tuesday evening, by the staff of the Health and Hospital departments of the Corporation. Lancashire Battaliois in India.</p>
        <p>Changes of station for infantry battalions now quartered in India have been arranged as follows :- Ist South Lancashire, from Subatnu to Quetta ; the King's (Liverpool), from Lahore to Peshawar ; the King's Own (Royal Lancasters), from Lebong to Lucknow ; Ist Lancashire Fusiliers, from Multan to Karachi ; 2nd North Lancashire, from Pootia to Bangalore ; Ist York and Lancaster, from Katachi to Jul) bulpore.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>Warrington Grocers at Bowls.</head>
        <p>Warrington Grocers at Bowls.</p>
        <p>On Thursday the members of the Warrington and District Grocers and Provision Dealers' Association had a delightful drive to Acton Bridge, and enjoyed some good bowling at the Railway Hotel. The winners of the Association's brize-i were Messrs. W. Monks, J. Blinstou and Edward Monks, in the order named, the President (Mr. W. H. Owen) adding his prize to the winner. In the second main Messrs. J. Rigby, J. H. Gittens and Milbourne were the prize-Winners.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>Crest ield's Band at New Brighton.</head>
        <p>Crest ield's Band at New Brighton.</p>
        <p>The fifteenth annual brass band contest— t ,ok place in the New Brighton Tower grounds on Saturday, when there were twenty-four entries. Of these no fewer than twenty-one bands competed for the prizes, amounting in the aggregate to :U25, together with the 50-guinea Tower Challenge Cup. The test piece was a selection from Balfe's " Bohemian Girl," in the treat- ment of which the various combinations secured the approbation of the judge, Mr. J. 0. Shepherd, who reworked that the playing throughout had been remarkably fine. Be placed the successful bands in the following order of merit :—l, Foden's Motor Works; 2, Hebden Bridge; 3, Crosfield's ; 4, Wingate's Temperance; 5, Black Dyke Mills ; and Irwell Springs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>Wycliffe Recipe and Quotation Book. _ . _ . _</head>
        <p>Wycliffe Recipe and Quotation Book. _ . _ . _</p>
        <p>A Recipe and Quotation 800 being a souvenir of an effort 'lade bv Wycliffd Sunday school workers for the sale of work tiiis week cn behalf of the new school- chapel, Elmwood Avenue, Padgate-lane, has been issued. Councillor Hawthorn, who has edited the brochure, is to be congratulated upon the appearance, contents and palpable success of the book. The recipes, with which he has been favoured by a numerous and varied body of amateur expert cols, cover the whole field of the art of preparing food with remarkable completeness, while the quotations are exceedingly good and cleverly arranged. As there have been only a limited number of the book printed and the demand for it at the sale of work will have been brisk those who obtain a copy may account themselves favoured by fortune. The hook has been printed by Messrs. John Walker and Co., Limited, "Examiner "Office.</p>
        <p>Funeral of the Late Dr. Willett. The funeral of the late Dr. James Willett, of Stockton House, Stockton Heath, the oldest medical practitioner in the district, took place'at St. Thomas's Churchyard, Stockton Heath, on Monday afternoon. The clergy and choir met the cortege at the church gate, the Gfficiating clergy being the Hon. and Rev. C. F. Cross, the Revs. H. V. Pigot, James Collins and E. W. Clarke. In the church the choir sang the hymn " Now the labourer's task is o'er." The chief mourners were Mr. and Mrs. A. J. Willett, Mr. C. W. Kemp, Mts. Kemp, Miss Edith Willett, Miss Mary Willett, Mr. Wm. G. Willett, Miss Gussie Miss Annie Willett, Miss Edgerley, Miss Elizabeth Willett, Mr. and Mrs. Hignett, Mr. F. Kemp, Mrs. Rotherir, Mrs. Gathergood, Dr. Binns, Dr. Hodgkinson, Dr. Peacocke ; Drs. Burrows, Bowden and Murray (representing the British Medical Association), Mr. I'. M. West, Mr. C. Jackson Holmes, Mr. H. J. Longton, Mr. J. Cooper, Mr. Hatton, Mr. J. Whitley, and others. Mr. Dean, Higher Whitley, sent his carriage. Many beautiful floral tributes were received from relatives and friends. The funeral arrangements were carried out by Mr. H. F. Greenhough, of Warrington. National Childrens' Home.</p>
        <p>A house-to-house collection on behalf of the National Children's Hume, Bonner-road, London, N.E., has been arranged to be made in Warrington during the week ending May 31st. The Home was established 43 years ago by Dr. T. Bowman Stephenson, and from a small house in South London it has grown to be one of the largest child-saving institutions in the country. Its primary purpose is the saving and training of destitute children, but in its various branches the orphan, crippled and afflicted are also received without distinction of creed or locality. A well-equipped sanatorium for children threatened with consumption is the latest development of this good work, and excellent results have followed this commendable enterprise. The Home has branches in Lancashire and Cheshire. Nearly 10,000 have passed through the Home since its commencement, 95 per cent. of whom have done well. Mr. W. Healey, 9, Worsley-street, Warringtun, is the local honorary secretary for the collection, and he will be pleased to give information as to the work, and receive offers of assistance from friends willing to help. We understand that nearly 800 children have been received from Lancashire.</p>
        <p>vulgarity. A whistling turn of exceptionally good quality is supplied by Arthur Slater, who is extremely popular in his "Poet and Peasant " selection, as well as mimicing various British songsters. As a whistling coster he is also very successful. Chiko, comedy acrobat, is a elever contortionist with a good turn of dry humour. Chiko is a clever hand jumper and table performer. Eglen and Percival score a success in a bright vocal comedy sparklet "Called for the Rent." The Four Kemptons are capital step dancers, both collectively and individually. Frank Sylvo gives a comedy juggling turn quite above the ordinary, his tricks being very clever and smart. Lilian Wesson is a vocalist of good quality, described as Erin's Daughter of Song."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>NEXT WEEK'S PROGRAMME.</head>
        <p>NEXT WEEK'S PROGRAMME.</p>
        <p>The programme for next v,eek will include : Keeley bros. in "Fun in athletic store " ; Grouch and Welch, that lively pair ; Haydees, miniature vaude- ville theatre ; Arthur Royd, tenor vocalist ; Wright, Aitken and Co., in cJinedy sketch " A bit o' bother " ; Raby Maybutt, dancer ; The Rawsons, entertainers ; and Peter and Potts, comedians.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>THEATRE ROYAL.</head>
        <p>THEATRE ROYAL.</p>
        <p>"A Slave of Sin."</p>
        <p>The fascinating drama" The Slave of Sin" has been presented with distinct success during the week at the popular Scotland-road house. Mr. Morton is to be congratulated upon getting together such an excellent company of artistes and he deserves the support of all Warrington theatre-goers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>NEXT WEEK.</head>
        <p>NEXT WEEK.</p>
        <p>The company will play next week in the great drama of intense human interest " The way of the world." The cast will be :—Matthew Maylon, Pro- prietor of the Circus. Mr. Leonard Yorke ; Captain Fred Slade, Mr. Charles A. Chandler ; Cecil Harding, Mr. Patrick Branigan ; Joseph Sparrow, Mr. Arthur Len Frere ; tir John Harding, Mr. Sidney Ellis; Horatio Bluff, Mr. George Perry ; Kate Montague, Miss Phyllis Morton ; Polly Bell, Miss Cissie Cleveland ; Little Eric Malyon, Miss Nellie Masterson, and Lady Adela Harding, Miss Hazel Head.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>PIGTUREDROME.</head>
        <p>THE PIGTUREDROME.</p>
        <p>During the whole of next week there will be shown a thrilling detective drama entitled "Shadows of the night." This is a fine story of a journalist and a detective who !bring to justice a gang of notorious criminals. With the exception of the above splendid picture, the programme will be changed as usual on Thursday.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>ROYAL VISIT. REQUEST FROM THE COTTAGE HOMES CHILDREN.</head>
        <p>THE ROYAL VISIT. REQUEST FROM THE COTTAGE HOMES CHILDREN.</p>
        <p>It was reported to the Warrington Board of Guardians on Wednesday that at a meeting of the Padgate Cottage Homes Committee, the Clerk was authorised to write the Town Clerk applying for pro- vision to be made for the children of the Cott ige Homes, with the ordinary school children, to view their Majesties the King and Queen on their visit to Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, May 24, 1913.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, May 24, 1913.</p>
        <p>MR. W. G. C. GLADSTONE, M.P. TO VISIT LYMM,</p>
        <p>Mr. W. G. C. Gladstone, M.P., will be the principal speaker at a great demonstration to be held in the Drill Hall, Lymm, on Monday evening in support of the candidature of the Hon. L. U. Kay-Shuttleworth.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>NEW STEAMER LAUNCHED</head>
        <p>NEW STEAMER LAUNCHED</p>
        <p>Meeting the Growing Requirements of Warrington Traders.</p>
        <p>KEEPING THE RAILWAY COMPANIES IN BOUNDS.</p>
        <p>- On Wednesday morning, at Garston, the launch took place of the " Evona," the new steamer of the Bishop's Wharf Carrying Co., which will be engaged in the Warrington and Liverpool trade of the company. The steamer has been built by Messrs. Grayson and Co., and a large assembly witnessed the event. The Lady Mayoress of Liverpool (Mrs. Harmood Banner) performed the naming and launching ceremonies, the first being accompanied by the drenching of the bow with a bottle of wine, and the second by the severing of a rope, which the Lady Mayoress accomplished in a very business- like way with an axe. For the advant9ge of the uninitiated the modern method of launching a steamer may be described.</p>
        <p>The hull is built upon a large framework of timber, which in its turn rests upon fixed timbers which slope to the water. Between these two pieces of framework a thick coat- ing of tallow is placed. When the time comes for launching the props are removed and the vessel and upper framework slide into the water. The rope, which is cut in the ceremony,releases a weight, whibh,as it falls, knocks away the last prop and leaves the ship free to slide as quickly as it likes along its tallowy bed. The' Evona " passed smoothly and grace- fully into the water, disentangling herself from the timbers, wedges and ropes that strewed the surface.</p>
        <p>The representative members of the assembly adjourned to the reception room of the firm to express good wishes for the future of the vessel and the company.</p>
        <p>There were present : The Lady Mayoress, Mr. and Mrs. W. P. Rylands, Mr. S. Rigby Armitage, Mr. H. P. Mortimer, Alderman H. Bloor, Mr. E. Tyson, Mr. Harold Forwood, Mr. C. S. Sellar, Mr. C. J. Hughes, Mr. J. J. Bleckly, Mr. Geo. F. Cullen, Mr. W. Neale, Mr. F. Fletcher Hunt, Mr. E. Jones (manager of Bishop's Wharf Co.), Mrs. Jones, Mr. Esplen, designer of the vessel, and Mr. ' A. Mounfield.</p>
        <p>Mr. Ambrose Grayson gave the • toast " Success to the Evona," and Mr. W. P. Rylands responded. Mr. S. Rigby Armitage gave the health of "The Lady Mayoress," and expressed the appreciation that was felt of her kindness in performing the ceremony. Mr. H. P. Mortimer expressed appreciation of the designer ; and Mr. J. J. Bleckly gave the health of Mr. W. P. Rylands, the chair- man of the company. 'they were, he said, in the throes of a struggle with the railway companies, and though small carrying con- cerns such as theirs might not seem important, yet it would perhaps be by means of them that the railway companies would be kept in bounds. Their. future might therefore be important. Mr. Rylands, in responding, said the Com- pany was meeting a substantial want. They did not quite know where they were being led, but wherever the interests of Warring- , ton traders pointed they would go.</p>
        <p>Presents of silver souvenir card cases suit- ably inscribed were made by Mr. A. Grayson to the Lady Mayoress and Mrs. Rylands. Mr. Burton proposed the last toast, "The health of the Manager, Mr. Edward Jones," paying high tribute to his character and business ability. The "Evona" measures 90 feet in length by 20 feet, and carries 200 tons upon a draught of 7 feet 6 inches. We ought not to omit to say that she is built specially for Warrington traffic. A double thickness of plating in the hull will protect from friction upon the river bottom; and several other special protections and adaptations have been made. She has compound engines of the Contraflo surface condenser pattern, developing an indicated horse-power of 265, her engines having been built by the Vauxhall and West Hydraulic Engineering Company, Luton, and the pro- pellor is on the Eaton's patent adjustable blades system. The name " Evona " was much admired.</p>
        <p>It is becoming difficult to fix upon a poetic name for a vessel registered at a large port, seeing that no two vessels of a port can have the same name, but in fixing upon " Evona " the directors have made a felicitous choice. Perhaps we shall not be giving away a secret_ if we explain that it is an invented name. A worthy gentleman, who is blessed with five daughters,composed it out of the initial letters of their names—Evelyn, Violet, Olive, and so forth.</p>
        <p>Warringtonians who are interested in the development of the town will be ready to give a welcome to the " Evona " when she makes her advent at the wharf. This event is expected in the course of three weeks.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>ALEXANDRA DAY,</head>
        <p>ALEXANDRA DAY,</p>
        <p>Arrangements for the Local Celebration.</p>
        <p>Warrington will keep Alexandra Day on June 2Sth, when the coming of Queen Alexandra to this country will be commemorated by a national effort to assist our hospitals by the sale of artificial flowers in the streets of our large centres. Many Warrington ladies have agreed to devote the day to selling artificial wild roses, made by the girls at Mr. John Groom's Crippleag,e, and the profits will be devoted to the funds of . the Warrington Infirmary. The following ladies have made themselves respon- sible for the arrangements in the various wards and districts :</p>
        <p>Bewsey Ward: Mrs. J. H. Smethurst and Mrs Simpson. Fairfield Ward.: Mrs. Willis.</p>
        <p>Howley Ward: Mrs. John Evans and Mrs. J. S. Hill. Latchford Ward: Mrs. Broadbent, Miss Marjorie' Fairclough, Mrs. C. Jackson Holmes, Mrs. T. Locker and Mrs. F. Short. Orford Ward: Mrs. Halliwell and Mrs. Harold Jolley. St. John's Ward: Mrs. Berry and Mrs. Powell. Town Hall Ward: Mrs. Firth Lindley and Mrs. Parsonage. St. Austin's (No. 1) : Mrs. Ed. Fox and Mrs. E. Osborne; (No. 2) : Mrs. J. C. Coppock and Miss Booth _ -</p>
        <p>Whitecross Ward: Mrs. P. Delaney and Miss Houghton. rappenhall : Mrs. F. Aylmer Frost an d Mrs. Pigot. Stockton Heath: Mrs. Lyon, Mrs. H. Roberts and Mrs. Fletcher (Stretton). Penketh and Sankey: Mrs. Murray and Miss Jones. Walton : Mrs. Ralph Johnson. The arrangements were discussed at a meeting, largely attended by ladies, which was held at the Infirmary on Thursday afternoon. Mr. J. H. Smethurst (chairman of the Infirmary Board of Man- i agTemheenste) pereretasided. rial work is being undertaken by Mrs. Henry Greenall.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
