      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>Photograhphs .by Burrell.</head>
        <p>Photograhphs .by Burrell.</p>
        <p>A DRY FOUNTAIN NEAR CROSFI ELD'S WORKS.</p>
        <p>Few persons may have noticed ,but it is nevertheless a fact, that the drinking fountains vi hich were once so prominent a feattue along the main roads leading through the borough,are rapidly disappearing. Within the recollection of comparatively young people the means of refreshment at these wayside places have diminished almost to vanishing point. Not so long ago the disappearance of the Old Bridewell took with it a drinking fountain that was much nsed, and the departing Old Bridge carries away yet another. The Park wall in Sankey-street contained a fountain before it was demolished. One has disappeared from the neighbourhood of the Blne Coat School. These very useful public conveniences were largely, if not altogether, the giis of private individuals to the public, and the way in which they appear to have been quietly and gradually "scrapped" wculd be the subject of a useful inquiry..</p>
        <p>Only three fountains have survived in our main thoroughfares, one at the Cemetery Gates, one at Trinity Church and one on the wall of Messrs. Crosfield's Works in Liverpool-road, the last one being dry and useless. On the one at the Cemetery Gates is inscribed the following :</p>
        <p>The Gift of Peter Rylands, Mayor, 155:3-4.</p>
        <p>FOUNTAIN AT TRINITY CHURCH.</p>
        <p>111)re elaborate is the message on the one at Trinity Church. ft runs:</p>
        <p>This fountain was presented by Benjamin Pierp')int Mayor of Warrington in the year 1849. Erected 1860. Our Lord Jesus Christ said whosoever drinketh of this water shalt thirst again, but whosoever drtriketh of the water that I shall give him bhall never thirst. The Liverpool-road fountain was Presented by J. B. Edelkten, Esq.. last borough reeve of Warrington. Erected 1861.</p>
        <p>It will be noted that the main road leading from east to west has the two efiective fountains while the road, from north to south has none. There is no horse trough nearer than Win w ick though, now the approaches to the town have been widened, there appears to be no reason against our placing a few in convenient situations.</p>
        <p>Much coHld be written in praise of water and the need for placing it within the reach of those in need of it--a need which was more clesrly recognised by a former generation—but perhaps we have said sufficient to cause our city fathers to set about a scheme by which a great public service could be rendered at a expeuse.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>FIFTY YEARS MARRIED.</head>
        <p>FIFTY YEARS MARRIED.</p>
        <p>BANK QUAY MISSION WORKER.</p>
        <p>Photograph by</p>
        <p>CAPTAIN AND</p>
        <p>To complete a term of 50 years of inarried life is not a very common experienc e and Mr. and Mrs. N.</p>
        <p>Hough, of Liverpool-road, were the recipients of many congratulations from a large, circle of friends, on Sunday, when they celebrated their golden wedding. They were married on the ISth May, 1803, at S. John the Baptist Church,Park-road,Toxteth,Liverpool. Coming to Warrington in 1804, Mr., or Captain Hough, as he is better known, was engaged by Messrs. J. Crosfield and Sons, Ltd., as captain ef one of their vessels trading between Live'pool. and Warrington. Despite a slight discontinuation of.service during this long period, Captain Hough has held the confidence of his employers for fully 40 years, and to-day he is honoured by being in command of the general dock traffic at Liverpool, carried on by the great soap firm. He is the oldest captain on the Mersey, above Runcorn. During his niutical career he has been instrumental in the saving of 14 lives. Some 42 years ago he associated himself with the mission work of the late Mr. John Cr afield, which resulted in the formation of what is to-day one of the</p>
        <p>CAPTAIN AND MRS. N. HOUGH CELEBRATE THEIR 'want ; GOLDEN WEDDING.</p>
        <p>Parkinson.</p>
