      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>GOLD COAST CONCESSIONS. A 'WARRINGTON COMPANY AND THE</head>
        <p>GOLD COAST CONCESSIONS. A 'WARRINGTON COMPANY AND THE</p>
        <p>COLONIAL OFFICE.</p>
        <p>Correspondence relating to the form of lease of oil palm lands in the Gold Coast Colony, which has passed between Apol Ltd., Warrington (of which Mr. J. J. Crosfield is chairman), and the Colonial Office was issued in the form of a Parliamentary White Paper on Monday. From this it appears that the company obtained concessions to which the Colonial Secretary took exception pending certain amendments. On the 23rd of November last Mr. Crosfield wrote to the Colonial Secretary enclosing proposals to bring the concessions within the Concessions Ordinance, 1900, and to meet the objections entertained by the Secre- tary of State. On the 2nd of December a letter from the Colonial Office stated that Mr. Harcourt approved the amended form of lease subject to the insertion of a provision securing the rights of the natives to the use of water for their domestic requirements (hunting, farming, &amp;c., were granted them in the lease), and</p>
        <p>stating that be would not raise any obstacle to the validation of such leases by the local authorities in accordance with the existing laws and regula- tions on the subject, including section 20 (2) of the Concessions Ordinance, under which the aggregate area of concessions held by one person relating to products of the soil is limited to forty square miles. The Governor of the Gold Coast would be instructed not to grant concessions under the pro.. posed Palm Oil Ordinance over any area in respect of which a lease for collecting palm oil bad been obtained and validated or was awaiting validation. In their reply, on December 18th, Apol Ltd., noted the provision re water to which they were quite agree- able, and noted with satisfaction the instructions sent to the Governor of the Gold Coast. Tra RAW MATERIALS FOR WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Interviewed by an "Examiner" representative, Mr. J. J. Crosfield said that thii Apol Co. had been formed to develop the supply oi raw materials in West Africa, for the purposes of the manufactures of Messrs. Joseph Crosfield and Sons, Ltd. The Gold Coast concessions bad been obtained in pursuance of that object.</p>
        <p>PREMIER CYCLES. Excelsior Cycles Lea Francis J Cycles, £1 10/-to £l6 16/-.—Sole Agent, ._S. GARLAND 51 to 55, Bridge st., Warrington.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>MRS. HOUGH.</head>
        <p>MRS. HOUGH.</p>
        <p>most flourishing causes in Warrington, the Working- men's Mission ,Bank Quay. He has held several offices in connection with the mission, and for the past 20 years has occupied the honoured position of president. A few months ago he was made the recipient of an illumin- ated address, an arm chair, a large dining table, and a clock, gifts which represented the expressions of esteem in which he is held by his fellow-worshippers at the Bank Quay Mission. His work at the mission has brought him into constant association with Mrs John Crosfield, of Colwy n Bay, and he has been of great assistance to her in the formation of many bene- ficent schemes, the outcome of which have been of material value in the growth of the cause at the Mission Chapel. A notable feature of Captain laugh's seafaring career, surrounded by its varying associations, is that he owes a large amount of his success to the fact that he is a life-long total abstainer and non-smoker. During their 50 years of happy married life, Captain and Mrs. Hough have been blessed with 14 children, 9 sons and 5 daughters, 11 of whom are now living.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>WARRINGTONIANS IN AUSTRALIA,</head>
        <p>WARRINGTONIANS IN AUSTRALIA,</p>
        <p>Our Manufactures at the Antipodes : "The best that can be had."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>COLONIALS AND PREFERENCE.</head>
        <p>COLONIALS AND PREFERENCE.</p>
        <p>An Effort to Control the Trusts that Tariffs</p>
        <p>Protect.</p>
        <p>We have received the following letter from "An Old Warringtonian " now in Claremont, West Australia:—</p>
        <p>Sir,—l am writing with thoughts of the good old town of Warrington fixed in my mind. I have the " Exaininer " sent out every week and I can assure you it is read with a great deal of interest and then passed on to other friends from Warrington out here. The troubles. or, shall I say, the comic sketches of the Town Council amuse them very much, but apart from this they.know, and I know, Warrington is going ahead. I was pleased to know the King was going to honour the town with a visit. There are various things in Australia which recall to me the town of Warrington. Working the other day, I had to use some fly wire used in making fly doors, and looking at the label on the bundle, I found it came from Greening's ; and then, again, going to the ironmonger's shop to buy saw files, I asked for Stubs', not hoping to get them, but the salesman said he was often asked for Stubs', and they were the best files that could be had. Crosfield's specialities also are to be had, but much dearer than at home. Talking to the Colonials on British politics, they want to know ; What benefit are we to get by a preferential tariff unless foreign 'heat is taxed? the chief exports from this State being gold, wheat and fruit. .The Liberal party in Western kustralia are pledged to a Free Trade policy, and the Labour Party, who are in power at present, a-e asking for powers at the Federal elections, which take .place in May, so that they may control the trusts which have sprung up, owing to the tariffs put un to protect them. There is an article in to-day's " West Austialia," the leading paper in this State, on "The American Tariff and .Australia," which I am sure would interest your readers, and give them some idea of the benefit to be had with a Free Trade policy. So I enclose the article in this letter, hoping you will find room for it in your valuable paper, as well as this letter from April IG, 1913</p>
        <p>AN OLD WARRINGTONIAN.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>AMERICAN TARIFF AND AUSTRALIA.</head>
        <p>THE AMERICAN TARIFF AND AUSTRALIA.</p>
        <p>The following is the article to which our c3rre- spondent refers : The Tariff 8111 at present under consideration by the United States Congress will, if carried, not only effect a fiscal revolution in the great Trans-Atlantic Republic but also prove a mighty engine of destruction against, extreme Protectionist systems the world over. No , wonder that the great trusts and manufacturing coin- bines of America are up in arms against the contem- plated wholesale reduction of duties, or that they are flocking to Washington in the hope of lobbying the Bill into the limbo of unaccomplished reforms. TOLL-TAKING CRAFT IN DANGER.</p>
        <p>Their craft of excessive toll-taking is in sore danger. The Washington correspondent of the " Daily Chronicle" predicts that the new tariff will save the consumers £175,000,000 annually, and that the loss of revenue will be £20,000,000, while the balance of £155,000,000 represents for, the most part the profits now accruing to tariff-fed industrial monopolies ! Hence these tears. The present occupant of the White House and the democratic majorities in both Houses of the United States Congress owe their election to a great revulsion of feeling against high tariffism. Of this there is not, and cannot be, nay manner of doubt. They have an unequivocal mandate from the people to seek in tariff reform not exactly a remedy for the high and rising cost of living, which has many contributory causes, but a certain palliative for that distressing national ailment. As the Tariff Bill stands it is a hold step towards Free Trade, and its effect cannot fail to open an enormous import market to British goods, ;ncluding Australian and New Zealand wool and meat, Canadian timbers, Irish potatoes, and English manu- factures.</p>
        <p>BORN IN WAR; NURTURED IN CORRUPTION. The present American protective system, undoubtedly the worst the world has ev-r seen, was born in war, and has been nurtured in corruption. It began when import duties were imposed to offset internal revenue taxes levied for aar purposes in 1861. These import duties yielded incidental protection, and, as Mr. Byron W. Holt, President of the Tariff Reform Committee, of the Reform Club, New York, puts it, "This whetted theappetite for more and more tariff favours." It was ever thus. The more protection that is granted the more protection is demanded. It grows with what it feeds on. Extravagant protection has flourished in the United States because the Republican party which so long monopolised the government of the country was liberally financed by, and so bound to, the great trusts and combinations whose huge financial interests were being built up through the agency of high duties. ENORMOUS GAIN TO AUSTRALIA.</p>
        <p>For Australians the United States Tariff Bill possesses a peculiar interest at the present juncture of Commonwealth affairs. All the States of Australia are eagerly searching the world for new markets for their primary produce. Steadily the Continental countries of Europe are being pressed by the working classes to remove the fiscal and other barriers that now block the way to the free admission of Australian meat, Australian wool, and Australian fruit. But Govern- ments in Europe are not so responsive to public opinion and public sentiment as they are in America, and we may have to wait indefinitely before the tariff walls in European countries are broken down or even appreci- ably lowered. Hitherto what was true of Germany and other Continental countries was true of the United States, but since 1907, the year of the great financial crisis in America, protection in its old extreme form has been seriously threatened and the victory in November last of the Democratic party sealed its doom. In the matter of wool alone tbe gain likely to accrue to Australia will be enormous. The woollen duties as they now stand vary from 2d. to Is. per lb. These duties have practically prohibited the entry of Australian wool, except of the finer sorts, to the American markets, and both the Americans and the Australians have needlessly suffered in consequence. But all this is to be changed. The duties are either to go altogether or will be so substantially reduced as to make trade both possible and profitable to the benefit alike of America, Australia, and the United Kingdom.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>BENEFICIAL TARIFF REFORM..</head>
        <p>A BENEFICIAL TARIFF REFORM..</p>
        <p>Such a tariff reform will help the sheep-breeding industry in the Commonwealth; it will enable the poorer citizens of the United States to be better clothed ; and it will stimulate the cloth-weaving business both in England and in America. Similar beneficent effects will. flow from like amendments of the United States tariff in other directions. Especially is good calculated to spring from the new tariff as it affects trade with Canada. Indeed, a dis- interested outsider finds it hard to understand how two neighbouring peoples, having so much in common have so long reconciled the war they have been waging inside their own borders against monopolies and restricted Icompetition with their submission to the monopolies and restricted competition which are imposed on them by the tariff wall they have, between them, built up along their own frontiers. Just as difficult is it to understand why if free trade between all the States of the United States is a good thing, and free trade between all the provinces of Canada is a good- thing, why it should not be considered an equally good thing, and even a better thing, to have free trade throughout the whole of North America. That some men do not think so is part of their esoteric faith, tbe inner mysteries of which do not lend themselves to rational exposition.</p>
      </div>
