      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>WHERE DOCTORS FAILED TO HELP</head>
        <p>WHERE DOCTORS FAILED TO HELP</p>
        <p>Lydia E. Pinkham's Vegetable Compound Restored Health to Two Women. Their Own Statements.</p>
        <p>Yotir medicine</p>
        <p>tas done me more good than all the doctor's medicines. 0 f ten I had to stay in bed four days because of hemorrhages, and my back was so weak could hardly walk. have been taking ,yclia E. , Pinkham's egetable Compound nd now I can stay 1p and do my work. I think it is the best</p>
        <p>medicine there is for women." (Name .and address on request.)</p>
        <p>Surgical Operation Avoided.</p>
        <p>"I can say that Lydia E. Pinkham's Vegetable Compound has done me more than anything else. One doctor said I must be operated upon for a serious -female trouble' and that nothing could help me but a surgical operation. "I had hemorrhages and at times could not get any medicine to stop them. I got In such a weak condition that I would have died if I had not got relief soon.</p>
        <p>'Several women who had taken your Compound, told me to try it, and I did and found it to be the right medicine to build up the system and overcome female troubles.</p>
        <p>" I am now in great deal better health than I ever expected to be, so I think I ought to thank you for it." (Name and .address on request.) Write to-day for a free private book (to women only) to the Lady Manager, the Lydia E. Pinkham Medicine Co., 42a .Hampstead Road, London, N.W. For ,2s. 9d. she will send you a bottle of the ,Lydia E. Pinkham Vegetable Compound, or for 4s. 6d. a much larger bottle. Any chemist will supply it at these prices.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>OFF TO CANADA.</head>
        <p>OFF TO CANADA.</p>
        <p>Co-operative Employes' Send-off to Mr. Eastham.</p>
        <p>THE RECENT DISPUTE.</p>
        <p>Mr. E. Eastham,who has for five and a half years held the position of bead grocery warehouseman to the V\ arrington Co-operative Society, sailed for Vancouver yesterday. Mr. Eastham, who served his apprentice- ship with a private grocer, was formerly a branch manager for six years under the Wigan Co-operative Society. At a social gathering held in the Winmarleigh Cafe, on Tuesday evening, under the auspices of the local branch of the A.U.C.E.(of which he had been president), Mr. Eastham received the following presents as tokens of respect and the high esteem in which he was held by his fellow-workmen and friends :—Purse of gold and silver medal from the employes ; set of books from the members of the Trades Council; silver cigarette case and silver cigar holder from Mr. G. Tryner, the licensee • of the Royal Oak Branch Hotel,Knutsford-road ; and a gift of money from an old friend, Mr. Hickman. About 80 members of the Amalgamated Union of Co-operative Employes and friends sat down to a sub- stantial supper, after which the presentatiens were made. Reference to the recent dispute,which threatened to develop into a strike, was made by the chairman (Mr. H. Billiugham) and other speakers. A letter was read from Mr. Hallsworth, assistant secretary of the Union, in which he stated that it had •been his intention to be present, but Mr. Lumley and he hid had to go to Lincoln where there was every possibility of a strike taking place. Mr. Taylor, of Flixton, a member of the North-Western District 'Council of the A.U.C.E., would no doubt speak in his ,place. " STILL GRIT IN CO-OPERATIVE EMPLOYES.' Mr. TAYLOR, who was warmly received, said be was in at the beginning of the recent fight for principle in Warrington and he could rejoice with them in the -victory they had gained. The Warrington branch of the A.U.C.E. was making history in the trade union world, for it was the first branch in the N.W. district to hand in strike notices. That was the third threatened strike within the last seven weeks. The eyes of all the employes in the land had been turned on Warring- ton to see if they bad bad any backbone in them. The employes of Warrington fought shoulder to shoulder or Weir principles, and they had shown all:the country that there was still grit in the Co-operative employes. They had been twitted by people who said that the .A.U.C.E. was a very good Friendly Society but lacking in backbone. The trend of the ,last few weeks had shown them that there was that grit in the, Union to help them to go forward in the trade union world and show them to be a real live trade union. They were pulling together in the interests of trade unionism and co-operation. (Applause). Calling upon Mr. Morris to mane the presentation on behalf of the local branch of the A.U.C.E.thelCifaritaiaN said they would rather part with the sittiug merubor for Warrington than Mr. Eastham. (Applause). Mr. MORRIS said they were losing a mien-needed stalwart in their branch. .Mr. TRYNER, in making his presentation, said he was one of Mr. Eastbam's late fellow-workers. He person- Ally, was losing one of his best friends. (Applause.) Speaking on behalf of the Trades and Labour Council i Mr. SMITH said he hoped those who had not done so would join a trade union at once. If a man was not a trade unionist and expected to reap the benefits accru- ing from trade union effort, he was not true to his class. There was a marriage taking place now between the Co-operative movement and the Trade Union movement and he had pleasure in making that presenta- tion to one who was a thorough Trade Unionist and a Co-operator. (Applause.) Mr. HICKMAN said he made his small presentation as fe'lowtrade unionist to a man who had been a true friEnd. Mr. EASTHAII, in reply said he marvelled at the ,growth of the union from its very simple origin. After referring to the novel motto " button up," he said Warrington seemed to be seething with revolution at the present time. He hoped they would all go forward, working as they had done in the past for the principles • which they held so dear. (Applause.) Messrs. HAMBLETON, MANN and others present ,expressed regret at Mr. Eastham's departure and all joined heartily in singing "For he's a jolly good fellow." Members of the Co-operative Glee Party, gave pleasing numbers. Mr. Hardman was well received for his cleverly executed concertino solo, and Mr. H. Sutton gave an impressive recital of " Devil May Care." Mr. A. E. Trow, who delighted with his piano- forte solos, also acted as accompanist.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>SANITAS</head>
        <p>SANITAS</p>
        <p>The 15th annual general meeting of the " Sanitas " Company, Limited, was held at their Limehouse (London) factory on the 21st inst., Mr. C. T. Kingzett, F.1.C., the chairman, presiding. The usual dividend of 7} per cent., which has been paid for many years, was again declared, £2,000 being placed to reserve, £1,200 to contingency account and £2,993 17s. 11d. carried forward. In the course of his observations the chairman emphasised the value of " Sanitas " Fluid as a first aid in the dressing and treatment of wounds and sores, and its importance as a mouth and throat germicide for preventing oral sepsis, in which respect it takes the highest rank of all available antiseptics. He also pointed out how useful " Sanitas" Fluid is for purifying the air, and said that, apart from the disinfection of dwelling and sick rooms, it</p>
        <p>could be employed with great advantage for spraying the air of churches, theatres, railway carriages and motor omnibuses. " Sanitas " Fluid restores the necessary modicum of vital oxygen to the air and prevents the infections which, there is no doubt, largely result from crowding in such spaces in more or less constant use and without any attempt being made to make them sanitary in character.</p>
        <p>ASTRIKING EXAMPLE of genuine value is afforded by the fact that Steedman's Powders have had a steadily increasing sale for nearly 100 years, and still stand unrivalled as a safe medicine for children. These powders contain no voisbn, Observe the EE in Steedman.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>Trttrrs to tilt (Mita</head>
        <p>Trttrrs to tilt (Mita</p>
        <p>rim EDITOR of the " Examiner " invites correspond- ence on all subjects of publi?, interest. LETTERS should be brief and pointed. Their object must be to impart or seek information, not abuse. COMMUNICATIONS received early in the week—except urgent matters—can always be given the best attention.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>MUSIC IN THE PARK.</head>
        <p>MUSIC IN THE PARK.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Whilst appreciating the splendid service rendered by our local bands in providing music for the people during the summer months, I think it is a matter for regret that such doubtful taste is exhibited by those responsible for the Sunday programmes. It would, of course, be difficult for any authority to define the class of music admissible for Sunday performance; at the same time, the line ought certainly to be drawn at (the often vulgar) comic song, comic opera and their like. Mnsic of a more refined and artistic character would. I believe, be more interesting to the instru- mentalist in study, and would certainly be better appreciated by the musical public. MODERATO.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0150" n="art0150">
        <head>LIVING WAGE IN THE CHURCHES.</head>
        <p>LIVING WAGE IN THE CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>Sir,—l wish to say a few words in regard to the poor pay that our curates get, and also the Noncon- iormist ministers. I know personally myself of one curate that hardly gets a living wage. It is no wonder that there is a scarcity of young men for the ministry when they have to pay so much for their education and suffer a loss of health in many cases. If some of the wealthy people we. have in this Christian land of ours would endow the churches more freely, it would be better for the welfare of the work- ing people, both spiritually and morally. If the ministers of all sects could have more time to visit the homes of the poor, we would have less crime. We all should know, or learn if we don't know, that good old Scripture test, " Bear ye one another's burdens and so fulfil the law of Christ." Let us all practise it and make for the betterment of this earthly life. PRO DEO ET REGE.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0151" n="art0151">
        <head>"RAG-TIME AND RUBBISH::</head>
        <p>"RAG-TIME AND RUBBISH::</p>
        <p>- - Sir,—Allow me to say a word or two re Mr. Down's remarks in his speech at the annual meetipg of the Bands Association. He said, skating rinks, picture palaces, S:c., were detrimental to music. Well, I am a musician myself, but I am speaking from public feeling, apart from my own theatre. If he paid a visit to one of them he would, more often than not, bear the best compositions of some of our great masters, and I know that the well-respected patrons do not go chiefly to see the pictures, as they would not enjoy that so much if the music was not worth listen- ing to. Of course, I grant Rag-time music is intro- duced, but Mr. Downs must acknowledge that is not, rubbish according to his own words re" syncopation.' In the picture hall he will find one of the best varied programmes being performed, if, the management have: an instinct for music. I trust Mr. Downs will not use the expression "Rag-time and Rubbish" on future occasions, as he will understand, under the present conditions of musical competition," the restaurant," and the "picture hail" are the only places for an instrumentalist possessing any abilities whatever. LE DOMINOR.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0152" n="art0152">
        <head>VIVISECTION.</head>
        <p>VIVISECTION.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Dr. Manson has adopted the Research Defence Society's usual device of quoting Lawson Tait as a believer in vivisection because he once stated that the practice had benefited bacteriology. The fact is that Lawson Tait said this in the early days of bacteriology, but six years later he wrote : " These experiments never have succeeded and never can, and they have, as in the cases of Koch, Lister and Pasteur, done little but cover our professinn with ridicule." This does not show in the least that his opinions were " wayward and inconsistent," but merely that, like other anti-vivisection medical men, he was at first strongly inclined to believe in the claims made by members of his profession until he examined them, after which he was honest enough to throw them over. Dr. Manson next alludes to Dr. George Wilson as i he were an anti-vivisectionist, whereas the value off Dr. Wilson's opinions consists in the fact that he holds them in spite of his refusal to join " the small medical group opposed to vivisection." That these two meciical men, one a surgeon of world-wide reputa- tion and the other a medical officer of health of great experience, should be dismissed with something very like contempt by Dr. Manson, is on a par with all vivisectionist argument. It is the invariable practice of members of the Research Defence Society to treat people as eminent when they happen to favour vivi- section, and to dismiss them as nonentities if they hold contrary views. BEATRICE E. KIDD. Secretary British Union for Abolition of Vivisection, 32, Charing Cross, S.W. May 21st, 1913.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0153" n="art0153">
        <head>CO-OPERATIVE STORES AND POVERTY,</head>
        <p>CO-OPERATIVE STORES AND POVERTY,</p>
        <p>Sir,—ln my last letter I made the remark that Mr. Morton was a study. I now feel convinced of the truth of that statement, for we find the most glaring inconsistencies running through his letter. For instance, after saying "for any body of men to lay down definite forms to which societies or industrial systems shall conform is unnatural and unscientific because social evolution proceeds through the individual "he adheres to a scheme definite and con- cise whereby society may be regenerated. That scheme, as an unemployed scheme, I do not wish to depreciate the value of. I will go so far as to say that it is, in my opinion, the best unemployed scheme I know of. But all unemployed schemes to me are shaky economics. To me there is no room for difference of opinion on economic questions. If there are certain symptoms that are bad in society there is a definite cause. Mr. Morton says : " Here we have so many thousands of unemployed men and women. We must find them work, producing their own necessities. And so as sot to interfere with outside society they must consume their own products." That is all right so far as it 0-)es. But what is the cause of unemployment, and what is the natural scientific remedy for that evil ? The unemployed are tke surplus labour beyond what is necessary to use to produce the commodities of life. That means that those employed are doing more than their fair share of work, doesn't it ? So, therefore, it must naturally follow that those employed should do less work and hand it over to those insufficiently emplcyed. That means that they should work shorter hours all round.</p>
        <p>Mr. Morton is in the same boat as the Syndicalist with regard to the monopolisation of labour. It is my firm opinion that human labour energy cannot be monopolised. I believe that if all the surplus labour that now exists were absorbed to-morrow the capitalist could introduce labour-saving devices that would soon recreate the surplus labour that is necessary to keep his dividend in existence. At the time of the great plague labour became so scarce that the labourer could demand whatever wage he liked until the landowner struck upon the brilliant idea of passing an Act of Parliament making it penalising to accept above a certain wage. The fact that I wish to impress is that we must not forget that, as far as we have advanced in our productive capacity, it is as nothing compared to the possibilities should occasion arise. Even if these factors were inoperative I still believe that the principle of monopolising labour is unscientific. I say there can be no two opinions upon economic subjects. There must be a definite cause and remedy. We know that the necessities of life are extracted from land by scientific machinery operated by human labour. We also know that the owners of the sources of production use these sources for their own enrichment. Humanity, so far, has been so perverse that those with power over the sources of wealth have divided the products in appallingly unfair proportions. In a few words the means of production are used for their benefit solely. The only alternative is that the source of wealth production shall be used for the benefit of all. Search as I will I cannot see that anything else will apply. And then, what about the Parliamentary fiddlers who are going to put these things right? The recent legislation of the Liberal party does not offer any hope to me. Mr. Lloyd George not long ago went up and don the country showing us how rich the rich were getting and how poor the poor were still. Soon after we had the Insurance Act which stipulates that the</p>
        <p>RUS KIN MANX HOMESPUNS. The St. George's Guild, of which Ruskin was master, founded at Laxey, Isle of Man, a mill for the produc- tion of good honest cloths. Anyone needing Dress Lengths, suitings, etc., as recently supplied to H.M. the King, of unimpeachable quality and beautiful design, should send for patterns of THE RUSKIN MANX HOMESPUNS, R the ST. GEORGE'S MILL LAXEY, ISLE OF MAN, or tO HOLHOYD &amp; SON, 110, Station St., Huddersfield</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0154" n="art0154">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, May 24, 1913</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, May 24, 1913</p>
        <p>poorest of us had to pay 3d. or 4d. a week to provide ourselves with money when ill. So magnanimous are the Liberal party that they bad not the heart to pro- vide us with insurance out of the tremendous increase of wealth which Lloyd George was so fond of telling us about. And then what are we to say about their treatment of the miners and railway men when out on strike for a better wage? They would not even force the employers to give a most ridiculous minimum. And now that the railway companies bave advanced wages they are assisting them to get it back by allowing them to raise their railway rates. The Liberal party motto is, " The consumer pays." Will Mr. Morton tell us how much legislation of the type we are now having will be necessary to abolish unemployment and poverty? No, Mr. Morton. Parliament is nobled by those who want to keep things as they are. The pre sent arrangements are quite suitable to them. When- the working class are as conscious as they are of the working of economic forces, the way out is an easy matter.</p>
        <p>LABOR OMNIA VINC-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0155" n="art0155">
        <head>ANOINTING OF THE SICK.</head>
        <p>ANOINTING OF THE SICK.</p>
        <p>Sir,—ln reading your valuable paper last Saturday I noticed an article taken from the Burtonwood Parish Magazine written by the Rev A. M. Mitchell, in which he writes in regard to the anointing of the sick. I hope you will grant me permission to reply to the gentleman in the interests of truth and light. He states the Church has not been true to her mission of healing, as applied to bodily sickness. I admit she has not, but the Church of Christ has been restored in these last days ; for a witness and a sign of the last days, then shall the end come. Another question of the same gentleman is, "When and why did the Gospel of twofold healing of body and soul cease to be heard ? " "When did it precisely prove ineffective ? " It never did prove ineffective to those that had faith, and "Faith without works is dead," and you cannot get anything out of dead matter. I refer the reverend gentleman to the great Apostle Paul. who, when writing to the Church at Galatia, in the last and Bth verse of chapter 1 of the Galatian letter, states, "Though we or an angel from Heaven preach any other Gospel unto you, than that which we have preached unto you, let him be accursed." You have got to admit that the Gospel that was given to the Apostles at Jerusalem is the only Gospel which will save the individual in this life and the</p>
        <p>life beyond the grave. The state of things to-day are brought about through lack of faith which the Lord said should come in the last days. The anointing of the sick is as true to-day as ever it was. To those that ask in faith knowing that He has commanded His servants with full authority to do it, there are wonders and signs and miracles being done to-day; but the people cannot stop to see them. The Lord does not like to see His people suffer. He has given them the plan of salvation, but they do not want it. They only want that which the Minister says, they don't want what the Lord has said, and shall be to the end of the world. But prevention is better than cure, as the reverend gentleman says, all the world over. The Bible says, "Prove all things, lrolclJast that which is good." We all admit that Gladstone was a good man in his day, but we want something for the present day. We are speeded up more to-day than in the days of , Gladstone, and it is sanctioned by the State, but as long as we neglect the principles of the Gospel of Christ, and His wonderful teachings, and let men deceive us, instead of Christ being our helper-and anointing us in time of sickness and trouble of mind, we will never alter things ; and the Lord will have to show us again when He comes, and He will say, "I told you to work out your own salvation and to seek first the Kingdom of God and His righteousness and all things would be added unto you! " G. GLEAVE. 216, Manchester-road.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0156" n="art0156">
        <head>DRAMATIC CRITICISM.</head>
        <p>DRAMATIC CRITICISM.</p>
        <p>Sir,—No good object can be sPrved, I think, by prolonging correspondence with Mr. W. Ashton, to judge by his letter in last Saturday's "Examiner." He disclaims any intention to impute wrong motives to me, and yet he writes of my "hypercritical attitude against the society," and goes on to declare it to be his honest conviction that I " attempt to impede the progress of the movement." lam unwill- ing to discuss with anyone who regards my criticisms on acting as a manifestation of hostility to a move- ment with which I am in entire sympathy, but, lam quite content to leave the judgment of my motives to your readers, who have followed the ShAespearean movement from its inception. _ _ .</p>
        <p>My claim is that I have always done full justice ti the staging of the plays, which has,in every instance, been admirable, and with regard to the acting I have simply recorded my own impressions, without bias or personal feeling whatever. I am sorry that it should be thought otherwise by Mr. W. Ashton or ,indeed by. any person. To make comparison of the work of an amateur society with professional productions appears to me to be out of place. The production of a play by a pro- fessional company is a commercial undertaking, primarily intended to make money, and must always be, to a large extent, a speculative venture. The enormous expense of such productions is only entered upon in the hope that an achieved success will recoup the promoters for the original outlay and by a king run of the piece bring in a revenue yielding a hand- some profit. But in the case of the amateur society, the main object is to educate the taste and widen the interest in Shakespeare's works, and particularly to provide a training in the art of acting for those who are willing to take part in the performances. With this in mind, in attempting the production of a play for one or two nights, the first consideration should be the extent of expenditure to be undertaken in view of the possible and probable receipts to be reckoned upon, and the aims and ambitions of the society should be to attain the highest perfection in the presentation of the play as a dramatic poem—and not to attempt to provide superabundant accessories involving expense out of all preparations to the extent of the receipts from one or two performances of a play. it should not be thought necessary to vie with the great London productions—in this respect—and with regard to the Stratford celebrations (not amatPur efforts by any means) we may well consider the advisability of learning to walk before we attempt to run.</p>
        <p>There is also the matter of the charity it is intended to benefit to be considered, and this again emphasises the need for keeping the expenditure within limits that will leave a fair chance for a reasonable proportion of the public patronage to be devoted to the cause named. Before closing, I think I ought to allude to one other paragraph in Mr. W. Ashton's letter—for he describes it as containing an exchange of pleasantries —and it certainly conveys a most ingenuous assur- ance to me and also propounds a question which I will answer. The paragraph begins, "Although I have not begged the question" (the meaning of this is some- what cryptic) "I know of no one who has suggested, or even hinted, that Mr. A. Perris could act the part better." I accept his assurance, unreservedly, Ido not, myself, believe anyone has done so. The "interesting news" Mr. W. Ashton has learned about "principals merely measuring off so many lengths of figured verbiage" has evidently affected his literary style to an extent he is unconscious of. Mr. Ashton further asks " Mr. Perris to name any stage productions of "The Merchant of Venice' where Act I. Scene 3is omitted." Candidly, I have not. This scene has invariably been acted whenever and wherever I have seen the play performed. Indeed, to omit it would mar the plot completely, the loan being negotiated in it and the "merry bond" arranged on which the entire interest of the play centres. Why this question is put to me I can only conjec- ture. Mr. Ashton thinks I "confuse it with Act 11. Scene 4, but I am not aware of having referred to the omission of either of these scenes. Possibly he has,-in mind my reference to the omission of a character who appears in two scenes in the play, Act 11. Scene 1, and Act 11. Scene 7. If so, I may tell him that the tragedians who played Shylock in the days when stock or resident companies were the rule almost invariably omitted all the casket scenes except Bassanio's. I first saw these scenes in Mr. Charles Calvert's revival, and afterwards in Mr. Irving's productions. Since that time it has been customary to include them, I am aware. _</p>
        <p>ALFRED PERRIS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0157" n="art0157">
        <head>NEWMARKET BY-ELECTION.</head>
        <p>NEWMARKET BY-ELECTION.</p>
        <p>The result of the poll in the Newmarket by-election was declared at Cambridge about noon on Saturday, when the figures were announced as follows : Mr. J. C. Denison-Pender (U) Mr. George Nicholls (L) 4,400</p>
        <p>Unionist majority This is a Unionist gain, the Liberals having won the seat from the Unionists in December, 1910, by 399.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0158" n="art0158">
        <head>DISTRICT NEWS.</head>
        <p>DISTRICT NEWS.</p>
        <p>LYMM.</p>
        <p>• INSURANCE COMMITTEE CLERKSHIP.—In our issue of April sth the Cheshire Insurance Committee invited applications for the position of Clerk to the Lymm and District Insurance Committee at a salary of 1.;20 per annum. It was for the purpose of selecting the most suitable candidate that a meeting of the above district committee was held in the Masonic Hall on Wednesday afternoon, when Mr. Ernest Mos% of Brook- field-terrace, Lymm, was appointed to the office, subject to the approval of the head committee.</p>
        <p>THE MAY FESTIVAL—There have been many inquiries during the week as to the financial result of this year's May Queen Festival, and the Hon. Treasurer, Mr. A. H. Hudson, has favoured us with the following opinion :—" That should the collecting books and cards which are still to be returned come up to last year's standard there is every reason to think that there will be a slight balance on the right side, including the clearing off of last year's adverse balance." LYMM PETTY SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>WEDNESDAY.—Before the Hon,C,E. _Cross, Messrs. T.</p>
        <p>J. Ridgway and S. Davies. MISCELLANEOUS CA SES.—Percy Kinsey, 43, Atherton- ame, Cadishead, was summoned for riding his bicycle without a light at Heatley. It was stated that defendant gave the wrong name and addrns. He was fined 103. and costs, 193. 6d. in all.—Joseph Thomason, Sandy-lane, Lymni, was fined 23. 6d. for having ridden his bicycle without a light at Rush Green.—Sirah Jones Moss Brow, Warburton, was fined 7s. 6d. for keeping a dog without a licenne on the 3rd May. OBJECTED TO "RED TAPE."—George Crompton Owen, 196, Chapel-street, Leigh, was summoned for failing to produce his driver's licence when requested by P.C. Gould in Knutsford-road,High Legh,on May Ist. —The officer stated that the friend with whom defendant was moter cycling stopped his machine to relight his lamp. Defendant also stopped, and when asked for his licence said he had left it in his pocket at home. Asked if he would send it on, he replied: " No ; if you want to see it ask the Leigh Police. It is only red tape." The licence had, however, been produced.—Mr. J. A. K. Ferns, Stockport, who appeared for defendant, said be had changed his clothes and left the licence in his pocket-book. It was only a technical offence and he suggested that justice would be met if the case was dis- missed on payment of costs.—Defendant was fined is. and costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0159" n="art0159">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>HILL CLIFFS BAPTIST CHURCH.—On Sunday special musical services were held in aid of the new organ fund, under the able leadership of Mrs. Kenworthy (choir leader). The afternoon meeting was presided over by Mr. Thomas Bixter, who incidentally stated that in his younger days he had been brought up at Hill Cliffe. The musical part of the programme consisted of the follow- ing solos : —Miss Walker, "The Nazarene" Mrs. Kenworthy, " I will give you rest " ; Mr. Torton, "The sailor's grave." The choir rendered the anthems" 0 taste and see,"" Great and marvellous," and "Hark ! hark my soul " (the solo parts being taken by Mrs. Kenworthy and Miss Walker). An address was given by the Rev. George Curtis on The vision of dry bones." The evening service included the following solos : Miss Walker, " Divine Redeemer" ; Mrs. Kenworthy, " Calvary " ; sad Mr. Turton, "The King of love my Shepherd is," and the choir rendered the anthems: "But the Lord is mindful of His own " and "Hark ! hark my soul." The soloists were highly appreciated, and sang with beautiful taste, the anthems given by the choir were all that could be desired. The Rev. Geo. Curtis preached from words "Sing unto God, sing praises to His name." Miss A. GrounAs vi as the organist, and Mrs. Massey the pianoforte accom- panist at both services.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0160" n="art0160">
        <head>PENKETH.</head>
        <p>PENKETH.</p>
        <p>AN INCREASE OF RATES.—The call for the county has been increased to such an amount that the overseers of Penketh, at a meeting last Friday, were compelled to levy an increase of twopence on the rates. The county call is £6ll 17s. 7d. as against £556 la. 4d. last half year. The total of the other calls, viz : Union, Rural District Council, Overseers and Parish Council only amount to £496. The county therefore exceeds all the local calls on the township by £ll5 17s. 7d. ST. MARY'S MISSION.—The members and friends of the above Mission Church held their annual musical and dramatic performances on Wednesday, Thursday and Friday of this week at the mission. Tne title of the work presented was "Amos, the cripple of Capernaum ; a story of New Testament times." The words, by Mr. C. Gillington,had been set to music by Mr. T. Mee Pattison. This annual event, uhich is looked forward to by the whole of the neighbourhood with great interest, was as usually successful as in the past. Much credit is due to those who have been responsible for the train- ing and also those who so willingly came forward to take part in the performance. The work was made up of solos, duets, dialogues choruses 6:c.. and con- sisted of the four scenes (or parts). The principal actors who were characteristically dressed, looked extremely well in their striking costumes. The leading characters were taken by the following : Chuzr (Steward of Herod), Mr. Wallace Ray ; Joanna (his wife), Mrs. W. Dwerrybome ; Amos (his child), Miss H. Hall; Micah (an old shepherd), Mr. W. Scott ; Baruch (a fisherman), Mr. B. Ray; Bartirmus (a beggar), Mr. A. Goulden ; Plaineas (a Pharisee Mr. J. Hall; Reuben (a Sadducee), Mr. H. Davies; Roman cmturion, Mr. W. Ireland ; Widow of Nail]. Miss B. Scott ; Ezra (a servant), Mr. N. Hatton ; Lamech (a Leper), Mr. Harold Clarke: Zillah (a maiden), Miss B. S.‘ott.—Miss Mary Standbh presided at the organ, with Mr. F. Hall and Mr. J. Ray, violins; and Mr. W. Whit- field (of Warrington), cello. The Vicar (Rev. J. R. Jones) introduced the several characters.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0161" n="art0161">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>LOSS OF HUSBAND. COMPENSATION FOR RUNCORN WIDOW.</p>
        <p>His Honour Judge Yorke Stanger, K.C., resumed the hearing at the Runcorn County Court on Tuesday of the action brought by the widow of the late William Nash, of Nelson-street, shipwright, to recover £295 as compensation from Mr. Samuel Stubbs,Greenway-road, with whom Nash had been employed. Mr. Greaves Lord (instructed by Mr. W. E. Hough, was for the applicant, and Mr. Singleton (instructed by Mr. J. W. Knowles, of Widnes), for the respondent. Nash, who was a strong, healthy man, was instructed by his employer to assist in taking a heavy handcart to Toplocks to load with logs. When going up High- street, which is a steep gradient. he stopped for a rest, and five minutes after reaching the crane at Toplocks, he fell down dead. The post-mortem examination showed that the organs of .the body were perfectly healthy, and the doctor attributed death to syncope, accelerated by the exertion of pushing the handcart. Mr. W. Knowles, on behalf of the respondent, said they had decided not to contest the claim, and con- sented to judgment for the amount claimed. Judgment was accordingly entered for the amount claimed, with costs on scale B.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0162" n="art0162">
        <head>DIED AT THE STATION.</head>
        <p>DIED AT THE STATION.</p>
        <p>TRAGIC END OF EX-WIDNES DIRECTOR OF</p>
        <p>EDUCATION.</p>
        <p>The sudden death took place at the Central Station, Liverpool, soon after five o'clock on Tuesday evening, of Mr. G. Hammond Danby, who recently relinquished the post of director of education at Widnes,after along public service of thirty-eight years in the borough. For the past few months Mr. Dandy had resided at Chiswick, London and at the moment of his death was on his way to Widnes to accept a presentation of a silver rose bowl and a gold pendant from the members of the Widnes Council, teachers, and townspeople. The gifts were in recognition of long and faithful service to the Education Committee, with whom, and the whole of the teaching fraternity, he was immensely popular. Mr. Denby was seventy-three years of age, and his Widnes connection begin in 1874, when he became clerk to the School Board. Under his supervision five elementary schools were built in the town. In 1902, on the passing of the Education Act, he became secretary and director of education for the borough, and it was principally through his effort that a system of evening continuation classes was introduced. A gathering of teachers was held on Wednesday in the Widnes Town Hall, when the Mayor, who presided over a large attendance, said that Mr. Danby was the pioneer of evening continuation classes in Widnes, and had laid the foundations of a system of which they were proud. A resolution of sympathy with the family was passed, and arrangements were made for conveying the gifts, which included a gold pendant for herself, to Mrs. Denby privately.</p>
        <p>Our forefathers neglected their eyes, the present generation</p>
        <p>now suffers.</p>
        <p>Don't neglect yours. CONSULT</p>
        <p>JOHNSTON WATSON,</p>
        <p>SIGHT TESTING OPTICIAN, 84, BRIDGE STREET, WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>PLEASE NOTE. —MR. WATSON attends on Monday and Tuesday from 11 to 4-30; Wednesday 11 to 5-30; Thursday 11 to 1; Friday and Saturday 11 to 5-30. Hours of Business: 9-30 to 7-30. Saturday: 9-30 to 9-30.</p>
        <p>Steel Spectacles from 2/6. Rolled Cold from 8/6.</p>
