      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0164" n="art0164">
        <head>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</head>
        <p>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</p>
        <p>MIRACULOUS HEALING. I wonder if the Rev. A. M. Mitchell is ' aware that, apart from the " Pentecostals " lately so busy in Sunderland, there has long been quietly at work in this country a sect that pins its faith to the passage in St. James' Gospel which the Burtonwood vicar so strongly emphasises in his current Parish Magazine. I am well acquainted with a solicitor and his wife who, Baptists up to that time, joined the "Divine Healers" some thirty years ago. When I attended one of the meetings which they forthwith began to conduct, I found them intensely enthusi- astic, and I hear that they are still " whole- hoggers," if sensitive readers will pardon the convenient expression. They have mean- while built a new house with a large room. specially for the services which they hold regularly. It must be five-and-twenty years since I was last there, and they laid par- ticular stress upon the very points now raised by Mr. Mitchell, even to the " prayer- ful use of anointing oil." Moreover, there were remarkable results while I was pre- sent, and I was assured that they were quite common. I do not know, indeed, whether our Vicar's magazine protest was called for. What with those already mentioned, Eddyism, Emmanuelism, Dowieism, the waters of Lourdes, and the boat of Treves, we have a perfect medley of wonder cures. Seriously, however, as was observed by a reviewer of Dr. Jacoby's "Suggestion and psychotherapy," there is a strange significance in the fact that faith- healing is as prevalent in the twentieth century as it was in the first ages of European culture, the days of Greeks and Hebrews,two thousand and three hundred years ago. The only difference is that medical science has recently studied the subject and established a body of experimental fact. This healing—of certain disorders only, be it understood—by faith is a reality, though it has been grossly exaggerated in popular belief. The crucial point of the whole business is that "sugges- tion," "auto-suggestion," influence of mind in any form, can never produce organic or anatomical change; it cannot, for instance, remove a diseased lung or renovate it, any more than it can restore an amputated limb. While that is true, what about hypnotism? Lecturing on this subject not long ago, Dr. Forbes Winslow said that every day he became more and more conscious of the fact that many diseases were rendered chronic by the neglect of the medical adviser to grasp the elementary fact of the power and influence of the mind on the body. The lecturer conducted experiments with an assistant to demonstrate the powers of suggestion and hypnotism, remarking that his assistant had had every disease under the sun transmitted to him by suggestion. Dr. Winslow claimed that by treatment by suggestion the deaf could be made to hear, the lame to walk, the dumb to talk, and the most excruciating pains could be instantly relieved. So there! BETTER THAN CURE. The Vicar of Burtonwood goes on to show unto us a more excellent way—though to my mind the late G.O.M.'s gospel of 36 chews to each small bite of bread and butter is a counsel of perfection. On the other hand the " stoking " which multitudes habitually practise at meals is indeed to be deprecated. But I must restrain myself, as this is supposed to be a column of jottings only, and I am sorely tempted to write at length upon this congenial topic. Besides, Mr. Mitchell gives more than a hint at eugenics, and—but I really must drag myself away from these inviting subjects, .and hasten on. THE REAL REASON. Those Australian " howlers " in the "Children's Corner " brought a host of others crowding upon me,but I have previously had my fling in this domain, and must reserve the rest for a more convenient season. For the sake of readers who may not have met with it, I may just mention that "The University Corre- spondent" tells a good story of the Bishop of London. In his curate days he once asked a Sunday school class "Why did David wish to be a doorkeeper in the House of the Lord?" "Because," replied one of the boys, "he could walk outside while the sermon was being preached ! " Well, I have known church officers who systematically took a walk in the midst of the service. To be sure they affected jealousy for ancient custom, and pretended that they were loth to break an interesting tradition; all the same, they never attempted to bring into church any loiterers such as their forefathers, who went out for the express purpose, would have made short work of. I understand, however, that to this day, notwithstanding the frowns of the Vicar and the envious glances of small boys anxioas for liberty, they continue to leave their pla -;s at the opening of the "Te Deum " and to stroll round the churchyard for a chat. APT, AND— The "make-up par" on page 6 of the last issue, set me thinking of other "things that one would have liked to have said." Con- sider some of the happy little expressions in Sterne, such as "They order this matter better in France" and "God tempers the wind to the shorn lamb "—which latter possibly odd ones here and there may be sur- prised to learn is not a Scripture passage. We are constantly quoting" That's another story" as Rudyard Kipling's, whereas it constantly recurs in Sterne's great novel as "'That's another story,' said my father." By-the-bye in Philip Gibbs' "The Street of Adventure" there. is a broad, not to say rather brutal, hint that one of the cleverest of our contemporary writers gets his ideas from Ibsen and certain German philosophers.</p>
      </div>
