        <p>" I REMEMBER</p>
        <p>RXXI</p>
        <p>:NISCENCES AND STORIES OF OLD WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>• CLEANED FROM MANY QUARTERS. gonnfield has had a gratifying response to his r Reg est for contributions and hints for his "I ernernbtr " column in the "Examiner," which is the most popular of the many features of the •UP". Further assistance along the same lines will vrelcomtd. The foilowi i ng s a letter from "An Old -outer WARRINGTON CHAPELS SIXTY ' at,rang. YEARS AGO. e Partiality 'for "Pubs." re'ear- A.M.—ln • the descriptions and Minigeenees you have been giving you :Tern to show a great partiality and a very ,go Be knowledge of "pubs." We have always ,li..gerstood you to be a temperance man and tistorian of teetotalism. It looks as if eritY were going to get some curious --Ares • to slons, and your descendents are likely peet have a lot to explain away. Perhaps peeritY. doesn't matter very much, but Pie.lcill put the worst construction upon things loiltlb°llght that as a change you might like ave something about chapels. unlY2three Chapels Remain. *DriltY years ago there were none of the eidsoent chapels in use except Cairo-street, girl'alem Chapel in GOlborne-street and the ake • . t, r Meeting house. vari, fventer at Cairo-street. rLetnember going once or twice to Cairo- Itee' had a three-decker pulpit, made of IraegaTnYtwhich was against the Cairo-street am not old enough to remember strero-street being made, but it was a new .134liketethen and the chapel entrance was from Y-street, On going in, the place had a trerY comfortable look. Many of the pews the! square and had curtains drawn round the'rs,' It looked as though they had built ler,`"aPel with the idea of making the pews •lettl comfortable, but not with the idea of ng People see the preacher. There was Railer friniolpit Y all along the side opposite to the and the minister talked to the gallery tire', There were very few people before Ope,"°r on the ground floor there was an B,Psce and nearly all the pews were "u on each side. Two aisles ran the • Nth of the chapel and broke it up rather 