      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0169" n="art0169">
        <p>BIRDS TO FLY 15,000 MILES EACH YEAR.</p>
        <p>Each week I receive from America some papers and magazines, and I am often interested in the scientific notes and articles that come from across the Atlantic. I have just been reading an article on how some American birds migrate, and am sure you will like me to quote a paragraph or two. I will copy a few lines which refer to the travels of a little bird, the American golden plover. Bird migration has always been and is yet a thing of much mystery. Let the man who has never felt the thrill of this mystery take his atlas and turn to a map of the Western Hemisphere. Let him locate the Arctic Islands north of North America, say 75' north latitude, and with his pencil draw from there a line down along the coast of Labrador, across to Newfoundland and down to Nova Scotia, then across the Atlantic to the Lesser Antilles in the West Indies, from there to Brazil and across Argentina, and finally halt his pencil in Patagonia He will have traced then what is said to be the southward migration of the American golden plover. But let him continue the course across to the Pacific, northward up the coast, then across Central America and up the Mississippi Valley ; 'through central Canada and back to the northern islands. He will then have mapped what naturalists have given as the yearly itinerary of this wonderful bird—a journey of some 15,0001 miles. _ _ _ • . .</p>
        <p>Very many of the little pilgrims that start for the South never reach it. Not the least of the dangers is the encountering of the dazzling electric lights, tall buildings, tele- phone and telegraph wires, and other obstructions in the cities,especially on cloudy nights when the flight is low. At such times the morning reveals the tragedies of the night when little bruised bodies lying here and there in the streets tell of the havcc wrought. Terrible sufferers in this respect are the warblers. Alsocountless thousands of migrat- ing birds have perished miserably by being blown out to sea by adverse gales, and blizzards and unusually severe snowstorms have claimed unnumbered victims. And yet the little creatures continue to face the terrible journey with all its hardship and difficulties year after year. It is a great mystery indeed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0170" n="art0170">
        <head>BOY AND THE BIRD.</head>
        <p>THE BOY AND THE BIRD.</p>
        <p>"Go weed in the garden till half after ten," Rib's mother said sharply; "I'll not speak again." " Dear me," said Rob, sighing, "I wish I could to The robin that's singing up there in the tree. ' Birds never weed gardens, they never bring wool, They do as I'd like to and would if I could. "They've nothing to trouble them, only to sing, And rock on the branch when they're not on tie</p>
        <p>wing." "See here, little boy," said the robin to Rob, "Though you think I am idle, I'm planning a job. "Four mstlings to care for, such great hungly things, There isn't much rest for a father bird's wings. " The cats try to catch us, the boys are as bad, Birds hare work, wants, and worries like others,</p>
        <p>my lad. "Be content aa Clod made us, as bird, boy or man, And do what needs doing the best way we can." —Eden E. Rexford.</p>
        <p>THE WEEKLY SMILE. JUST LIKE A LANCASHIRE BOY.</p>
        <p>Meeting a boy, the Bishop stopped and asked him, " Can you tell me the way to the nearest station ? " The boy pointed out the way. "About a mile," said the boy, staring hard at the Bishop's knee-breeches and gaiters. As his lordship turned away, the boy added: "What's up? Somebody pinched yer bike ? "</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0171" n="art0171">
        <head>HINT ABOUT SUGAR.</head>
        <p>A HINT ABOUT SUGAR.</p>
        <p>The late Sir Henry Thompson, one of the greatest authorities on diet, wrote: "It is scarcely sufficiently known that sugar is a very valuable food where much muscular exercise is taken, and much bodily labour performed. Especially in adults, who are becoming exhausted with labour, sugar may be tiken largely, with the effect of rapidly affording a fresh supply of power." By far the best sugar in the world is grape- sugar. That is the kind which nourishes and sustains the body; is easily digested, and every way beneficial. This invaluable substance is found in the Cu rrant. In fact, Currants are nearly all grape-sugar, and that is why they are so good for the health. Those who lead an open-air life should make a point of eating plenty of Currants. Whether raw or cooked is immaterial, but, as Sir Henry Thompson unmistakably indicated, sugar is a provider of power, and should be used by all in need of nourishment. Common sense dictates the uRe of the best and most palatable form of sugar, which is undoubtedly the Currant. The Christmas pudding, mince pie, Eccles cake, aid many other delicacies owe their perfect flavour to the presence cf Currants.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0172" n="art0172">
        <head>DOG MOTHER.</head>
        <p>A DOG MOTHER.</p>
        <p>A farmer of Beamont, Saone-et-Loire, went with his dog to a wood ten miles away from his house to fetch a load of wood. As he was about to return he saw that the dog bad given bitth to three puppies. He placed the mother in hi 5 cart and drove home, leaving the puppies behind. During the night the dog left the farm and made the journey to the forest and back three times, thus covering over sixty miles. In the morning the animal was found dead from exhaustion in the farmyard with the three puppies by her side.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0173" n="art0173">
        <p>mentioned in Back Dallam-lane. It was entered through Chapel-yard, but I was never inside it, and do not know whether it was fitted up as a chapel. It is the same room which the " Ranters " afterwards used, and is now Mr. Furnival's warehouse. The only chapel I have not mentioned was the Friends' Meeting House, known to .us as the Quaker Chapel. This has changed so little that I hardly need mention it except to say that the approach was from Academy street and not from Buttermarket-street. There was a passage and a pair of iron gates which led through the burial ground to the chapel. Then and Now. The chapels then were very different from the tasty modern buildings we worship in. There was a different flavour about them. In nearly all of them the dead were buried under the floors and memorial tablets were placed On the walls. This was so at St. John's, as well as at Cairo,street, Friars Green and epney. It was a bad custom, I suppose, ' but the gravestones in the aisles gave solemnity to the place and used to impress even childish minds. I don't suppose anyone would want to change back to the old style, but old folks are hard to convince that changes are all for the best. For myself I would like to get back sixty years and hear some of the preaching I used to hear then. Other folks would vote both me and the preaching com- plppely ovt of date. Be it so.—Yours, AUMINATUS:</p>
      </div>
