      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0179" n="art0179">
        <head>PAGE OF THE FREER MANUSCRIPT IN FACSIMILE. Reproduced from The Times" of Wednesday, May 14, 1913, by Special Permission</head>
        <p>A PAGE OF THE FREER MANUSCRIPT IN FACSIMILE. Reproduced from The Times" of Wednesday, May 14, 1913, by Special Permission</p>
        <p>• Ktirrt tilt-A*7ol4'T(°) Xtirystrtic ern av 017701 r dropins KCI 74)1 tirwrias larariip tio-ren itt) • wre(vit)tirwr drdeapra rip AiOftar Tor. a('0)-i'. tcarnAto.o-001 (Kai) rtiplr. lea ✓.ro dw-otraNrlkor crot; tr}r em-YIKKT6- rqv fir?q, iKeirrl be-rot, rti; X(pttrr-)7; cal ti X(pnr.) irurpocrarrir riTt 11-cr Virte- rnt ivos rCor iTior r rtrkts rm.) • tiAAA tiX.Xn ;(r)trA • mt-'4 yip (r)itw [i-yle] eiparnyerdrrtor (et) rard.',l)tr tis Crater Ira ioretrrpt4totrtr r 4 r, dAiOt:a ii pirs-17-1 Altacts4c7torrtr, TM rip ir r.ftirarq; it-r(trpt,r)tictir Kai 6- Ohirror r't;s t`trcv-o.e•rns &amp;far .I.:),lvf,),Pitipsteoss. AAA woo vOivrts . .</p>
        <p>Our readers will be glad to see in facsimile the page from the new copy of the Gospels in which so much interest has been aroused. The new passage begins at the ninth line upon the page and ends on the twenty-fourth. For the advantage of those to whom ancient Greek is unfamiliar we give the passage in modern Greek characters. The article contains an English translation. The pages are of vellum or antelcp3 skin of the size shown in the illustration and are wonderfully well preserved. The accompanying block is reproduced from "The Times" of Wednesday, May 1 4th , 1913, by special permission.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0180" n="art0180">
        <p>Egypt has been for many years a happy hunting ground of the archteologist. Its dry sand and sealed tombs have preserved the relics of an early past in a manner that is little less than miraculous. So well do old inscriptions last in the land of the Pharaohs that no one would hazard a prediction of what may or may not come to light. Keeping in the present article to inscrip- tions which relate to the New Testament our readers may like to know of some very remarkable things which Egyptian sand has preserved for us and which now after long ages have come to light.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0181" n="art0181">
        <head>NEW SAYINGS OF JESUS.</head>
        <p>NEW SAYINGS OF JESUS.</p>
        <p>In 1897 Dr. Greenfell and Dr. Hunt, when excavating upon the site of Oxyrhynchus, a city of ancient Egypt, were rewarded by the discovery of a very large collection of Greek papyri. Papyri, it may be explained, is a paper made from the pulp of the papyrus plant. It has proved as indestructible as granite, and the writings upon it are, in some cases, as clear as when first inscribed. Among the batch of papyrus pages dug from the sand was one of extraordinary interest. It contained a series of what were called the Logia (Sayings) of Jesus. Again in 1903 the patient diggers found another papyrus leaf also containing "Say- ings of Jesus." Though these sentences have been widely discussed there are many of our readers who may be glad to have them set forth. We will quote the most arresting.</p>
        <p>Jesus salth, Wherever there are two they are not without God, and wherever there is one alone, I say. I am with him. Raise the stone, and there thou shalt find me; cleave the wood, and there am I. Everyone that hearkens to these words shall never taste of death. Let not him who seeks cease until he finds, and when he finds he shall be astonished; astonished he shall reach the kingdom, and having reached the kingdom he shall root. I stood in the midst of the world and in the flesh was I seen of them. And I fol:nd all men drunken, and none found I athirst among them, and my soul grieveih over the sons of men, because they are blind In their heart and see not. Jesus saith, Except ye fast to the world, ye shall in no wise find the kingdom of God; and except ye make the Sabbath a real Sabbath, ye shall not see the Father. Jesus saith, A prophet is not acceptable In his own country, neither does a Physician work cures upon them that know ;lin'.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0182" n="art0182">
        <head>FRAGMENT OF A LOST GOSPEL.</head>
        <p>FRAGMENT OF A LOST GOSPEL.</p>
        <p>Another discovery made ten years ago was that of a fragment of a lost gospel which contains the familiar exhortation (though in other words) "Take no thought for food or raiment"—and which adds: His disciples say unto him • when wilt thou be manifest unto us, and. when shall we se thee P He salth, when ye shall be strippod and not be ashamed.</p>
        <p>These sayings are upon tattered pieces of papyrus, and all of them more or less incom- plete.</p>
        <p>THE NEWEST DISCOVERY. -</p>
        <p>The manuscript about which I am about to write must not: be confused with these scraps from ancient rubbish heaps. The latest Bible treasure which Egypt has provided is a well-kept volume made of vellum. Concerning its resting-place during</p>
        <p>the past fourteen centuries or so no one can do more than hazard a guess. It may have lain in a tomb or upon a monastery shelf. Certainly its condition does not allow us to suppose it has had extensive use.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0183" n="art0183">
        <head>WHAT THE DOCUMENT IS LIKE.</head>
        <p>WHAT THE DOCUMENT IS LIKE.</p>
        <p>Six years ago it appeared to view along with three other copies of parts of the Old and New Testament. The owner was a dealer, Ali Arabi, whose story was that they came from Akhmim, the ancient Panopolis But no one places any reliance upon Arab state- ments, and experts, driven to forming their own theory, have concluded that they have come from the White Monastery, near Sohag. It chanced that a wealthy American, Mr. C. L. Freer, was in Egypt when the manu- scripts came to view, and he was able to purchase and carry them upon a long journey to Washington. From thence comes a long report and translation by Professor 11. A. Sanders, of Michegan University, and from thence has come as a present to the British nation a photographic copy of its numerous pages. I propose for the interest of my readers to indulge in a familiar talk about it, and about other famous Bible manuscripts with which it is to be compared. America will be proud of the distinction of holding this treasure, and it is probable that extravagant estimates will be placed upon it by zealous New World writers. Remembering that America has shown so much interest in the Bible no one will regret that at least one outstanding manuscript should rest there, but it is needful to give the warning that it is by no means proved that this new copy of the Gospels is such. THREE GREAT MANUSCRIPTS.</p>
        <p>Those to whom the subject is new may appreciate a brief mention of the three most famous Bible manuscripts which have yet come to light. These three are divided among three great branches of the Church. The Greek Church holds the "Codex Siniaticus." The Roman Catholio Church has the "Codex Vaticanus," ard we in Protestant England have "the Codex Alexandrinus. " The "Codex Siniaticus " is so-called because it was found in a monastery at the foot of Mount Sinai. Its discoverer was Constantine Tischendorf, who visited the monastery in 1844, and who found the monks kindling their fires from pages of old vellum inscribed with portions of the Bible. It was not until 1859, after an unsuccessful visit in 1853, that he got possession, upon loan, of the bulk of the documents he was seeking..</p>
        <p>_ These pages now rest in the Imperial Library of St. Petersburg. There are 346 of them, all of antelope skin, and they contain almost the entire Bible. The date of the writing is supposed to be A.D. 350. The "Codex Vaticanus " rests in the Vatican at Rome. It belongs to the early</p>
        <p>part of the fourth century and contains almost the entire Bible. The treasure of the British Museum is the fine manuscript known as the "Codex Alexandrinus," which came to England as a present to King James I. It has 776 leaves, upon which are written in Greek the Whole</p>
        <p>of the Bible except a few passages which are lost.</p>
        <p>The value of these elaborate manuscripts is that they show to us what were the accepted Scriptures in the third and fourth centuries. They differ, of course, in many respects, sometimes seriously, but they show that in the main the various sections of the Church held the same opinions as to what was Scriptures, and the Bible as we have it is almost entirely based upon them. They are,however, built up from numerous earlier documents, evidences of which can be easily traced, and the interest of scholars is kept perpetually awake by the hope that these earlier records may come to light.</p>
        <p>A NEW PASSAGE IN MARK'S GOSPEL.</p>
        <p>A quiver of expectation passed through the Christian world when it was announced that Mr. Freer's vellum contained a new portion of Mark's Gospel, and as this gives to the MS. its chief claim to distinction, I may write of it here in some detail.</p>
        <p>The new passage follows Mark xvi. 14, and makes it read:</p>
        <p>(v. 14.) And he upbraided them with their unbelief and hardness of heart, because they believed not them which had seen him after he was risen. And they excused themselves, saYing that this age of lawlessness and unbelief Is under Satan, who, through the agency of unclean spirits, suffers not the true power of Cod to be apprehended. For this cause, said they unto Christ, reveal now at once thy righteousness. And Christ said unto them, The limit of the years of the power of Satan is (not) ful- filled, but it draweth near (the text, here and elsewhere, Is corrupt): for the sake of those that have sinned was I given up unto death, that they may return unto the truth and sin no more, but may inherit the spiritual and incorruptible glory of righteousness in heaven. (v. 15 But go ye into all the world and preach the gospel to every creature.</p>
        <p>The new words do not impress us as having the weight and tone of a teaching of JCBUS. Yet even if the tone were not lacking there would have to be much evidence supplied before they could be confidently regarded as such.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0184" n="art0184">
