      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0188" n="art0188">
        <head>LOST PAGE FROM MARK.</head>
        <p>THE LOST PAGE FROM MARK.</p>
        <p>To begin with, it is commonly known that the last twelve verses of Mark's Gospel were not written by John Mark. The closing portion of his Gospel is lostUt an early day the last page or pages became detached from the M.S. and have never been recovered. The Gospel finishes in the middle of a sentence,ch xvi., v.B "Neither said they any thing to any man for they feared.—" My friend, Dr. Bendel Harris, has said that when the lost page comes to view the sentence will be found to finish "feared the Jews,' but this is only an enlightened guess. Our translators rounded off the sentence "for they were afraid." This is as far as we go with the original Gospel of Mark, but very early the Church began to give finish to the story and three very ancient endings have come down to us. HOW THE GOSPEL WAS ROUNDED</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0189" n="art0189">
        <head>OFF.</head>
        <p>OFF.</p>
        <p>The first stands in our English version. It has no value to us seeing that all that is there said is contained in the other Gospels. As a matter of fact we know almost to a certainty who it was who added the verses. Not long ago Mr. F. C. Conybeare found an ancient Arminian M.S. in the Convent at Edsch Miazin, in which they are spaced off, and in the intervening space there is written in red the name " Ariston Eridzou," which is by interpretation, " Ariston the Presbyter." Ariston,we may add, is mentioned by Papias as living in the second century and as having a great reputation in Christian circles for his acquaintance with the traditions and facts of the life of Jesus.</p>
        <p>Another ending to Mark's Gospel is given in a manuscript called Codex Regius, and now comes a third in the pages of this newest document.</p>
        <p>The last is, of course, only an expansion of the one that is familiar to us. It has no doctrinal interest and gives no new emphasis; it makes nothing more clear nor does it help in the elucidation of anything else. The interest is purely literary. Obviously it is late in date, and in my own view may be dis- missed as apocryphal. Such a summary judgment upon the most striking feature of the new Codex may flavour of conceited scholarship. It is, in reality, a conclusion which it requires but an ordinary knowledge of the Gospels and of the manuscripts to reach. Perhaps, too, it ought to be added that the passage is not entirely new, for Jerome quoted part of it in Latin, and in this form it has been long known to scholars.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0190" n="art0190">
        <head>NEW READINGS.</head>
        <p>NEW READINGS.</p>
        <p>Passing to another feature, the Freer Manuscript is obviously composite. It is made up (or is copied from something which is made up) from many originals, in some parts it reveals purity and value ; in others, particularly in Matthew's Gospel, it slavishly follows late Byzantine texts. This marks the writing as a late one, and it is hard to understand Professor Sanders's contention that it dates from so early a period as the fourth century. In the portions which are copied from good and early M.S. there are some interesting points to be noted. Thus in Luke's Gospel it agrees with the two very oldest manuscripts in omitting " Blessed art thou among the women" (Chap. i. 14.). In Chap. ii. 4, the song of the Angels is given " On earth peace among men in whom he is well pleased." Like the Vaticanus and Alexandrinus it omits mention of the bloody sweat, and along with the Vaticanus it omits the word from the Cross " Father forgive them." In Luke xxiv. 36 it reads "Jesus himself stood in the midst of them and said unto them, It is I, be not afraid, peace be unto you." This is found in no other manuscript. Nor is there anywhere else the wording which appears in Mark i. 27, "What is this new, this authoritative teaching, and that he commandeth even the unclean spirits and they obey him." Other variants of this kind will give food for thought and speculation, but we shall be surprised if after mature consideration a high place is assigned to this newest manuscript, With the gravest deference to the learned and capable professor who has Aeciphered and minutely studied it I cannot believe that it will ever be ranked among authorita- tive writings. It is not homogeneous, and while some parts may prove of extreme antiquity, it is a patchwork in which the old will have to be sharply separated from the later and corrupt. In all probability the manuscript purchased by Mr. Freer formed parts of the Bible of the Christians of Upper Egypt. prawn from other copies of all ages and values its parts would naturally vary and the good and the doubtful would blend together, In A.D. 639 the Moslems overran Egypt and the day of the Christians was over. What more likely than that these volumes were hidden or buried at that time and have now come to light. This would give them an antiquity dating from the end of the sixth or beginning of the seventh century. _</p>
        <p>Every circumstance points towards this theory. Thus the three other volumes which came into Mr. Freer's hands were of similar kind, and contained in one of them Paul's Epistles, in another the Book of Psalms, and in another Deuteronomy and Joshua, the</p>
        <p>Contimieel in next column.</p>
      </div>
