      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0193" n="art0193">
        <head>LITERARY GLEANINGS.</head>
        <p>LITERARY GLEANINGS.</p>
        <p>INFLUENCE OF THOUGHT.</p>
        <p>Mr. Algernon Blackwood has written a new story, to which he has given the title "A Prisoner in Fairyland" (the book that "Uncle Paul" wrote). It centres round the idea that at the present time is demanding so much consideration from serious-minded people, namely, the tremendous influence of Thought, an influence that each of us can wield for the good, not only of those around us, but of the world at large. Perhaps it is hardly necessary to add that the workman- ship displays unusual power of imagination and much charm. The book will be published by Messrs. Macmillan &amp; Co. very shortly.</p>
        <p>FIERCE AND EVER-ENDURING WORK.</p>
        <p>When I see the gigantic prosperity and magnificence of Lord Strathcona's position to-day in the heart of the Empire, I cannot help thinking of all the splendid courage, tenacity and endurance which lie behind in those terrible thirteen years in Labrador, amid the tents of the Esquimaux and under the terrors of the Arctic lands. Frozen in winter, tortured in summer by the terrible mosquito, with no companionship save that of a few fellow-settlers, Donald Smith served this grim 'prenticeship. T repeat that what- ever glories there have come to his old age this youth of fierce and ever-enduring work under such conditions has more than earned.—" Lord Strathcona : A study in personality," by T. P. O'Connor, in the June "Pall Mall Magazine." A NEW MONTHLY:" THE PATRICIAN." A new monthly, under the title of the Patrician, has entered the field of sixpenny magazines, and the June number, which is just issued, indicates that it intends to make a special place for itself. Among the varied contents are "An Appreciation" of Dr. Page, the new American Ambassador; an out- spoken article on "Polo's Stormy Petrel," dealing with the dispute as to the governing body of that game; and an enthusiastic review of the far-reaching ramifications of golf by Henry Leach. The English reader is introduced to the well-known author- artist, Miss Kate Carew, famous throughout the States, whose pen in prose and illustra- tion strikes a novel and entertaining note in London theatrical criticism. Mr. Gordon Craig, Mr. W. G. Menzies, Mr. Bernard Capes and Mr. H. M. Brock, are among the other contributors. The magazine, which is liberally illustrated on art paper, makes a special appeal to readers of cultivated tastes. SAY "NO," AND SAVE YOUR SOUL. "The man who has learnt to say no to the demands of his own insurgent nature," says Mr. G. W. E. Russell in the "Manchester Guardian," "has armed himself betimes against assaults from without. Take the case of the politician, the clergyman, the man of letters, the man of business. Each in turn has to bear the brunt of an assault on his moral rectitude. Pick a quarrel with some weaker Power, and your Government will survive the General Election; or support that wrongdoing, and you will save your seat. Preach a dubious doctrine which is popular in the high places of Church or State, and you may die a bishop. Write what you know to be poisonous and debasing, and you may acquire a reputation for artistic realism, and the profit which that reputation brings. Sail very near the wind in professional trans- actions, and you may make a fortune, while the more scrupulous practitioner dies poor. To such voices as these—and who has not heard them?—there are only two possible answers. Let yours, my brother, be No, and you will have saved your soul alive."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0194" n="art0194">
        <head>ENDED IN PARA YSIS.</head>
        <p>ENDED IN PARA YSIS.</p>
        <p>Nervous Exhaustion, the evil of to-day,</p>
        <p>cured by Dr. Williams' Pink Pills.</p>
        <p>In every industrial circle to-day con litions makes much heavier call upon the nervous system. Employers and employed cry out alike, and sad evidence of the strain is seen in the shoals of victims of Nerveus Exhaustion, Neurasthenia, Neuralgia, St. Vitus' Dance and Paralysis.</p>
