      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0198" n="art0198">
        <head>9d.</head>
        <p>9d.</p>
        <p>mum</p>
        <p>ZIEN=111:1</p>
        <p>MEAMMD</p>
        <p>Socialists.</p>
        <p>Inowne</p>
        <p>INDUSTRIAL REVOLUTION.</p>
        <p>Address on "Syndicalism " to Warrington</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0199" n="art0199">
        <head>ANTI-MILITARIST CAMPAIGN.</head>
        <p>ANTI-MILITARIST CAMPAIGN.</p>
        <p>"Syndicalism" was the title of an Interesting address delivered by Mr. Guy Bowman (editor of "The Syndi- ealiq ") to a large attendance in the rooms ef the Bri iii Boni% i-t Party, Market Gate, on Monday evening. _ . .</p>
        <p>The Rev. Dr. S. A. MELLoR presided, ard, in his open- ing remarks, said that he bad once asserted that there were not half-s-dozPn men in England who knew any- thing about sndiolisrr. Ce!tainly Mr. Bowman was one of tie half-dozon, if there were only that number with a good knowledge of • the subject. They would certainly get an authotitat ye statement on that matter, which was of considerable importance and great interest, pat ticularly to those who had any regard to the future. (Applause.) A FEDERATTON OF PRODUCERS AND DISTRIBUTORS.</p>
        <p>Mr. BOWMAN, who was cordially received said he favoured the amalgamation of existing trade unions into industrial unions, and when that process had been got through, there ought to be formed a national federation of those industrial unions, thereby bringing about a complete organisation of all the prolucera. Oa the other hani, he wanted to see a national federation of all trades councils, thereby establirning a national federatic.n of all the distributors, both producers and distributors being consumers. Pro- ceeding, he w. nt on to describe what was meant by direct action, and raid tte organisation that be bad described would work with its two arms. the one arm being industrial warfare and the other arm being social warfare. Describ:ng what he meant by industrial war- fare, he went on to give examples of what had been done in °the r countries and in this country in the way of sabotage.</p>
        <p>ANTI-MILITARIST PROPAGANDA. Referring ta social warfare, he kid stress on the an i•militarist propaganda that had vt to be carried on now and then. He said that ever since the close of the South African war le bad been watcl.ing miltarist developments in this country and he was sore that con- scription was ge ing to be introduced in the very new future, A conscript army thus brought about would be used in order to maintrin the workers ill subjugation, and they Lad to make up their n inds from now onwards as to how they were going to use those arms once they had been put into thci, control. He Advised the workers to use those very aims in order to kill two birds with one stone—namely, war and the capitalist system. A GENERAL STRIKE.</p>
        <p>Retelling to the general strike,he said the expression was very tittle understood as yet in this cr untry. Most people imagined that i t. consis ed of the workers dropping their arms at one given moment, hut it was notbing of the kind. The general strike would take place at the psychological moment, and no one knew when it would take place. All the workers had to do was to use the organisation he first mentioned to achieve three-fold purpose, firs., the obtaining of better con- ditions under the capitalist system; secondly, the fight- ing of the revolution at the p3yohological moment; and thirdly, so be ready to manage the industries themselves after the revolution had taken plane. (Applause) Questions were invited and comments on the address were given by several members. Votes of thanks concluded the meeting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0200" n="art0200">
        <head>NO PLACE IN ENGLAND FOR A GIPSY PEOPLE, PROFESSOR BRIDGE AND THE CARAVAN</head>
        <p>NO PLACE IN ENGLAND FOR A GIPSY PEOPLE, PROFESSOR BRIDGE AND THE CARAVAN</p>
        <p>CHILD.</p>
        <p>Speaking at the annual meeting of the Chester branch of the National Society for the Prevention of Cruelty to Children on Monday, Professor Bridge, organist of Chester Cathedral, said he was pleased to note a decrease in the number of children who were to be seen dragging barrel .organs about the streets. He thought if they took extreme measures with regard to these children, caravan children, and children living on canal beats they would have done three great things in con- motion with their work. It was not right that there should Le 20,000 van-dwellers travelling e:out the country, for this meant that the children never a eat to school. Neither was it right that children should live on canal boats, ond if the society could only get some measure passed whereby these children could have a immanent home they would do a great deal for the moral welfare of the children. He did not believe that In this cc untry we a ere prepared to admit that &amp; gipsy people was desirable.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0201" n="art0201">
        <head>LORD CHARLES BERESFORD AND TU BOY SCOUTS.</head>
        <p>LORD CHARLES BERESFORD AND TU BOY SCOUTS.</p>
        <p>At the Welsh Harp, Hendon, on Saturday afternoon, the Archdeacon of Hampstead dedicated a boat to the local Boy Scouts Association, whose chief Sea Scout is Lord Charles Beresford. Subsequently Lord Charles Beresford prffented a medal to Scout Doxsey for an act of bravery in stopping a runaway horse, and thus saving the lives of others. His Lordship characterised the boy scouts as the greatest national movement of our time, and said that tbe boys were now healthier, CHOICE COMMERCIAL STATIONERY. ! Nippier, and far bett,er ompaniono op gip whole.</p>
