      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0208" n="art0208">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, May 24, 1913. I COMPLETE ' EXAMINER ' STORY.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, May 24, 1913. I COMPLETE ' EXAMINER ' STORY.</p>
        <p>those rascally boys and girls choose to take on their journey, said the Colonel, with a gallant, old - world bow. "You are kindness itself, my dear Colonel, and I should appreciate to the full your de- lightful company and the charming surround. ings if I were not a little bit worried about that mischievous Hatt." I glanced away rather quickly from the Colonel's admirins tzase. however, nothing daunted, he bent for ward and took my gloveless hand in his strong brown one and raised it to his lips. " Dear Lady Susan, we have known each other a long time, and my heart has been yonrs always." lie paused, a nervous tram()) shaking his usually clear, steady voice, / turned to him, and I believe he saw the tears which welled up in my eyes. " You best of friends—l had already guessed what you have put into words," I murmured, indistinctly. We were both very happy—the future pro- mised much that was beautiful. Colonel Sellock and I waited for more than no hour, then we started back. Both of us were rather silent; doubtless his thoughts ran parallel with mine, our respective godchildren were giving us a very uncomfortable half-hour. The landlord met us at the door of the inn and told us the young people had been de- layed in starting, owing to one of the horses having suddenly developed lameness—pro- bably they had reached the monastery shortly after we left..</p>
        <p>We had, perforce, to be content with this small comfort. We spent another hour in the Three Feathers parlour, and I am afraid I allowed the Colonel to talk a lot of non- sense—yes, I, Susan Redmond, plead guilty to having flirted. The delinquent young people arrived look- ing disgracefully happy. " Well, really—how naughty of you to give your chaperons the slip," I said, laughing. My fears were stilled, and I saw no harm in what had happened. "We could not help it," cried Kitty. "Now, will you two dears go into the orchard to the summer-house and make yourselves awfully comfortable while we order lunch—an al-fresco meal is so delicious." The child threw her arms round me and kissed me. " Dear, darling godmamma," she whispered, with an odd tremor in her voice. We lunched in the lovely old orchard, sur- rounded by the most glorious flowers. Brilliant red ramblers formed a canopy above our heads, and on every side were bushes hoary with all colours of roses; it was a beautiful finale to our excursion, and I was sorry when the time drew near for the home- ward journey. "You and Colonel Selloek wait here while we get everything ready," said Kitty, and the three others followed her. " You might employ the time picking roses; the landlord says we may have as many as we like." We did as we were bidden, the Colonel and I, then waited until we saw our motor, driven by Captain Ormonde, come up to the inn door, Kitty stepped in beside him. "We had better be going," I said, rising and walking towards the wicket gate. "Look, Lady Susan!" cried the Colonel. And there before our amazed eyes a second car appeared beside ours, and in it was Ena Marlowe and Hilary Wentworth. Only then were our eyes fully opened—but, strangely enough, I was not angry. I ran for- ward as the motors were about to start—the landlord absolutely deluged the cars with rice. "They've been married this morning!" he shouted, with a hearty laugh. The Colonel and I said nothing; speech was beyond us at that moment. "Forgive us and wish Us luck!" tried Captain Ormonde, with a winning smile, and my heart went out to those happy young people. I threw my roses into Kitty's lap— Colonel Sellock sent his flying into Ena's, and they were off, laughing and waving their hands. My beautiful, wild Kitty, I loved her just as much, and I know the Colonel bore his goddaughter no ill will. We discovered later that Captain Ormond° knew everyone and everything at Lexton, that was w)iy they chose to be married in the little town; and, being well chaperoned, no difficulty from home quarters could arise. The landlord helped the lovers (there was only one road up to the ruins). I love to think of that June day when I chaperoned Kitty. I laugh now—although at the time it was no laughing matter to face the guns in the shape of angry parents. Kitty's wedding-day is always beautiful to look back upon—the luncheon amongst the roses—l close my eyes and see the wealth of blossom and feel the heavy scent in the air, and hear my sweet goddaughter saying, "Dear, darling godmamma!" The Colonel and I are marl and wife now, so our excursion to Lexton AVM a thorough success. But lam much afraid we proved oursslTss sorry failures at chaperoning!</p>
        <p>LTHS END.4I</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0209" n="art0209">
        <head>[ALL RIGHTS RBURVED.]</head>
        <p>[ALL RIGHTS RBURVED.]</p>
        <p>_</p>
        <p>" Where do we start from, dean r And, Kitty, you have not yet told me if your mother thoroughly approves of this eseapaaer "Rather—they all know at home. Mother said me raised no obji•otions If you enaper- oned me."</p>
        <p>" Oh! then we can go easy at heart, and enjoy ourselves to the full." I Wse dressing as hurriedly as possible, and keeping up a fly- ing conversation with Kitty, who remained in my boudoir. I heard her step out on the bal- cony, and not many minutes later caught a fleeting glimpse of a motor dashing up to my front door. Suppressed giggles floated up to me. I believe they were round some conveni- ent corner waiting for a signal from Kitty. Anyhow, everything happened like clockwork. " They have arrived! The car is here!" celled Kitty, more excitedly than I thought necessary. "Very well, dear. Julie has me almost ready for the fray." "Colonel Sellock has not COMe yet—but by the time you are dressed he is sure to be here. "Of course, we cannot start until he does come," I put in, firmly. I was determined not to be the unfortunate "fifth " on a motor ex- cursion. Kitty, with all the best intentions in the world, can be a trifle selfish at times; so I had to be careful. "Don't be a darling goose; of course the Colonel is coming. Do you think we should be so cruel as to ask you out and not provide the necessary escort for you? You have got a poor opinion of me," Kitty said, petulantly. "I'm not saying anything nasty, my dear, only things do sometimes happen to prevent people turning up—the knocking on the head of my parties for this afternoon is proof posi- tive of what I say." I laughed, and peeped into my boudoir. The young men were stand- ing near the window, gazing out anxiously for the appearance at Colonel Sellock's military figure. Ens and Kitty were sitting on the couch holding each other's hands. "Dear Lady Susan, my godfather would no more disappoint me than—well—than the moon would drop out of the sky," cried Ens, 3xcitedly. "Well, nowadays, my dear child, it is never wise to compare or put forth anything as a certainty to go by—really, such extraordinary things do happen. However, it is quite use- less for me to go on preaching, as I hear Colonel Sellock's voice. I am very nearly ready, so the motor trip can be counted as still on the cards," I finished, laughingly. The delightful feeling which swept through me as I heard the Colonel's cheery tones was a de- cided surprise to me. However, my own little throbs of pleasure were forgotten as I listened to the chatter in the boudoir—Kittv's shrill treble rising high above the others: her keen enjoyment was something to wonder at—for just a motor run! A suspicion of something in the air again shot through me • but the merry voices of the four young people soon dispelled this feeling, and, being fully equipped, I joined them. The missing member of the party had turned up whole in limb, and we were ready to start. "How about refreshments? Had we not better take a hamper? Wilson can pack up some eatables in a very short time. You boys and girls never think of these essentials—do they, Colonel?" I turned to the godfather chaperon, and found him eyeing his god- daughter queerly. He evidently was as uneasy as I had been some time previously. "I've seen hampers of all sizes and condi- tions in the ear, so I don't think you need to bother, Lady Susan," he said, drily, "Well, they hhve been provident, and we must congratulate such forethought in the young." I smiled round at Kitty and her friends. "Come along; no time to lose!" cried Kitty—the dare-devil amongst us. And so, laughing and chatting, we stowed ourselves into the big car. With an angry snort the motor bounced off at a good pace, and we started our never-to-be-forgotten June excursion. The road to Lexton was perfect. Every- thing, in fact, tended to make the trip de- lightful. We made several stops en route, and at last, towards evening, arrived at the Three Feathers. "Isn't it a jolly little place?" Kitty said, enthusiastically. "Rather!" agreed her companions. "Wait till you try the beds," said Colons.! Sellock, bringing us down from heaven to earth rather sharply—if practically. "Yes, I suppose that is where the Three Feathers' shoe pinches," I said, thinking of my eosy home. Certainly the coming night did not hold out many possibilities of comfort, However, I did no,t wept to depress the party, " But it ha.s been a glorious run. I think your excursion has been most successful, Kitty dear." This brightened up the others, and we went Into the inn to look up our rooms and take off the generous supply of good old English dust cleaving to us. We brought the evening to a close with it jolly little dinner, and retired early and slept soundly, despite the fact that the beds were hard. They could not have had more than three feathers in them so I concluded that the inn must have been christened after the mattresses and pet Royalty, The next day Wati as beautiful as its preda- cessor, and we all met at breakfast in the highest of spirits. 'Now, I suppose the correct thing to do is to sight-see a bit, before making for home," said Hilary Wentworth, cheerily. "There are the famous monastery ruins and the a te rfalls." " Tes—whlk tun!" cried Ens Marlowe, clapping her hands. II 'Voli will enjoy your, self, godfather—you love ruins, you know." "We must hire a waggonette. The road is quite impossible for the car," Captain Or- monde broke in. "It .will be the greatest lark," Kitty ex- claimed, jumping up and ringing for the land. lord. . . .</p>
        <p>"Kitty, my dear, don't be so impetuous. What do you say to having a look at the ruins?" I said, turning to the Colonel. "I'm perfectly ready to go anywhere you like," he said, good-naturedly. The landlord appeared, and told us he could only provide us with dog:carts. We del ided to have three and drive in twos; all other arrangements were knocked on the head. "There are a good many different roads leading up to the monastery," said the land- lord; and then he went on and gave us a great deal of unnecessary conversation andinfor- mation.</p>
        <p>"We can all take a separate road and meet at the ruins," Kitty said, brightly. And 804 with many misgivings, I consented, Of ourse we wo chaperons were bundled off together. We enjoyed the drive. The polonel was most entertaining, and the time slipped by very pleasantly. The ruins were really magnificent, and the Colonel and I surveyed the surroundings thoroughly. Then we settled ourselves on the grass to•wait for our charges. - They must have taken a mighty long road," the Colonel ejaculated, after looking round the country for the missing members of our party. "I don't see any other road, But perhaps they lie behind that hill yonder, and may have steep parts, which always means delay where horses are concerned." I said this hopefully, but I was beginning to get my suspicious ilymptorns beck again, and was feeling heartily sorry I had undertaken the task of chaperoning Kitty on such a mad excursion. "My experience is that happy young people are shockingly unconscious of the length or the state of roads when they have the companion of their choic9 "With thl?J• 4 folog-c;art, I!! the most sociable vehicle. I know —without a footman at the back eaves.- ilropping," added Colonel Sellock, with a laugh. He stood up and glanced at the country round, in what I thought a very dis- interested way; evidently he was not so keen on sighting the rest of the party as was. fie rfl!lrhec! his (oPO My sOP I)fter 4 woni or two, with a distinct sigh of content which eent_a ridiculous school-girl flush to my cheeks.</p>
        <p>"Well, •We are certainly lucky to have found such a lovely spot in which to wait," I said, hurriedly, to hide my foolish confu- sion. -</p>
        <p>And if I do not bore you, Lady Susan, I am (mite happy to wait bere for as long as</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0210" n="art0210">
        <head>CHAPERONING KITTY</head>
        <p>CHAPERONING KITTY</p>
        <p>BY LILA FIELD Author of " Honoria," "One of a Leg4on,4 "Reaping the Harvest," "Countess Vera's Flight," Arc,</p>
        <p>I was sitting in my boudoir one glorious June morning, sipping my chocolate, and feel- ing very happy .and comfortable—l was at peace with all the world. The little pile of letters at my elbow were either invitations or delicious, chatty notes; I fingered them al- most lovingly, and re-read some. One was from Kitty Fane, my beautiful, madcap god- daughter. Her letters were like her dear self—. sparkling with wild spirits. - I smiled at the large handwriting and the exclamation points which were scattered generously over the paper. I glanced up on hearing the door open, and there, in the dancing sunlight, stood Kitty herself. With a run she was on me, smothering me with kisses., and nearly hugging me out of existence. Kitty! Kitty! Spare a poor old woman I cried, trying my best to unwind her arms from about my neck. "Oh, you darling godmamma!" said Kitty, setting me free and slipping to her knees be- side me.</p>
        <p>" What is it, dear?" I said, warily. I knew perfectly well that these volcanic eruptions of affection meant rocks ahead, in the shape of favours to be asked.</p>
        <p>"You positively beautiful pet, / have such a lovely invitation for you; you will be charmed, darling." Kitty half-rose, but I put my hands on her shoulders to spare myself another attack from her strong arms. "Is that why you have come at this ungodly hour?" I said, laughing. "I am really awfully sorry. Have I dis- turbed you at your breakfast?" The child's face fell. "I was only joking." I patted her cheek, and the smiles appeared. "'You see, Kitty, I am not a girl like you, and I must take care of myself during the season, so I won't apologise for being at breakfast at this hour. I have no wish to deposit a small fortune with beauty doctors, and with a little sense a woman can be her own beauty doctor." "Yes you are wonderful!" Kitty acquiesced; but the twinkle in her eyes did not please me. "Well, my dear child, don't speak in a tone of voice which implies that, for my extreme antiquity, I have worn well. I am not a curio —quite.' I turned to my chocolate, which was getting distressingly cold, and drank it up, not looking at my impertineet god- daughter. "Yon are not offended, dearest godmamma?" The sweet voice was almost tearful, so I kissed Kitty, and the sun shone once more. I rang for my maid, and the breakfast para. pliernalia having disappeared I ensconced myself in a cosy chair, and Kitty settled her- self on a stool at my feet. "Now for this surprise invitation, dear. Tell me all about it. I am much afraid there is mischief in the air. I feel it in little electric shocks down my back; besides, your eyes are tell-tale, Kitty. I laughed as I saw the thickly-fringed lids fall swiftly over the bright blue eyes. "Not a bit of it—it is just an orthodox in- vitation. I want you to chaperon me on a little excursion I have planned, that's all." Kitty did not look at me while she spoke, and I detected an almost imperceptible nervous tremor in her voice. "Well—that's all. What is all, dear? Of course, you know I delight in chaperoning you—but I must be told something about the excursion. Your parents are very strict, Kitty dear, And while I am responsible for you, I cannot be A party to anything in the least bit naughty." I did not smile on the lovely face turned up so anxiously to mine. "Don't preach, there's a darling. I have only planned a motor trip—nothing naughty about that, is there?" "Well no," I decided, carefully. "Where do you propose going, and what people are you asking?" "I thought to Lexton. I have asked Ena Marlowe, Colonel Sellock, Captain Ormonde, Hilary Wentworth; then you and I would complete the party. It would be such fun— just great!" cried Kitty, jumping up and dancing round me. "Do chaperon me, dar- ling godmamma." 'Captain Ormonde! That is the young man who has been paying you so much atten- tion—or so people say. lam told your mother has a more brilliant match in store for you, though," I remarked, eyeing Kitty closely. " Nonsense—simply gossip!" "Well, I like your guests, any way. By the way, is not Ena engaged to Mr. ;Wentworth? Also, in her case, her parents don't approve of the attachment, I believe." "That has nothing to do with our party, dear. Ens is oonetantiv about with Hilary, and the engagement is still on." Kitty re- seated herself, fondling my hand suspiciously. I noticed her expedition had already become "our party." I smiled. " I eee nothing against your excursion, dear; I think I can chaperon you. But Lexton is 80 far off; surely we can't manage to go and come in one day?" "No, of course not; that is where the fun lies. We go one day, and come back the next. We can put up at the Three Feathers— such a jolly old inn," cried the child, raptu, rouely. "Oh, Kitty! The Three Feathers has put me off rather. I suppose there is a bar and all that? Think, deart hew horrid to have a lot of rough men drinking underneath one's very beds!" I held up my hands depre- catingly. "Not quite, dear. You know it is usual to have a floor between," said Kitty, wickedly; and we both laughed hilariously. The forth- eoming excursion was beginning to excite me, and I believe from that moment I became both blind and dense. "Is not Colonel Sellock some relation to Ena?" I asked, disjointedly. " Yee, the same as you are to me." "Oh, so a godmother ad a godfather are to look after you four young people and keep you safely out of mischief?" I pinched the rosy cheek, and Kitty nodded. My answer is the only thing necessary to complete the arrangements?" " Yes' the whole trip is off if you refuse." "That would be heartless of me; besides, this idea of yours of an unconventional jaunt In the height of the season has rather intoxi- cated me. The Feather, too, le glorione," I said, gleefully. "Yes, isn't it?" chirped Kitty. " Now, you won't be long getting ready, dearest?" "What! You don't mean you have fixed to- day? That is most terribly energetic of you, iftty. Dear me, to think of having to jump into my motor ialothee and whisk out ef town, after having been mad enough to go to three parties last night." I rose slowly, made pre- tence at a yawn, and glanced at myself in the glass. Yes, I was looking very well— for a widow nearing forty, and having lived a very full existence ever since I made my debut, and the thrill of pride which filled me with delight was pardonable. "My dear child, the engagements I have made for this afternoon—how can I dispose of this formidable difficulty?" I sat down again, a blank look on my face. "Just wire if necessary,'; said my god- daughter, airily. f` There is the Crayshaws' party and Lady Set!s eoncert." went arms to my escri- toire and picked up my engagement-hdek. "The Crayshaws are 'giving two more parties, and Lady Seton is having another concert in July. Come, dear, don't refuse pie; besides, think what an awful disappoint- ment it will he for Colonel Sellock if the party falls through,tl Kitty laughed and shook her finger at me playfullf, and was fooiish enough to lose control of my feelings and allow a slight blush to heighten the tone of my complexion. I was nearly angry, but my goddaughter kissed away the rising temper. "How can you help having heaps of ad- mirers if you are so fascinating and lovely?" she said. Saucy, flattering minx, she fairly wound me round her little finger.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0211" n="art0211">
        <head>PEACE SOCIETY REPORT,</head>
        <p>PEACE SOCIETY REPORT,</p>
        <p>PRAISE FOR THE PURSUIT OF PEACE BY</p>
        <p>THE GREAT POWERS</p>
        <p>American Light and Shade.</p>
        <p>The rerort of the Peace Soc'ety for 1912-13 adopted at its annual meeting, on Tuesday, opens with a reference to the cessation of hostilities in Tripoli and the Balkans, and expresses satisfaction with the united and patient pursuit of peace on the part of the great Powers, especially the co-operation between Germany and Great Britain. The lessons of these wars are pointed out, and note is taken of the growing body of pacific senti- ment in all lands. _ _</p>
        <p>Disappointment is expressed with the action of the American Senate both es regards the rejection of the arbitration treaties and the refnsal of arbitration in the question of the Panama Canal tolls; but gratifica- tion with the new politiral regime of peace in America, the whole-hearted support of arbitrAtion by President Wilson and Mr. 'Aryan, the celebration of tAta Centenary of Peace, the Vranco-Cierman Conference at Geneva,and similar incidents ; other current events are also noticed. The ordinary work of the society has been well sustained during the year. 'I he Council has met more frequently,considerably over 500 addresses and meetings standing to the account of the secretary and agents, while its auxiliaries and affiliated societies have increased in activity and numbers, Pence Sunday was observed as usual on December 132nd. The circulation of the " Herald of Peace " and the "Olive Leaf" has kept up, notwithstanding their less frequent issue. Other agencies and activities are in a healthy condition. As part of the centenary work a bust of the late Ring Edward VI!. is being placed is the Peace Palace at the Hague, and the Secretary will leave early in Septem- ber for a propagandist tour in Canada in response to invitations from the Eastern and Western Provinces. Reference is made to the seciety's co-operation with other societies in the general work of the peace propa- ganda, and especially to its work in anticipation of the forthcoming riague Conference, and to its losses by death. The report concludes with a recapitulation of reasons for gratitude with the affirmation that the work and special testimony of the society were never more needed than now, a call to renewed t ffort and consecra- tion, and an appeal both for loyalty. WO], and prayer, and for larger support and 3 new souse of stewardship.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0212" n="art0212">
        <head>DEATH OF A YOUNG SCOTTISH ACTOR.</head>
        <p>DEATH OF A YOUNG SCOTTISH ACTOR.</p>
        <p>The death of Mr. George Talley Greig at the early age of thirty-four his removed one of the number of North-country players who were brought into close touch with the London and provincial theatrical public by the production of Mr. Graham Moffat's well-known comedy " Punty." Born In Ayrshire, where his father was proprietor of a woollbrohing buoiness, Mr. Greig went on the stage at the age of nineteen, and for some years was associated with Mr. Osmond Tearle in Shakespearean works, but his familiarity with the dialect naturally endowed him with a bent for Scottish pieces, and when "Bunty " was first produced at the London Haymarket he was cast for the part of Jeemes. He had the distinction of taking part in COO consecutive performances of" Bunty " in London slope,</p>
        <p>WOMEN'S QUESTIONS.</p>
        <p>-- Colour in Dress and House Decorations :</p>
        <p>Its Influence on Our Lives.</p>
