      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0213" n="art0213">
        <head>NURSES SCARCE AS CURATES.</head>
        <p>NURSES SCARCE AS CURATES.</p>
        <p>King Manoells bride is distantly related In h-11 She is very pretty, has small. well-ahaped lient"rr" A POW. dark hair. She, like our ewe Primeness, Mary aine a Louise of Fruiter's, is an only daughter, and large fortune, for her father, tho hood of the 11."'.. Catholic branch of the House of Hoheirsollerar fi'Oil rich. Her mother was Princes, Maria nnred% of Bourbon, Sicily, and a niece of the late Rinitt,floo Anstria. Her grandmother' who married ,f Ferdinand of Ssze-roburg Gotha, was a ron""Tee Prince Albert, the Corson ef Queen Vietoria... ,_et ladies of Lisbon have started a fund with the 'IL, of presenting the bride with a suitable preeent marriage. COLOURS TO LIVE WITH. The present is the meets when Spring cleanin Is 0g Its worst and the painter and paperhanger 7for possession of favourite rooms, ousting the occuloni_iii ' the time being, who feels like a fish out of water a, • he resumes possession. However, this should prwv.iti blessing in disguise, if it has provided the opeertnn_er for having an ugly florid and much-patterreci replaced by a really soothing artist's wsli-coisr.:l Following the fashion Of so many revivals, some of 'tho modern decorators are suggesting a return W primitive whitewash. From a hygienic point of this is moat desirable, but dead white walls are 1.1..• ir jurious to the eyes and should be banned. m. 100-!;/ for black is morbid and sad. Rooms should be ii and cheerful as it is possible to make them-1101 ":,-„el Is no doubt that the colours with which we surrn—ot onrselves influence us mentally to a much greeter 01" than is generally apprehended. THE INFLUENCE OF COLOURS. 1,0 Yellow briugs the sunlight into a north for a south aspect it is too bright. It is said to the effect of a tonic' on the system. Green le ,4514 soothing to the tired and weary brain, but sheer res. in this colour should be avoided as they so often oe%°.. tam n arsenic. Psychologists assert that rod Ise:bil ing, but strangely enough it has a very irritating 0117,4 on some temperaments. For a dining-room it is a 11°"it choice, but should be avoided in • room used for end mental work. Scientists maintain that esitt, germs are antipathetic to particular colours and 1;1 the bacilli of cholera and typhoid dislike ultra Infos blue. Blue induces a more or less spiritual weeny s said to act as a narcotic on some brains. For u:t maj pcity of rooms, eepecially in the eass those of medium proportions' a plain • I. preferable, since it increases their apparent 0' It is also more restful and affords a better beokgrnir.r for ptctnrer. Distempered walls are decidedly Dupe"' to papered from a hygienic point of view. IHE DRAWBACKS OF THE NURSING PROFESSION. There is every likelihood that there will be a 'cartel of nurses, as of curette, In the future. The pretend° of nursing is also a vocation for women ef educeSinil and when first taken up by a trained and intellect.° cla.,e, it attracted numbers of eernest women elset t.'d spend and be spent In the public service. But the 167 hours, rigi I discipline and low rate* of Wm"; together with a lack of consideration WO. by governing bodies for the comfort and erslistio of the nurses have caused a turn of the tide. Thins t a falling off in the number of volunteers, and man! these are not unquestionably the best of their oleo': There, too the nursing profession offers few 01105 tunities of distinction, compared with others, An, the professional life ef a nurse is extremely short, Ina; there is a tendency to appoint young matrons ". important positions. At the end of ten or fifteen ye,11,..r_ a nurse may find herself without reguiar occupetwi: and without any adequate provision for her old 417 Nursing, as • profession has many attractions for women, but the nurse to-day, who is considered we.l:g' .. out at thirty-five or forty, is undoubtedly the elotif: of a 'tasteful hospital system, and unless impro/7; conditions are offered a shortale of the best cis. " volunteers must result, THE BACK VIEW. There is no part of the dress, coat or hat that reoeir° more attention than the back. Time was when worts': concerned themselves not at all about the back view' e'L, their dresses or headgear. The former, as a matter n` course, was all perfectly plain, whilst for their COO they aspired to nothing more ornate than a panel hal But time. have altered, and the trimming !IL, ornamentation of the front of a garment is scarcely important than the back, and diversity is sought for tn" o. e quite as much as for the other. The innoesttn. began with buttons and went on to hams, and now M. are bows, loops, d O draperies, embroidered motifs, 0!,1; ribbons or tiny device that affords a change. The, prot,',/ - coats of &amp;leered silk that are to he worn over the Irb"; • lingerie dresses this slimmer—even- if we get one—lov, , themselves admirably to these ornamented books. 1 curious idea, until one gets aoonstomed to it, is that ' ;finishing the back of an afternoon or evening frock wlth a knot of tiny fiJwers. Sirmetimes rosettes ere 13s'd_,. Instead of these, made of gold lace, maitipuiated in 0111". a way as to resemble asters or dahlias, and there sr. frequently wen On the backs of pretty gowns. THE CULT OF COLOUR. ti Rose from palest pink to damask red, and ys•-° from pale lemon to deep orange, coppery toed tO gamboge, are favourite shades for evening silks ne,, other fabrics. It is scarcely an exaggeration to 07 that colour is almost worshipped just now end the glowing tints of the Italian Renaissance are all in nill,bA favour. The Court dress of to-day is of Dome II"' brocade, embossed and embroidered with gold, silver: or copper, and ableze with colour. Such dresses rnig.b: have been worn in old Florinoe in the days of in; Medici, and are evidently inspired by the plottires el" Italian artists of that time. In every accessory of 01 toilette of the fashionable woman the cult for elri' colouring is apparent. The indispensable handbag 01 ' fallen under the spell and is gorgeous in colour as Of rainbow or the tail of the peso ck Gold and silver mei! purses have clasps, jos! lied with red, blue, green ana..," violet stonreend leather in every bright hue emploYa" for the newest handbags. Coloured bead purees arC coming into favour again and Can be quite easily mea. at home. THE BLACK HANDBAG AND NECKLACAS. • The sober black bag of former years has lost ita sobriety and is adorned with clasps of opalesoto4 colours in stones and enamel, or it has corners and clasps of Dresden china, glowing with colour; whilo baskets of roses in the brightest hues 44011111 other bee' Last year amber-coloured beads hid a great y-ngue; this year necklaces contrast vividly with the &amp;teflon with which they are worn. The long chain of beads will be universally worn this summer, and it moat b. admitted that some of the new ones are in anything but good tatty, The effect may be wonderful, tint werild spoil, rather than enhance, the beauty of • dainty dress.</p>
