      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0225" n="art0225">
        <head>LANCASHIRE COUNTY CLUB COMPETITIONS.</head>
        <p>LANCASHIRE COUNTY CLUB COMPETITIONS.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Lancashire County Bowling Association, held on Tuesday evening in Manchester, permission was granted to the Ship Hotel (Irlam) Club to hold a charity handicap, the proceedings of which are to be given to the widow of a well-known bowler in the district who died suddenly last week. The draws for several club competitions were also made, and resulted as follows (matches to be played to-day, Saturday, on the greens as stated) : CLUB CHAMPIONSHIP.—Third Round.</p>
        <p>Leigh Railway v. Chorlton Bowling Green, on Swinton Liberal green. Wigan Subscription v. Swinton Liberals, on Radcliffe Witbins green. C.W.S. Irlam v. Whitecross, Warrington, on Kersal green. Wigan, Limited, v. Warrington Cemetery, on Newton- le-Willows green. Lord Nelson, Urmston, v. Rylands Recs., on Roebnck, Flixton, green. Warrington Bull's Head v. Man and Scythe, on Rail- way, Cadishead, green. Roebuck v. Midway, on Chorlton green. Newton-le-Willows v. Radcliffe Withins, at Wigan Subscription green. CONSOLATION COMPETITION.—Second Round.</p>
        <p>Peel Green v. Middleton Old Cock, on Clowns Hotel green. Prestwich Red Lion v Didsbury, on Ship Hotel green Irlam.</p>
        <p>Heaton Park Conservatives v. Railway Hotel, Cad's- head, on Railway Hotel green, Heaton Mersey. Sandon v. Refuge Adsurance, on Rylands Recreation green, Warrington. Westinghouse v. Clowes Hotel, on Lord Nelson green, llrmston. Old Bowling Green v. Kersal, on Harpurhey Cemetery green. HarPurhey v. Chapel House, on Prestwich Conserva- tive green. Whitfield v. Angel, on George (Prestwieh) green. Colveston Park v. Prestwich Conservative, on War- rington Bull's Head. Earlestown v. Park Hotel, on Stanley Arms green, Liverpool. Stanley Arms v. Warrington Junior Conservative, on Wigan, Limited, green. Byes : Moston Conservative, Ship Hotel (Rainhill), Railway Hotel (Heaton Mersey), and Red Lion (With- ington).</p>
        <p>SECOND TEAMS COMPETITION.—Second Round.</p>
        <p>Earlestown v. Newton-le-Willows, on Warrington Cemetery green. Roebuck (Flizton) v. Whitefield, on Midway (Levens- hulme). green. Wigan Subscription v. Radcliffe Withins, on Peel Green (Patricroft) green. Swinton Liberals v. Rylands' Recreation, on Earles- town green. Refuge Assurance v. Angel (Stretford), on Didsbury Hotel green. Prestwich Conservative v. Belmont, on Waterloo green (Withington). Griffin (Broughton) v. Warrington Cemetery, on Angel green (Stretford). Leigh Railway v. Man and Scythe, on Heaton Park Conservative.</p>
        <p>The draws for the next rounds will take place on Tuesday evening next, May 27th.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0226" n="art0226">
        <head>BRITISH CROWN GREEN ASSOCIATION.</head>
        <p>BRITISH CROWN GREEN ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>COUNTY CHAMPIONSHIP. LANCASHIRE V. DERBYSHIRE.</p>
        <p>Five Warrington players appear as chosen members of the Lancashire teams selected to play against Derbyshire in the match arranged for June 7, 1913. Home team to play at the Midway Hotel,Levenhulme:— Messrs. J. W. G. Coombs (Roebuck) (captain), W. W. Wells (Roebuck), H. Banks (The Height), C. Fearns (Park Hotel, Southport), J. Isherwood (Radcliffe), I. Valentine (Stretford), J. Sladen (Radcliffe), J. Gleave (Warrington), J. W. Jolley (Warrington), T. Sykes (Heaton Kersey), J. Unsworth (Newton-le-Willows), G. Ball (Warrington). Reserve : F. Saxton (Prestwich),R. Harris (Cliorlton), and W. H. Barlow (Heaton Park Conseviatives). Referee, J. Lawrence. Measurer, J. Cawley. Away team, to play at New Mills: H. S. Connolly (Roebuck) (captain), G. W. Corner (Roebuck). C. W. Smith (Roebuck), J. Grundy (Leigh Railway), A. Gleave (Whitecross), J. Platt (Belmont), J. W. Ward (Liverpool), G. Johnson (Stretford), .1. Grundy (Man and Scythe), H. Fairhurst (Wigan), J. Charnock (Rain- hill), and T. Humphreys (Rylands.) Reserves : J. Bridge (Wigan), W. Maudsley (Rain- hill), and W. Sharley (Sandon), Measurer, A. Goodwin.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0227" n="art0227">
        <head>NOTES AND COMMENTS.</head>
        <p>NOTES AND COMMENTS.</p>
        <p>Local bowlers certainly got a hustle on last Satur" day, for in the three principal competitions nearly 30 games were decided. In the Warrington and District Associaticn competition the following teams enter the second round: Bull's ilead,Fearnbead,Senior Conserva- tives, Junior Conservatives, Crosfield's No. 2, and Cemetery Hotel. The most decisive win on the home and home matches was that of Fearnhead who defeated the representatives of the King and Queen Hotel by 79 points. Crosfield's No. 2 won by the narrowest margin, being only eight points ahead of the Penketh Tannery men.</p>
        <p>Bowling of good quality was w:tnessed in the Works competition first round matches on the beautiful Horse and Jockey green. Four single figure victories were recorded and the best win of the afternoon was only one of 29 by Monks Hall and Co. (Rivet) over Richmonds (Grappenhall.) The individual handicapping system is working splendidly and one feels that the success of the Association is again asiured for the present season,</p>
        <p>The Liberals are again going ahead in the Peter Peacock Challenge Cup Competition. No fewer than nine games were decided before a fair number of spectators on the Bank Quay Green, the various matches proving highly interesting. Fairfield had recorded the greatest victory—on paper—disposing of the Bewsey Club men with 65 points to the good. There was not a single dull moment throughout the afternoon.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0228" n="art0228">
        <head>GAMES.</head>
        <p>THE GAMES.</p>
        <p>Good ofd Bull's Head ! To beat the wiremen by 46 clear points is an achievement of which you can be justly proud. Rylands played exceedingly hard in the first match and when they crossed over to the Church- street sward, they were only a dozen points behind.</p>
        <p>But the Bull's Head contingent at home are a dangerous lot and, only losing on three individual games, entered the second round with a 34 point victory. They lost only six games all the afternoon.</p>
        <p>Thomas Rigby (Ball's Head) was in rare form, and</p>
        <p>at Gorsey-lane defeated T. Humphreys by 21-7 in 22 ends. In the second match he soon showed H. Langley the right about, that worthy leaving the green with only ten points to his credit. Langley landed four doubles and two singles, but Rigby had both there on no fewer than eight occasions.</p>
        <p>S. Massey (Rylands) made a plucky stand against J. Cooper at Gorsey-lane, losing by the odd chalk in 31 ends. In the early stages A was "evens" on the game, for at the 15th end the score was 10-10. To the finish it was a ding-dong encounter, the players peeling with 11, 15, 16, and at the 29th end the game was 19 all. With a good wood Massey got the lead, but Cooper came up with a couple of beauties, winning by a single point.</p>
        <p>.Andrews (Bull's Head) did not have matters al: his own way in his game with J. Langley, for at the beginning he was badly behind, the wireman leading 103 at the 10th end. Andrews ran the woods well and landed three doubles, a single and a double, peel- ing with 12 at the 17th end. The scores were again even at 16 but at this juncture Langley made a spurt gaining a two point lead, only to fall away on the next two lengths, where Andrews gained three points. Excitement ran high when the score was 19 all and Langley took the little 'un. But the length suited Andrews who landed the winning couple of points.</p>
        <p>What a great game it was between J. Isherwood, Bull's Head, and Herbert Langley, the popular Gorsey- lane trundler! Words cannot express the consterna- tion of the wiremen when Langley had not recorded a single chalk at the sixth end, whereas his opponent was seven up. Langley was always in arrears up to the 27th end, when the game was 18-17 in favour of Isherwood. On the next length Langley bowled well for a double. Isherwood played very determinedly and peeled with 20, only to fail cn the next length where Langley was successful with one of his woods.</p>
        <p>Fred Casey (Bull's Head) gave J. Gleave a good run for his 21-19 win. Casey led all the way up to the last two ends, when Gleave showed what he could really do in landing a double and a single, which concluded a splendid run of seven and also the game.</p>
        <p>Now Jolley (Bull's Efea6)—yes, the great J. W.— must admit that he had all his work cut out to beat A. Stringer by the odd chalk in 29 ends, The scores were equal at 10, 12 and 13. When the last peel came off, stringer ran away with the block and led 20-10 at the 20th end. Two doubles and a single, well earned by skilful bowling, made Jolley the winner.</p>
        <p>On the King and Queen green, the contingent were well away, even H. Williams (Rylands) only recording six points against A. Handles, who played exceedingly well all over the green.</p>
        <p>Humphries made amends for his shocking defeat on the Gorsey-lane sward, by disposing of J. Isherwood to the tune of 21-12 in 26 ends. There were no really big runs, but Humphries was the better man all through.</p>
        <p>W. Roughley, Rylands captain, although defeating G. Shaw (No. 1) 21-9 in the first game, had to knuckle under to G. Gleave (No. 2) on the Church-street sward, but it was only by two points. The game was touch rtud go from the start, and Gleave only had 20-19 at the 32nd end. He landed a real beauty on the next length, which won him the game.</p>
        <p>Eyes (Bull's Head) gave a fine exhibition of green craft in his game with J. Gleave, winning finally by 21-20. Weave was rather unlucky, for he led practically all the way up to the 29th end, when Eyes peeled with 19. After peeling again at 20, Eyes was fortunate enough to land the winning wood on the next length.</p>
        <p>The King and Queen men made the journey to Fearnhead with too few points in hand, to ever hope of success, and the Fearnhead trundlers did not mince matters in the least, but revelled in their S 2 point victory. Needless to say, it was a one-sided show, for it was only on two men that Fearnhead had to give way to their opponents.</p>
        <p>The best game of the afternoon was that in which J. Ho!croft (Fearnhead) beat T. Timperley 21-19 in 30 ends. The players peeled with 4,8, 12. 17 and 19 at the 28th end. Here, however, Holcroft got on a bit of favourite land and scored a single and a double which gave him the game.</p>
        <p>J. Warburton (King and Queen) recorded the lowest score, one of four in 17 ends, against J. Bradshaw. Short and sweet, Bradshaw, eh? That's the way to win, and win quickly.</p>
        <p>W. Ball played a splendid game. Would you believe it, that be was S-17in arrears at the 20th end, but securing the jack, made a splendid 13 run to the dismay of Gleave, who was defeated 21-17.</p>
        <p>A battle royal was fought between the Liberal Club and the Conservatives, the latter winning on the home and home matches by 36 points. The Liberals were quite happy at Bewsey-street, for they gained a magnificent victory by 45 points. The Conservatives wele only successful on three individual games which gave the Liberals plenty of confidence when they journeyed to the Sankey-street green.</p>
        <p>A. Scott and F. Dale (Liberals) were a well-matched couple. They peeled at 3, 14 and 18, Dale never being in arrears until only at the finish, Scott 19-20 behind landed a couple and won by the odd point.</p>
        <p>J. Broadhurst (Liberals) was one of the trio to lose, but in losing to Hughes by two points, he was in no way disgraced. This was a keen game from the first, and the scores were even several times, namely at 8, 12, 17 and 18.</p>
        <p>One of the best games in the match was between H. 0. Turner (Conservatives) and T. O'Brien, the latter winning by on. y 21-20 in 30 ends. There was not a pin to choose between them, and even when the scores were 19 each, not one on the green could have forecasted with certainty who the winner would be.</p>
        <p>W. Patrick (Liberals) was in good form and although his opponent W. Thomason led 19-15 at the 2,lth end, the secretary of the Works Association went home with a splendid run of fine, three singles and a double.</p>
        <p>At Sankey-street the Conservative won by Si, each player being quite at home and confident. Four of the Liberal men failed to record a double-figure score.</p>
        <p>F. Heeson (Conservative) had to play hard to heat W. Patrick by fl-19, the latter playing good bowls on every end.</p>
        <p>R. Straw (Conservative) could not come up to A. Lawton, for that player had the better of matters nearly all the game, to win finally by 21-20 in 33 ends.</p>
        <p>The Conservatives are out for big game this season, and they will put up a good fight against all corners. One was sorry in a sense to see the Liberal team defeated, for they were considered to have one of the best teams in the competition.</p>
        <p>The Junior Conservatives are coming out this year, for on Saturday they entered the second round of the competition by beating Whitecross by 38 points. At Whitecross the home contingent won only four indi- vidual games, but on the total they were victors by 26 points. At Orford-lane the Conservatives won by 64, winning all but two games.</p>
        <p>The games were intensely exciting, t,Pe best being between J. Horton (Whitecross) and J. Monks, the former winning by 21-20 in 33 ends.</p>
        <p>H. Broadhurst (Whitecross) came pretty near winning against D. Clayton, who led 20-13 at the 24th eud. Broadhurst then had a beautiful rise of six, hut eventually lost by two chalks.</p>
        <p>A game similar in character was played between F. Hardman and A. Thornley, but the latter made a slightly better show than his predecessor, for after being 13-20 in arrears at the 26th end, he peeled with 20 at the 31st length, only to lose by one.</p>
        <p>Crosfield's No. 2 gained a noteworthy victory over the Penk-eth men, winning the first match at Hood- lane by 47 points, but having that advantage reduced to one of eight on the Penketh green.</p>
        <p>The Penketh men are veritable terrors on their own land and played exceedingly well on Saturday. Wilcox played a good gamo against J. Lloyd (Penketh), only to lose by the odd point.</p>
        <p>Some excellent games were witnessed at the Cemetery Hotel, where Croefield's No. 1 were enter- tained.</p>
        <p>Whatever was A. Gleave doing to lose to W. J. Wood. (Cemetery) after having led 20-13 at the 24th</p>
        <p>end? Woods then had a nice run of eight amidst the applause of the Cemetery men.</p>
        <p>At Crosfields, the soap men won by five points, several tight games being played. The best wins were those of J. Woods, W. Barton (Crosfields), J. Hill (Cemetery), W. Ellison (Cemetery), and A. Webb (Cemetery).</p>
        <p>The Cemetery men are in fine form this season and fully deserved their 53 point viciory.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Association in the Pelican Hotel on Tuesday evening, the draw for the second round. was made known as follows :7 _ _</p>
        <p>May 31st and June 7th.</p>
        <p>Junior Conservatives v. Senior Conservatives. Bull's Head v. Fearnhead. Byes :—Cemetery and Crosqeld's No. 2. Other arrangements were : June 7th to 14th. CONSOLATION COMPETITION. Penketh v. Liberal Club. King and Queen v. Rylands. Byes :—Whitecross and Crosfield's No. 1.</p>
        <p>The Warrington and Liberal Association were accepted members of the Association.</p>
        <p>The annual handicap will be played on June 14, 21 and 28.</p>
        <p>A full day's programme was gone through in the Works Association Competition, the green adjoining. the Horse and Jockey being in excellent condition.</p>
        <p>The games were of an exceptionally high standard, an interest and enthusiasm being displayed which augurs well for the success of the competition.</p>
        <p>Crosfield's No. 2 were by far the better team when they encountered the Union Tanneries' representatives. Only one of the Boapery men failed to reach a double figure score, this being C. Dagnall, who only added three points to his handicap start of four against F. Lloyd (7), who won in 10 ends.</p>
        <p>Rylands Wet Shop found Robinson's Glass Works a tough handful, and had to admit defeat to the even t of six points. S. Fair, 3 (Robinson's) was a fair match for J. Hilton 4, who, however, finally won by the odd point in 18 ends. If it had not been for the start they received, several of the player.' would have had a very meagre score to record.</p>
        <p>Howley Tannery are out for honours again and thoroughly earned their nine point victory over the, Ropery No. 2 team.</p>
        <p>Orford Beam Shed were just a shade better than the Cheshire Lines trundlers, and a four point win in their favour truly represented the play. Surely G. Higham (5) Orford ought to have won over G. Higham (1), the Railway men's captain, for at the 14th end he led 13-10. The Railway man secured the jack and ran his woods well for twoldoubles and a single. W. Povey (4) Orford led 10-5 against T. Timperley at the Bth end, but from this juncture he had barely a look in and Timperley won a two point victory.</p>
        <p>The t.sl o remaining matches between the Joiners. and Fletcher's No. 1 and Monks Hall (Rivet) and and Richmond's Grappenhall, bristled with interesting incidents. In the latter match the rivet shop men had matters practically all their own way, some of their opponents only adding singles to their start.</p>
        <p>What a great day the Liberals had on Saturday in the Peter Peacock Challenge Cup Competition ! The matches were got through in record fashion, but the palm for the best win must go to Fairfield D who won 116-51 over Bewsey C. To those who know the Bewseyites, this looks inexplicable. As a matter of fact, the only man to win outright for Fairfield was G. Monks, who beat W. Bouchier 13-3 in 13 ends.</p>
        <p>The only two men who took the green for the Bevrseyites were W. Bicken and W. Bounell, who beat T. G. Gallop and J. Warburton 13-7 and 13-9 respec- tively in 17 and 16 ends. There is vely little satis- faction out of games of this character, and I feel sure it is the wish of all that there should be as few of these farcial contests as uo,sible.</p>
        <p>One of the best games was that between D. Plinston (Howley F) and J. E. H. Sutton (Orford B). The- latter led 10-3 at the 9th end, but he was in a sorry plight on the next three ends, failing to record a single chalk. Such was the excellence of Plinston's play that. he peeled with 12 at the 15th end, only to go under on the next length. He really deserved to win for the plucky game he played, but such is the fickleness to fate.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0229" n="art0229">
        <p>-</p>
        <p>Walker (Howley B) made a determined and success- ful stand against S. Hunt (St. Austin's C) whom he defeated 13-12 in 19 ends, after having been in arrears 4-10 and 7-11. The last ends were decidedly interesting and in a degree, exciting, it being" evens" on each man.</p>
        <p>Weather permitting, some good games should be witnessed to-day and it is expocted that there will be a large gate at Bank Quay.</p>
        <p>The "Harold Smith "Cup ward competition should commence next week on the green attached to the Brooklands Hotel, which Mr. Earle reports to be in fine condition. The competition will be governed by a committee consisting of one delegate from each polling district, along with the following council representatives: Messrs. T. S. Steel, W. H. Bellian, J. A. Rushworth and Councillor W. J. Wilson. The following are the officials for this season : Chairman, Mr. T. S. Steel: vice-chairman, Mr. W. H. Bellian ; hon. secretary, Mr. R. F. Anderton ; hon. treasurer, Mr. H. Hayes. The entries closed on May 20th. The teams are eight a-side, with two reserves, and there will be ten prizes for both winners and runners-up, so that the reserves will also come in for a prize.</p>
        <p>"THUMB BIAS."</p>
        <p>12</p>
