      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0238" n="art0238">
        <head>FOOTBALL CLUB.</head>
        <p>FOOTBALL CLUB.</p>
        <p>that the committee of either club had any right to enter the ground without paying during a Cup competition any more than other members of the club. (Hear, bear.) Continuing, Mr. Wright said they must all admit that the Warrington Club was in difficulties. If each competing club was entitled to half the gate receipts, why should the Warrington members have to pay the full entrance of is. ? There were a number of honorary members who paid their subscriptions every year, but saw few matches, and when they put in an appearance at a cup-tie they found they had to pay is. fid. before they could gain admission to the ground and stand. He thought this was very wrong and also that there should be some compromise whereby members of the cluhneed not pay the lull amount. If the club was in financial diflicuties the best thing was to bring the matter before the members, not to extract 6d. from them in such an unjust manner.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0239" n="art0239">
        <head>POSITION OF THE CLUB.</head>
        <p>THE POSITION OF THE CLUB.</p>
        <p>Continuing, he said he was sorry to note the position the club occupied to-day. He thought that to a great extent the members were responsible for that position. For evidence of that they need only look at the agenda of that meeting. There was a club with 1,200 members, and they found that there were only nine nomination for the eight places on the committee. The members of the club were evidently not taking that interest which they ought to show. He felt satisfied that the committee was not one of the best they had had. The club was in low water and he was sorry to see that two valuable men (Mr. Ashton and Mr. Davies) were retir- ing after having served the club faithfully and well for a considerable time. He deeply regretted that they had taken that course.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0240" n="art0240">
        <head>LEAGUE CHAMPIONSHIP.</head>
        <p>THE LEAGUE CHAMPIONSHIP.</p>
        <p>Mr. E. ENGLAND complimented the secretary on .the faithful and honest report which he had piesented. They were extremely sorry to lose the services of Mr. Ashton. He (the speaker) could renienik,er playing shoulder to shouldet with Mr. Ashton over 33 years ago, and he had been a loyal and faithful memter to the club since that time. (Hear, hear.) He (the speaker) hoped they would make Mr. Ashton a life member of the club Mr. Davies had also been a hard worker for the club as a member of the committee. Although he (the speaker) was pretty well satisfied with the season's doings, he was exceedingly sorry to note the low position of the club in the League. He %wild not be satisfied until he felt sure that they had the best team in the League, and he could see no reason why, in the course of time, they should not ' be at the head of the table. That was a more worthy position to attain than that of winning the cup. He felt exceedingly proud of the players for the noble way in which they responded to the efforts of the committee, and he noted with satisfaction that the committee had managed to get a good grip on the team at last. The criticism during the last three years had borne good fruit. (Hear, hear.) Every member of the team played his part nobly in the cup-ties. They surprised not only the Warring- ton officials, but the whole of the football world. It must be remembered that in the final they were up against the finest team which had ever played since Rugby football was instituted. WHY MEMBERS HAVE TO PAY.</p>
        <p>Mr. ASHTON regretted the remarks made by Mr. Wright, and said he was rather surprised at the reflec- tions he had made with regard to toe Warrington club and the Northern Unioh on the question of members having to pay at cup-ties. When the resolution in ques- tion was passed, another resolutit.n was put on the books with regard to the action of the Warrington Committee in bringing forward a motion to the effect that members should not pay. If the club had nO4 conformed to the Northern Union resolution they would have been ditaualified from taking part in the cup ties and perhaps would have had a heavy fine of £5O or £lOO imposed upon them. "What," asked Mr. Ashton of the members, "would you have done ? " (A voice : You have done right.) Continuing, Mr. Ashton said the club were very sorry but they issued a notification. before members took their catda out, that charges would have to be made. Mr. WRIGHT : But did all the members pay? Mr. ASHTON : I will refer to that. All the members of the club paid for admission to the cup tie. ' Mr. WRIGHT: Did they? Mr. ASHTON said he might tell them that the Northern Union allowed thirty tiukets for each committee of a competing team. That was the only means by which any member could obtain free permission to the cup- ties. All the others had to pay. In conclusion, the retiring secretary said it had been the ambition of the club to win the League, but there appeared to be more interest taken in the cup-ties. They had now been in the final for the cup five times and there was every possibility of the cup coming again to War- rington in the course of time. (Applause.) Mr. BRADSHA.W read a letter from Mr. Platt, the hon. secretary of the Northern Union, sent out after the first round of the cup ties last season, in which he stated that thirty complimentary tickets would be issued to the committee of the visiting team and twenty to the committee of the home team. Ladies and other members were to have no preferential prices fixed for their benefit. The CHAIRMAN: We have always worked against this and alicays shall do. The Secretary's report was then adopted. On the proposition of Mr. WRIGHT, seconded by Mr. R. B. EVANS, the Treasurer's report was adopted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-24_WidnesExaminer_art0241" n="art0241">
        <head>MR. LOCKER RE-ELECTED PRESMENT.</head>
        <p>MR. LOCKER RE-ELECTED PRESMENT.</p>
        <p>Mr. OSBORNE proposed the re-election of Mr. T. Looker as president. It was not necessary for him to eulogise Mr. Locker, as they all knew him and tha work be had done for the club in the past. The members fully recognised his services, for nothing was too much for him to do. He accomplished everything</p>
        <p>als9 in an excellent manner, and, in re-electing him) - would be doing honour to the club. (Applause.) Mr. W. HEWITT seconded and the proposition carried with unanimity. upig Mr. LOCKER, in his reply said that, whilst thsfrd them very sincerely for re-electing him to the horialloy position of president for the third time, he mast ato that he had hoped to have handed over the ofli,e-bio someone else. Still he was fully prepared to °°,od very best for the club for another year, but at thl,,ept. of the coming season they must have a new preol';,bo With regard to the doings of the club last season,:job could add another word to the masterly report. sl'icio Mr. Ashton had given them? In his opinion t the quite useless for anyone to say a single word ;halloo club's performances that night. He could not without expressing his own deep regret and the ser oss experienced by all at the resignation 0' rieD lsecretary. Those who had worked with him in tbe:e and who therefore knew most of his its thoroughness, its unselfishness and its etbilr,oo were keenest in their regret He sincerely trustea 1000 in the very near future the club would have, ir3 SArgl capacity or other, the benefit of his experience, Lai - and devotion. (Applause.) THE NEW SECRETARY. tit In proposing the election of Mr. T. P. Bradslebor secretary, Mr. ASHTON said that although Mr. BraotibisO had not long been a member of the committee, 0,4,0 been most attentive to tbe work and had given aib., to amount of time, patience and assistance, not or" the committee, but to the team generally. Clobsl Mr. ENGLAND seconded. Any duty Mr. —ra ,efe undertook he carried out faithfully, and thef fortunate in getting such a man as secretary. The resolution was heartily carried.</p>
        <p>THE TREASURER RE-ELECTED. gr.- Mr. CRITCHLEY proposed the re-election Of were Pemberton as treasurer. His services to the clu.„ so well known that the members were bound tv unanimous in their support of the proposition. per Mr. WEBSTER seconded. He said no one coulA' heerj., the accounts better than Mr. Pemberton, who ha'ileocv congratulated on numerous occasions on the exce of his work. toie° The meeting unanimously confirmed the appoin-</p>
