        <p>MR. BRADSHAW'S REPLY. Mr. BRADSHAW, in responding, said he full,' ,te,! tho the responsibilities attaching to the office and sui".s fig he had to follow in the footsteps of a man, as Northern Union football was concerned, goo oilog• class of his own. It was extremely difficult fur a 10019 man likeihimself, who had been on the committee,. It 12 months, to follow where Mr. Ashton bad trc!uog to was impossible to please all, and he was not 000'0 try. Given a fair chance, however, he hoped t° their full confidence. He would :WlwAye end es°,°oooe.) carry out his duties honestly and fearlessly. (API"</p>
