        <p>WAIFS AND STRAYS. Warrington's Effort for their Reclamation. The cry of the reformer to-day is " We must protect, educate and uplift the and all interested in social reformation agree that that is a wise course to adopt. Therefore, an organisation like the Church of Englund Waifs' and Strays' Society, whose one object is to give the poor and down-trodden a real chance in life, should not be unsuccessful in its appeal to the public for help. Under the auspices of the local branch of the Silver Thimble League of the above institution, of which Mrs. Morley Stevenson is the energetic seore- tary, a sale of work was held on Wednesday afternoon in the Patten hail. The Rev. iCanon Stevenson, who presided, said the society was doing a good work for the little waifs and strays of the land. They were only trying to carry out the Master's injunction : " Feed my lambs." He appealed to them more particularly in Warrington, because of the home in Salisbury-street, which was quite full and which was doing such a good work. He was sure they were all glad to see Mrs. J. D. Buckton, of Nilbley House, Dursiey, back in Warring- ton, to open the sale. (Applause). Mrs. Bu( Krrox said that the Liverpool diocese not only provided for its own homes, but it sent an affiilia- tion lee to the headquarters in London in addition.</p>
        <p>NAVAL BOATING DISASTER.' : Seven Sailors Drowned in the rirt' of Forth. •• _ . • .. A boat belonging to H.M.S. Itchen was swamped rigi,i4 frA4 Granton, on Sunday afternoon' resulting in the 105"dr' seven men of the crew. About half-past four a six-0AI), rowing boat put off from the Itchen, calling on b° „b , the Kale and Uie, and made for Granton Harbour, about a score of men abroad. A heavy sea was and when within 50 yards of the pier a strong citar,,, was encountered,which carried the boat in the direct.o" of Leith. Three times the crew tried to make the hatboa: without success' and the boat was then put abut the intention of returning to the ship. SuddenlYt the horror of the onlookers, the bow of the boat:o7, lifted by a big wave. There was an inrush of,*att:j..• and the boat turned turtle, the occupants h eing OtcP into the sea. Some of the men clung to the upturned boat,; bu others struck out for the harbour, and, getting 1P,5, the swift current,were soon engulfed. One man reach the destroyer in s%fety, and the others, .who cluog. the boat, were rescued by the boats which put off fr° the flotilla. It is stated that an artificer of the Reber], on findily. himself in the water, struck out for the Eke. Lie thrown a lifebuoy, with which be swam back to vment his shipmates were Struggling in the water. To one these be threw the lifenuoy, and both were picked yr . by a rescuing boat. . WANTED IN EVERY HOME! , _ The Handiness of 'Zam-Buk.</p>
