        <p>Should Members Pay to see Cup-ties? HARMONIOUS ANNUAL MEETING. The annual meeting of the Warrington Fcotball Club, which was held in the Co-operative Hall on Thursday evening, was quite harmonious and unevent- ful. General regret was expressed at the retirement of Mr. H. Ashtpn from the secretaryship a position he has filled with credit to himself and the club for thirteen years. Tribute was paid to the excellent work he had done and every good wish was extended to his successor, Mr. T. P. Bradshaw. The principal , discussion was on the Northern Union resolution "that all members shall pay for entrance to cup matches." Mr. T. Locker presided, and he was supported on the platform by Messrs. H. Ashton, T. Pemberton, T. P. Bradshaw, J. F. Forter, E. Osborne, T. Barnes, T. Bunch and J. Whitham. Mr. R. F. ANDERTON proposed, and Mr. WEBSTER seconded, the adoption of the Secretary's report, which Vfe published last week. ADMIStION TO CUP-TIES. SHOULD CLUB MEMBERS PAY? Mr. S. WRIGHT, J.P., congratulated Mr. Locker on being nominated as president for another year. tie bad rendered valuable service to the ctub—(hear, hear)— having supportsd it in times of prosperity and depres- sion. He had never deserted it, but had done all he could to further the sport in Warrington. (Applause.) Continuing, Mr. Wright said in regard to the Secre- tary's report, one question he would like to ask was how it came about that the Northern Union had power to compel members of the Warrington Club to pay for entrance to the ground for cup-ties, because the rules of the club distinctly sated " that , all members had free access to the ground ' at all times from the Ist September to the 30th April"? The members ought to take some action in the matter. He did not think the Northern Union had the power to make them pay, and if they insisted on entering the ground, neither the Football Committee nor members of the Northern Union could refuse them that liberty. They should not allow their liberties or their rights to be taken from them. (Hear, hear.) When the 131111 were issued for the cup matches they said "All ply," and he would like to know whether all did pay. He was given to understand that a number of free tickets were issued, not only to members of their own committee,but to members of the competing clubs. lie did not see</p>
